material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83110d4f-ad80-52aa-8fda-11d6b01ac385', 'word_count': 498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83110d4f-ad80-52aa-8fda-11d6b01ac385'}, {'abstract': '“The End of Love” follows a struggling Los Angeles actor and dissolute single dad as he faces the responsibilities of raising his toddler.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/01/movies/the-end-of-love-directed-by-and-starring-mark-webber.html', 'snippet': '“The End of Love” follows a struggling Los Angeles actor and dissolute single dad as he faces the responsibilities of raising his toddler.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Children, in their candor, spontaneity and emotional transparency, can be riveting camera subjects, which the writer-director Mark Webber milks to the limit in his slender, semiautobiographical film, “The End of Love.” Of course, as many people will admit but parents can forget, children are seldom as interesting to others as they are to their families. This story of a struggling Los Angeles actor and dissolute single dad (Mr. Webber) trying to face responsibilities and raise a 2-year-old (Isaac Love, Mr. Webber’s real-life toddler) is a reminder.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/01/arts/01RDP_END_SPAN/01RDP_END_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/01/arts/01RDP_END_SPAN/01RDP_END_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/01/arts/01RDP_END_SPAN/01RDP_END_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/01/arts/01RDP_END_SPAN/