ation, or less worried about unemployment, than their peers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Fed Presidents, Economics Is Local', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T01:00:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Binyamin Appelbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Binyamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appelbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39e1fd12-8feb-5919-9807-1a690a3bd4cf', 'word_count': 332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39e1fd12-8feb-5919-9807-1a690a3bd4cf'}, {'abstract': 'Dan Sallitt’s latest movie, “The Unspeakable Act,” which follows two siblings, is about the familiar more than it is about the forbidden.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/01/movies/the-unspeakable-act-directed-by-dan-sallitt.html', 'snippet': 'Dan Sallitt’s latest movie, “The Unspeakable Act,” which follows two siblings, is about the familiar more than it is about the forbidden.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The problem of the unspeakable is rife in the movies of the filmmaker and critic Dan Sallitt. Variants on “Let’s talk about it later” are practically a refrain in “Honeymoon,” his painfully acute 1998 film about two friends turned lovers. More often than not the emotional bind is not shocking or taboo at its root, and indeed Mr. Sallitt’s latest, the Brooklyn-set feature “The Unspeakable Act,” is about the familiar more than it is about the forbidden.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [