d newfound love of mascara.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/01/face-time-keri-russell/', 'snippet': 'Russell, star of the new FX drama “The Americans,” chatted with T about her new job, closet workouts and newfound love of mascara.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the new FX drama “The Americans,” Keri Russell plays the role of Elizabeth Jennings, a knockout K.G.B. spy living in America in the early 1980s. With the recent announcement of the show’s second season and a new movie in theaters now (“Dark Skies”), Russell’s one busy lady, but not too busy to chat with T about her new job, closet workouts and newfound love of mascara.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Face Time | Keri Russell', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cosmetics and Toiletries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Russell, Keri', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T14:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Turnbow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Turnbow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51d69804-e432-5c7e-8c07-db7bd0b66d4a', 'word_count': 843, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51d69804-e432-5c7e-8c07-db7bd0b66d4a'}, {'abstract': 'Thomas James Brennan, who served in Iraq and Afghanistan, recalls building a school in Helmand Province, Afghanistan.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/01/remembering-a-silent-success-in-afghanistan/', 'snippet': 'Thomas James Brennan, who served in Iraq and Afghanistan, recalls building a school in Helmand Province, Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'December in the mountains of southern Afghanistan greeted me and my men with strong and seemingly endless gusts of wind. The frigid temperatures were equally unforgiving. Our living quarters were constructed out of cardboard boxes and plastic sheeting, which didn’t create much of an escape. The highlight of my day, despite the obvious threat, was leading patrols as a squad leader. The physical activity kept me comfortably warm and allowed me to distance my mind from our frosty reality. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Remembering a Silent Success in Afghanistan', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Afghan National Police', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Taliban', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Marine Corps', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Helmand Province (Afghanistan)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T14:01:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas James Brennan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': 'James', 'lastname': 'Brennan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc186630-695f-5a3f-a021-66cd8d0c22e6', 'word_count': 923, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc186630-695f-5a3f-a021-66cd8d0c22e6'}, {'abstract': 'A fresh exploration of ways to forge progress on a turbulent planet amid varied views on risks.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/01/more-on-divergent-paths-to-human-progress/', 'snippet': 'A fresh exploration of ways to forge progress on a turbulent planet amid varied views on risks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The University of Illinois has posted a well-produced video of my talk there last October, titled “9 Billion People + 1 Earth = ?” It’s worth posting here given that this has been the central question shaping this blog since its inception. I explore many ideas, but focus particularly on the need to get comfortable with the wide range of human reactions to risks of various kinds. (Around minute 13, I credit the “cultural cognition” work of Dan Kahan at Yale University.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More on Divergent Paths to Human Progress', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disasters and Emergencies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Population', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McKibben, Bill', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T14:15:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec6d22ee-dd7d-5d23-b6fe-746fe63bd23b', 'word_count': 409, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec6d22ee-dd7d-5d23-b6fe-746fe63bd23b'}, {'abstract': 'Global capital flows declined in both 2011 and 2012, when they were 61 percent lower than in the peak year of 2007, according to estimates by the McKinsey Global Institute.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2013/03/01/business/More-Money-Stays-at-Home.html', 'snippet': 'Global capital flows declined in both 2011 and 2012, when they were 61 percent lower than in the peak year of 2007, according to estimates by the McKinsey Global Institute.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Global capital flows declined in both 2011 and 2012, when they were 61 percent lower than in the peak year of 2007, according to estimates by the McKinsey Global Institute.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Off the Charts: More Money Stays at Home', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T14:41:11+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/0cc38c00-cb36-5b5a-9830-97a750f756b8', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/0cc38c00-cb36-5b5a-9830-97a750f756b8'}, {'abstract': 'The world’s money is staying closer to home; global capital flow dropped 13 percent in 2012 from 2011, and is 61 percent below the peak levels of 2007.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/02/business/economy/globalization-as-measured-by-investment-takes-a-step-backward.html', 'snippet': 'The world’s money is staying closer to home; global capital flow dropped 13 percent in 2012 from 2011, and is 61 percent below the peak levels of 2007.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A FEW years ago, capital was flying around the globe faster than it ever had. The world economy became more tightly integrated, and technology contributed to making it easier for banks and investors to deploy their money on the other side of the world if prospects looked more attractive there.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Globalization, Measured by Investment, Takes a Step Backward ', 'kicker': 'Off the Charts', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Globalization, Measured by Investment, Takes a Step Backward', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Investments', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T14:44:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Norris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Norris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ae7bdfb-4343-5a20-bb09-6a48cdb39a70', 'word_count': 545, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ae7bdfb-4343-5a20-bb09-6a48cdb39a70'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/01/what-were-reading-640/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Epicurious: As an alcoholic partner for chocolate, red wine is the conventional choice. But give a thought to Irish stout. – Patrick Farrell', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T14:51:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e8fe6ff-6aa5-548b-b328-b357d62121c3', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e8fe6ff-6aa5-548b-b328-b357d62121c3'}, {'abstract': 'Is our Europeans learning?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/01/disastrous-predictions-and-predictable-disasters/', 'snippet': 'Is our Europeans learning?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Joe Weisenthal is wrong. He writes that the unfolding economic disaster in Europe is what everyone predicted. Not so. It’s what he predicted, it’s what I predicted, but it’s not at all what many people were predicting. And the people who got it completely wrong happen to be the people still running European economic policy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Disastrous Predictions and Predictable Disasters', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T14:53:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/333af14a-e659-53b9-89a7-552613405c6e', 'word_count': 306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/333af14a-e659-53b9-89a7-552613405c6e'}, {'abstract': 'Fairway, seen as the key to the Red Hook neighborhood’s rebirth, opened Friday for the first time since Hurricane Sandy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/02/nyregion/fairway-market-in-red-hook-reopens-after-hurricane.html', 'snippet': 'Fairway, seen as the key to the Red Hook neighborhood’s rebirth, opened Friday for the first time since Hurricane Sandy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There came a moment on Friday during the grand reopening of the Fairway in Red Hook, Brooklyn — after the schoolchildren had sung the national anthem, but before Miss America cut the ribbon — when St. John Frizell, a restaurateur, stood at the lectern and described what might be called the indigenous importance of the event. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cheers in Brooklyn as Storm-Struck Grocery Store Returns', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cheers in Brooklyn as a Storm-Struck Grocery Store Returns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fairway Supermarkets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Feuer, Alan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Red Hook (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hurricane Sandy (2012)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T14:56:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Feuer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feuer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e867b266-47c2-5aae-9689-8711907fbf32', 'word_count': 791, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e867b266-47c2-5aae-9689-8711907fbf32'}, {'abstract': 'Her first labor was uneventful. So why, a writer asks, “has it come to pass that I feel even more terrified by the idea of laboring with this second baby than I did with the first?”', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/01/unreasonably-terrified-of-a-second-labor/', 'snippet': 'Her first labor was uneventful. So why, a writer asks, “has it come to pass that I feel even more terrified by the idea of laboring with this second baby than I did with the first?”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Stephanie Lucianovic, who has written here about picky eaters and being “pregnant but thin,” sent me a quandary: she’s pregnant with her second child and “terrified.” Her first labor, she writes, was relatively uneventful. No trauma, although the doctor provided “a little extra help from the vacuum.” So, she asks:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Unreasonably ‘Terrified’ of a Second Labor', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Babies and Infants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pregnancy and Childbirth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T14:58:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/086946ab-9c87-5cea-bfd0-4db666bc8250', 'word_count': 545, 'uri': 'nyt://article/086946ab-9c87-5cea-bfd0-4db666bc8250'}, {'abstract': 'Jon Pareles and Ben Ratliff discuss “Amok,” the new album by Atoms for Peace, the quintet which is at heart a collaboration between Radiohead’s Thom Yorke and his longtime producer Nigel Godrich. Atoms for Peace rests somewhere uneasily between progressive rock and electronic dance-music; it’s as dependent on aggressive and transformative post-production as it is on the creation of tracks by people playing instruments — but where does that process come from, and what does it signify?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/audio/2013/03/01/arts/music/01popcast_pod.html', 'snippet': '