'headline': {'main': 'Christian Dior: Fall 2013 RTW', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T16:53:44+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': "Women's Runway", 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/98bd7c82-1432-5be2-b1bc-51c72af7359d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/98bd7c82-1432-5be2-b1bc-51c72af7359d'}, {'abstract': 'What makes for a successful literary byline?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/books/review/by-any-other-name.html', 'snippet': 'What makes for a successful literary byline?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though I endured some grade-school ribbing for my slightly unusual first name, professionally, it’s been a boon. Readers are much more likely to remember a byline with Teddy, my somewhat gravitas-deficient nickname since birth, than one with my more common legal name, Derek. According to the Social Security database, in 1979, the year I was born, Teddy was the 485th-most-popular male name; Derek was ranked 72. These days, I’m not even in the top thousand. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Wayne/0303-Wayne-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 697, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Wayne/0303-Wayne-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 697}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Wayne/0303-Wayne-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 882, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Wayne/0303-Wayne-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1079, 'width': 929, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Wayne/0303-Wayne-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Wayne/0303-Wayne-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Wayne/0303-Wayne-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'By Any Other Name', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'By Any Other Name', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T16:54:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Teddy Wayne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Teddy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wayne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3886fc91-362b-50d8-b794-e49904cc302c', 'word_count': 1343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3886fc91-362b-50d8-b794-e49904cc302c'}, {'abstract': 'Satire mingles with warmth and pathos in Sam Lipsyte’s second collection of stories.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/books/review/the-fun-parts-by-sam-lipsyte.html', 'snippet': 'Satire mingles with warmth and pathos in Sam Lipsyte’s second collection of stories.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s a point at which garden-\xadvariety fecklessness bleeds into tragedy, but where? How? To what effect? For the answers to these and other pressing questions, you could do worse than look to the sublime mayhem of Sam Lipsyte’s new story collection, “The Fun Parts.” Lipsyte expertly works the line between hilarity and pathos, presenting characters who stay airborne only so long as they’re pedaling their rattletrap fantasy machines. The fun is watching how earnestly and furiously they pedal, their legs cranking faster and faster even as their fantasies roll into their final dives.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Fountain/0303-Fountain-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 932, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Fountain/0303-Fountain-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 932}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Fountain/0303-Fountain-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 660, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Fountain/0303-Fountain-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1362, 'width': 877, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Fountain/0303-Fountain-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Fountain/0303-Fountain-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Fountain/0303-Fountain-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Die Laughing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Die Laughing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lipsyte, Sam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T16:55:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Fountain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fountain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd0b80ab-67ca-5de6-a220-c2c03335e4a5', 'word_count': 1288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd0b80ab-67ca-5de6-a220-c2c03335e4a5'}, {'abstract': 'Andrew Solomon, who reviews Emily Bazelon’s “Sticks and Stones” this week, remembers some harrowing trips to school when he was a child.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Andrew Solomon, who reviews Emily Bazelon’s “Sticks and Stones” this week, remembers some harrowing trips to school when he was a child.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Andrew Solomon, who reviews Emily Bazelon’s “Sticks and Stones” in this week’s issue, remembers some harrowing trips to school when he was a child. “I didn’t tell any adults about being bullied on the school bus, where it was at its violent worst,” he told us by e-mail. “I was afraid that they would somehow see why I was being bullied, that it had something to do with the shameful, damaged, wrong side of me — the gay side.” When his mother eventually learned about the bullying from someone else, she arranged to have a chaperon on the bus. This chaperon “was ineffectual,” Solomon recalled. “While I consistently found adults sympathetic, I didn’t see how they could fix the problem — and neither did they. It felt to me like the weather.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Upfront/0303-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 975, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Upfront/0303-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 975}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Upfront/0303-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 631, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Upfront/0303-Upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1953, 'width': 1202, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Upfront/0303-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Upfront/0303-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Upfront/0303-Upfront-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Solomon, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T16:56:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/351810da-1cd0-5a67-8547-a76dbdb92000', 'word_count': 291, 'uri': 'nyt://article/351810da-1cd0-5a67-8547-a76dbdb92000'}, {'abstract': 'With “Damn Few,” new on the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 10, Rorke Denver becomes at least the seventh veteran of the Navy SEALs to have a best seller since Osama bin Laden was killed in 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'With “Damn Few,” new on the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 10, Rorke Denver becomes at least the seventh veteran of the Navy SEALs to have a best seller since Osama bin Laden was killed in 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ACTION HEROES: Another week, another best seller about the Navy SEALs — this time it’s Rorke Denver’s “Damn Few: Making the Modern SEAL Warrior” (written with Ellis Henican), which enters the hardcover nonfiction list at No. 10. Denver is at least the seventh veteran of the SEALs to place a book on the list since Osama bin Laden was killed in 2011; he follows Eric Greitens, Howard Wasdin, Chuck Pfarrer, Chris Kyle (who’s back at No. 1 after his death at a Texas shooting range earlier this year), Marcus Luttrell and Mark Owen (currently at No. 9). And that’s not counting Suzanne Brockmann’s line of Navy SEAL romance novels, which sold well even while bin Laden was still alive.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03list-SUB/03list-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 511, 'width': 495, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03list-SUB/03list-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 511, 'width': 495, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03list-SUB/03list-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03list-SUB/03list-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03list-SUB/03list-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T16:57:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/628851fc-8f39-577d-9311-17b7237c5a8f', 'word_count': 631, 'uri': 'nyt://article/628851fc-8f39-577d-9311-17b7237c5a8f'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE LOVE SONG OF JONNY VALENTINE,  by Teddy Wayne. (Free Press, $24.99.) The full weight of our obnoxious, vacuous, fame-sodden culture is heaped squarely on the shoulders of the Bieberesque hero of Wayne’s very funny novel.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "Editors' Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T16:58:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd16c3ce-eb61-5837-8f39-face15f1d0c9', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd16c3ce-eb61-5837-8f39-face15f1d0c9'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRAVE DRAGONS: A Chinese Basketball Team, an American Coach, and Two Cultures Clashing,  by Jim Yardley. (Vintage, $15.95.) In 2008, Bob Weiss, a veteran N.B.A. coach, was brought to Shanxi Province to reverse the fortunes of the Brave Dragons of the Chinese Basketball Association. Yardley, a New York Times reporter who was then the paper’s Beijing bureau chief, followed the team on an engaging road trip through modern China, a country caught between an enduring Communist system and a growing entrepreneurial spirit.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T16:58:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/34368b6b-251f-568e-82ad-897d8d98268b', 'word_count': 492, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34368b6b-251f-568e-82ad-897d8d98268b'}, {'abstract': 'Emily Bazelon discusses her new book, “Sticks and Stones,” about the culture of bullying.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/01/book-review-podcast-the-bully-problem/', 'snippet': 'Emily Bazelon discusses her new book, “Sticks and Stones,” about the culture of bullying.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week in The New York Times Book Review, Andrew Solomon reviews “Sticks and Stones,” Emily Bazelon’s new book about bullied children and how to contain or prevent such harm. Mr. Solomon writes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03cover-shadow/03cover-shadow-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 214, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03cover-shadow/03cover-shadow-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 214, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03cover-shadow/03cover-shadow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03cover-shadow/03cover-shadow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03cover-shadow/03cover-shadow-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Book Review Podcast: The Bully Problem', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bullies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bazelon, Emily', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cowles, Gregory', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lopate, Phillip', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tanenhaus, Sam', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T17:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b67ed66-883e-5925-98db-5a01ab814f68', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b67ed66-883e-5925-98db-5a01ab814f68'}, {'abstract': 'A novel of Israelis and Palestinians looks to the days when there was an intimacy to the violence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/books/review/the-wanting-by-michael-lavigne.html', 'snippet': 'A novel of Israelis and Palestinians looks to the days when there was an intimacy to the violence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Well into the 1990s, tens of thousands of Palestinians worked in Israel and thousands of Israelis headed to West Bank towns to shop and get their cars fixed. It was the era before proliferating barriers and checkpoints and Arab satellite stations. Palestinians watched Israeli television and understood Hebrew. If a Palestinian worker’s son was getting married, he might well invite an Israeli colleague or boss — and that Israeli might well attend. The relationships were in some sense colonial, hardly between equals. But they were real. Each side was forced to face the fact that on the other were human beings with legitimate concerns, not merely enemies to be feared and despised. There was a surprising, even poignant, intimacy to the conflict, something unimaginable today.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03BRONNER/03BRONNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 588, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03BRONNER/03BRONNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 588}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03BRONNER/03BRONNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1003, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03BRONNER/03BRONNER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1373, 'width': 1402, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03BRONNER/03BRONNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03BRONNER/03BRONNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03BRONNER/03BRONNER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Invasions of Space', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Invasions of Space', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bronner, Ethan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lavigne, Michael', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T17:01:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ethan Bronner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ethan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bronner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe385796-fb0f-53de-bbce-1a82484fa955', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe385796-fb0f-53de-bbce-1a82484fa955'}, {'abstract': 'Two analysts believe that United States policy in Iran should emulate the opening to China.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/books/review/going-to-tehran-by-flynt-leverett-and-hillary-mann-leverett.html', 'snippet': 'Two analysts believe that United States policy in Iran should emulate the opening to China.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is much to be said for understanding how the Islamic Republic of Iran sees itself on the international stage. We make little sense of history, and less progress toward resolving our conflicts, when we demonize our adversary and ascribe to him dark motives and irrational thoughts. Flynt and Hillary Mann Leverett, veterans of the State Department and the National Security Council, might have written a book that made a powerful case for empathy in foreign policy. They would have devoted much of it to illuminating Iran’s strategic interests and its view of history. They might have done so while observing, as they have elsewhere, that American policy must not be blinded by sentimentality about Iranian human rights and democratic aspirations — concerns, they could suggest, that have no place in the cold calculus of global strategy. Whatever we think of the Islamic Republic, the Leveretts might argue, it is the policy maker’s job to understand it as it is, not as he would like it to be. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03SECOR/03SECOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 536, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03SECOR/03SECOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 536}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03SECOR/03SECOR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 915, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03SECOR/03SECOR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1830, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03SECOR/03SECOR-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03SECOR/03SECOR-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03SECOR/03SECOR-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Iran Syndrome', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Iran Syndrome', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Secor, Laura', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leverett, Flynt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leverett, Hillary Mann', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T17:03:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Secor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Secor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/99c4f80c-e6e8-5de3-b854-33aec4dbe08d', 'word_count': 1184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99c4f80c-e6e8-5de3-b854-33aec4dbe08d'}, {'abstract': 'Katherine Bouton explores the experience, and the science, of hearing loss.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/books/review/shouting-wont-help-by-katherine-bouton.html', 'snippet': 'Katherine Bouton explores the experience, and the science, of hearing loss.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine waking up one morning to find the world unaccountably dim. You turn on the lights and check for the usual suspects like a burned-out light bulb or a particularly cloudy day. Then you notice that the dimness is only in one eye and is interspersed with sudden flashes of bright light. You would probably head to a doctor pretty quickly.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Horrowitz/0303-Horrowitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 723, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Horrowitz/0303-Horrowitz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 723}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Horrowitz/0303-Horrowitz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 851, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Horrowitz/0303-Horrowitz-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1701, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Horrowitz/0303-Horrowitz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Horrowitz/0303-Horrowitz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/0303-Horrowitz/0303-Horrowitz-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Sound of Silence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Sound of Silence', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bouton, Katherine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ears and Hearing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deafness', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T17:03:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Seth S. Horowitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Seth', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Horowitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/45f19692-3256-5fd2-bcdd-8e22ab24dfe6', 'word_count': 976, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45f19692-3256-5fd2-bcdd-8e22ab24dfe6'}, {'abstract': 'The protagonist of Amity Gaige’s novel, worried about an impending divorce, takes off on a road trip with his daughter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/books/review/schroder-by-amity-gaige.html', 'snippet': 'The protagonist of Amity Gaige’s novel, worried about an impending divorce, takes off on a road trip with his daughter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Fiction is all about experimental selves, so it’s not hard to see what drew Amity Gaige to the title character of her third novel, “Schroder.” In his grim urban youth, Erik Schroder picks up a summer camp brochure in his pediatrician’s office and is so smitten by the photographs of fearless, handsome boys leading an outdoorsy life that he concocts a fallen-aristocrat persona in order to apply for a scholarship. Rebranding himself as Eric Kennedy, he invents a personal history (in which he was born and raised in a small town “not far from Hyannis Port”) that does much more than get him into Camp Ossipee. It opens so many other social doors that he never really drops the pretense: he goes on to college, gets a job, marries and fathers a child, all as Eric Kennedy.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DEE/03DEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 753, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DEE/03DEE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 753}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DEE/03DEE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 816, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DEE/03DEE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2039, 'width': 1624, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DEE/03DEE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DEE/03DEE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DEE/03DEE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Custody Flight', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Custody Flight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gaige, Amity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T17:03:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Dee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4d3b470-36e3-57fb-9f42-631f9d0afb2b', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4d3b470-36e3-57fb-9f42-631f9d0afb2b'}, {'abstract': 'Wide-ranging essays and a writer’s guide from Phillip Lopate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/books/review/essays-and-a-writers-guide-by-phillip-lopate.html', 'snippet': 'Wide-ranging essays and a writer’s guide from Phillip Lopate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The personal essay has always been a stepchild of serious literature, seemingly formless, hard to classify. Lacking the tight construction of a short story or the narrative arc of a novel or memoir, such essays have given readers pleasure without winning cultural respect. Written in a minor key, they could be slight and superficial, but their drawbacks could also be strengths. The style of the first-person essay tends to be conversational, tentative — in tune with our postmodern skepticism about absolutes, the trust we place in multiple perspectives. Few writers have pursued this more resourcefully than Phillip Lopate, who started out as a novelist and poet but gained traction when he began writing lively first-person essays in the late 1970s, later editing a landmark anthology, “The Art of the Personal Essay” (1994). ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DICKSTEIN/03DICKSTEIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 639, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DICKSTEIN/03DICKSTEIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 639}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DICKSTEIN/03DICKSTEIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 962, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DICKSTEIN/03DICKSTEIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1200, 'width': 1127, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DICKSTEIN/03DICKSTEIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DICKSTEIN/03DICKSTEIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03DICKSTEIN/03DICKSTEIN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Daily Round', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Daily Round', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lopate, Phillip', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T17:04:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Morris Dickstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Morris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dickstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9eadb313-cc3e-5a67-9c24-5f1375174fb3', 'word_count': 1335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9eadb313-cc3e-5a67-9c24-5f1375174fb3'}, {'abstract': 'A former Federal Reserve vice chairman examines the 2008 financial crisis, how it was handled and what remains to be done.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/books/review/after-the-music-stopped-by-alan-s-blinder.html', 'snippet': 'A former Federal Reserve vice chairman examines the 2008 financial crisis, how it was handled and what remains to be done.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On March 12, 1933, shortly after temporarily closing the banks in order to clean them up, Franklin Delano Roosevelt held the first of his presidential “fireside chats” with the American people. He delivered this radio address in the belief that the actions of his government, thus far described only in the jargon of banking and law, “ought to be explained for the benefit of the average citizen.” He had three more chats that year, and two the following year. They played a crucial role in bringing the public with him as his government helped lead the economy out of the Great Depression. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03BISHOP/03BISHOP-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 613, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03BISHOP/03BISHOP-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 613}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/books/review/03BISHOP/03BISHOP-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1003, 'subTy