 systems for the car-obsessed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A look at high-tech parking systems for the car-obsessed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/28/realestate/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 285, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/02/28/realestate/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 285}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/28/realestate/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 487, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/28/realestate/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 974, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/28/realestate/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/02/28/realestate/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/02/28/realestate/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR/30302013_Deal_Parking-slide-F6RR-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Automated Parking Garages', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parking Garages and Lots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T17:05:12+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Real Estate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/141b9952-178e-5b6e-a5b9-61f2839ef61d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/141b9952-178e-5b6e-a5b9-61f2839ef61d'}, {'abstract': 'Futuristic garages are equipped to whisk car and driver to upper-floor apartments, or park vehicles without human assistance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/realestate/automated-parking-garages-for-the-car-obsessed.html', 'snippet': 'Futuristic garages are equipped to whisk car and driver to upper-floor apartments, or park vehicles without human assistance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE OF THE BIGGEST curses the wealthy must endure in their otherwise pampered lives is the dreaded valet parking. You toss the keys of your Bentley to a parking attendant, who ends up changing your radio presets, sweating on your seats or, worse, leaving a scratch on your pristine paint job. Is this the good life? ', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/realestate/03DEALCOVER_SPAN/deal-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/realestate/03DEALCOVER_SPAN/deal-cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/realestate/03DEALCOVER_SPAN/deal-cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/realestate/03DEALCOVER_SPAN/deal-cover-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/realestate/03DEALCOVER_SPAN/03DEALCOVER_SPAN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/03/realestate/03DEALCOVER_SPAN/03DEALCOVER_SPAN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/realestate/03DEALCOVER_SPAN/03DEALCOVER_SPAN-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hate Valet? Not to Worry; Help Is at Hand', 'kicker': 'Big Deal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hate Valet? Not to Worry; Help Is at Hand', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barrionuevo, Alexei', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parking Garages and Lots', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parking', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Miami (Fla)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T17:05:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexei Barrionuevo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexei', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barrionuevo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40f8278c-7a03-5711-9141-5e659311427f', 'word_count': 1539, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40f8278c-7a03-5711-9141-5e659311427f'}, {'abstract': 'The discovery of a long-lost manuscript reveals the fate of an ancient German kingdom in Caleb Carr’s epic fantasy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/books/review/the-legend-of-broken-by-caleb-carr.html', 'snippet': 'The discovery of a long-lost manuscript reveals the fate of an ancient German kingdom in Caleb Carr’s epic fantasy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s unfortunate for Caleb Carr that the craft of writing so often disobeys a beloved American maxim: Hard work pays off. The colossal effort Carr has exerted to produce his latest book, “The Legend of Broken,” weighs distressingly on each of the novel’s 734 pages, including nearly 80 pages of endnotes. This is a fantasy epic — the tale of a fabled Germanic kingdom that rose and fell during the Dark Ages — but with its convoluted prose, its trudging plot and its onslaught of unsurprising period detail, it’s a perverted one. Sweat drips from the pages. Magic does not rise.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Order by the Sword', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Order by the Sword', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carr, Caleb', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T17:05:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Peed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Peed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ed49d29-2288-55ea-a04b-8370b683b426', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ed49d29-2288-55ea-a04b-8370b683b426'}, {'abstract': 'Siblings chafe against their assigned family roles in Lucinda Rosenfeld’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/books/review/the-pretty-one-by-lucinda-rosenfeld.html', 'snippet': 'Siblings chafe against their assigned family roles in Lucinda Rosenfeld’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lucinda Rosenfeld’s novels are often praised for getting real. The women she usually writes about have a snide streak that Rosenfeld’s own sardonic tone amplifies, and the situations they face are less affirming than the books’ chick lit packaging would suggest. “What She Saw . . . ” (2000) reminds us that suburban girls from loving families can grow up to be lost and self-hating, and that being desirable to men isn’t a very satisfying end in itself. “Why She Went Home” (2004) explains that it isn’t easy to make it in New York City. “I’m So Happy for You” (2009) — didactically labeled “a novel about best friends” — points out that female friendship can be undermining and hateful. Rosenfeld’s fourth and latest novel, “The Pretty One,” levies more cold truths.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Three Sisters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Three Sisters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ROSENFELD, LUCINDA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T17:05:36+0000', 'document_type': 'a