 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2a7f5b7-bc89-539d-8139-1ca826820230', 'word_count': 1017, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2a7f5b7-bc89-539d-8139-1ca826820230'}, {'abstract': 'The debut album of the brothers Raul and Mexia Hernández, sons of the bassist for Los Tigres del Norte, is definitely not Los Tigres 2.0.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/arts/music/raul-y-mexias-arriba-y-lejos-is-not-los-tigres-2-0.html', 'snippet': 'The debut album of the brothers Raul and Mexia Hernández, sons of the bassist for Los Tigres del Norte, is definitely not Los Tigres 2.0.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — In 2010 Raul and Mexia Hernández set up a camera and a keyboard in their living room in San Jose, Calif., to sing a song about immigrant rights. Mexia rapped about cooks and domestic workers and Latinos being “on the rise like blood pressure.” Raul crooned the Spanish-language hook (“I just want to succeed/And now they want to deport me”). Their brother, Giovanni, played a beat on a trash can. They called the song “Todos Somos Arizona” (“We Are All Arizona”) and uploaded the video to YouTube as a direct response to the state’s passage of a strict anti-immigrant law. The song quickly racked up thousands of views and earned the siblings a publicist. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/arts/03TIGRES2_SPAN/03TIGRES2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/arts/03TIGRES2_SPAN/03TIGRES2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/arts/03TIGRES2_SPAN/03TIGRES2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subty