', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Conlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Conlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b430645-5e31-500e-81e6-b343fdaaeaf9', 'word_count': 2821, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b430645-5e31-500e-81e6-b343fdaaeaf9'}, {'abstract': 'Richard Hell, the proto-punk rocker and writer, has kept a handmade book for 40-some years, since it was created and given to him by its author.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/fashion/safekeeping-a-punk-creation-possessed.html', 'snippet': 'Richard Hell, the proto-punk rocker and writer, has kept a handmade book for 40-some years, since it was created and given to him by its author.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every day, we upload and unload, migrating more and more of our hard stuff to the digital realm. Films, music, photos, magazines, even tools like levels, flashlights and thermometers are now mushed into megabytes. Yet there is one object we remain ambivalent about letting go of, and that is that much-loved portable media-holder known as the book. ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/fashion/03SUBPOSSESSED1/03SUBPOSSESSED1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/fashion/03SUBPOSSESSED1/03SUBPOSSESSED1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/fashion/03SUBPOSSESSED1/03SUBPOSSESSED1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}