he-philharmonic-in-carousel.html', 'snippet': 'The New York Philharmonic’s presentation of “Carousel” was given a spirited kick by Tiler Peck and Robert Fairchild in the second-act ballet.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tiler Peck, wearing a pale pink dress with her brown hair streaming around her shoulders, dashes across the stage at Avery Fisher Hall with her arms open wide and her heart exposed. As Louise, Billy Bigelow’s daughter in “Carousel,” Ms. Peck is a vulnerable spitfire — and just about as transcendent as the music. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CAROUSEL/02CAROUSEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 611, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CAROUSEL/02CAROUSEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 611}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CAROUSEL/02CAROUSEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1007, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CAROUSEL/02CAROUSEL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2013, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CAROUSEL/02CAROUSEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CAROUSEL/02CAROUSEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CAROUSEL/02CAROUSEL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Exuberance Is Bustin’ Out All Over, Under the Loving Gaze of a Father', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Exuberance Is Bustin’ Out All Over, Under the Loving Gaze of a Father', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Peck, Tiler', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hammerstein, Oscar II', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rodgers, Richard', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fairchild, Robert', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-01T20:55:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f85ff92b-9939-55b1-9722-acc86fbd3b4f', 'word_count': 455, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f85ff92b-9939-55b1-9722-acc86fbd3b4f'}, {'abstract': 'The Collegiate Chorale paired works by Philip Glass and Osvaldo Golijov at Carnegie Hall on Wednesday evening.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/02/arts/music/collegiate-chorale-with-american-symphony-at-carnegie-hall.html', 'snippet': 'The Collegiate Chorale paired works by Philip Glass and Osvaldo Golijov at Carnegie Hall on Wednesday evening.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Collegiate Chorale had logical reasons to pair the pieces it performed in its concert at Carnegie Hall on Wednesday evening, Philip Glass’s  Symphony No. 7 (“Toltec”) and Osvaldo Golijov’s  “Oceana.” Both are substantial statements by well-known, popular living composers. Both deal with Latin American culture, though in disparate ways. And, on the evidence offered here by the chorale with the conductor James Bagwell, the American Symphony Orchestra and other guests, both works recall stronger efforts by these important, distinctive composers.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CHORALE/02CHORALE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 411, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CHORALE/02CHORALE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 411}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CHORALE/02CHORALE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 701, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CHORALE/02CHORALE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1401, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CHORALE/02CHORALE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CHORALE/02CHORALE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/02/arts/02CHORALE/02CHORALE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'th