k', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-02T01:15:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By PETER LATTMAN and CHRISTINE HAUGHNEY', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'LATTMAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'HAUGHNEY', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f23c1fea-09fe-5796-ae22-f74c28ecc53d', 'word_count': 1149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f23c1fea-09fe-5796-ae22-f74c28ecc53d'}, {'abstract': 'Why many Boston blacks refused to join the famed 54th Massachusetts.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/01/equality-first-guns-afterward/', 'snippet': 'Why many Boston blacks refused to join the famed 54th Massachusetts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When recruiting began in February 1863 for the 54th Massachusetts – one of the first regiments in the Union Army composed of Northern free blacks–many Bostonians expected Robert Morris to be among its strongest boosters. A key figure in the city’s black leadership, Morris had spent much of the previous decade demanding a place for his black military company in the Massachusetts Volunteer Militia. He passionately believed that the spectacle of black men arrayed in the state’s uniforms, marching in disciplined ranks, would force a change in how most whites perceived them, replacing inchoate fears of slave rebellion with admiration for the newest members of the citizen-soldiery. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Equality First, Guns Afterward’', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'k