g-the-land-as-a-spiritual-quest.html', 'snippet': 'A growing number of pilgrims are lacing up boots and sneakers to walk across America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KEN ILGUNAS, who is 29 years old, arrived at the Gulf Coast town of Port Arthur, Tex., last month after hiking 1,700 miles from Alberta, Canada, crossing through the American heartland. On his arrival, he met a big-hearted Texan who knew of him from reading his blog and generously offered him dinner at his home and a place to stay for the night. “To walk across this country is to fall in love with mankind,” Mr. Ilgunas said. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03WALKER_SPAN/03WALKER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03WALKER_SPAN/03WALKER_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03WALKER_SPAN/03WALKER_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03WALKER_SPAN/03WALKER_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1362, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03WALKER_SPAN/03WALKER_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03WALKER_SPAN/03WALKER_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03WALKER_SPAN/03WALKER_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Walking the Country as a Spiritual Quest', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Walking The Country As a Spiritual Quest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Walking', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murphy, Kate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pilgrimages', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-02T17:56:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/30e70839-e057-535e-90d6-7915973dbef8', 'word_count': 1108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30e70839-e057-535e-90d6-7915973dbef8'}, {'abstract': 'A good quotation can serve as a model for a writer’s own work.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/02/we-are-what-we-quote/', 'snippet': 'A good quotation can serve as a model for a writer’s own work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Quotes are the mental furniture of my life. From certain angles my inner landscape resembles a gallery hung with half-recalled citations, the rags and tag-ends of a lifetime of reading and listening. They can be anything at all, the exquisitely chiseled perceptions of poets and philosophers or the blurts of unscheduled truth-telling by public figures caught in the spotlight (the former Jersey City mayor Frank Hague’s “I am the law” or Richard Nixon’s “I’m not a crook”); the punch lines of 1930s comedians or the curtain lines of Jacobean dramatists; or words of wisdom or anguish or ridiculous humor, or simply, for instance, M.F.K. Fisher’s recollection of “the potato chips I ate slowly one November afternoon in 1936, in the bar of the Lausanne Palace.” They are the dangling threads that memory can latch onto when everything else goes blank. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'We Are What We Quote', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'We Are What We Quote', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quotations and Proverbs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-02T17:59:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': "By Geoffrey O'Brien", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Brien", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d38bd18f-6256-5468-b453-d873d0744601', 'word_count': 1084, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d38bd18f-6256-5468-b453-d873d0744601'}, {'abstract': 'Readers discuss options for the last year of high school.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/opinion/sunday/sunday-dialogue-a-cure-for-senioritis.html', 'snippet': 'Readers discuss options for the last year of high school.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Readers discuss options for the last year of high school.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03LETTERS/03LETTERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 502, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03LETTERS/03LETTERS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 502}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03LETTERS/03LETTERS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 856, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03LETTERS/03LETTERS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1379, 'width': 1649, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03LETTERS/03LETTERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03LETTERS/03LETTERS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03LETTERS/03LETTERS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sunday Dialogue: A Cure for ‘Senioritis’?', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sunday Dialogue: A Cure for ‘Senioritis’?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-02T18:05:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/0987edcb-cea8-53ff-bd14-5c055abb2c39', 'word_count': 2315, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0987edcb-cea8-53ff-bd14-5c055abb2c39'}, {'abstract': 'The brutal winter drought in China is connected to a global wheat shortage is connected to the Arab Spring is connected to ...', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/opinion/sunday/friedman-the-scary-hidden-stressor.html', 'snippet': 'The brutal winter drought in China is connected to a global wheat shortage is connected to the Arab Spring is connected to ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN her introduction to a compelling new study, “The Arab Spring and Climate Change,” released Thursday, the Princeton scholar Anne-Marie Slaughter notes that crime shows often rely on the concept of a “stressor.” A stressor, she explains, is a “sudden change in circumstances or environment that interacts with a complicated psychological profile in a way that leads a previously quiescent person to become violent.” The stressor is never the only explanation for the crime, but it is inevitably an important factor in a complex set of variables that lead to a disaster. “The Arab Spring and Climate Change” doesn’t claim that climate change caused the recent wave of Arab revolutions, but, taken together, the essays make a strong case that the interplay between climate change, food prices (particularly wheat) and politics is a hidden stressor that helped to fuel the revolutions and will continue to make consolidating them into stable democracies much more difficult.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/thomas-l-friedman/thomas-l-friedman-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Scary Hidden Stressor', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Scary Hidden Stressor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Friedman, Thomas L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drought', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wheat', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weather', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Slaughter, Anne-Marie', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-02T18:06:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas L. Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/4504f371-a7c4-5e3f-b6ac-e8010052a78e', 'word_count': 855, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4504f371-a7c4-5e3f-b6ac-e8010052a78e'}, {'abstract': 'The Royal Botanic Gardens’ arborist on bluegrass, fly-fishing and polar bears.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/opinion/sunday/tony-kirkham-an-indiana-jones-of-plants.html', 'snippet': 'The Royal Botanic Gardens’ arborist on bluegrass, fly-fishing and polar bears.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tony Kirkham is the chief arborist at the Royal Botanic Gardens at Kew outside London. He is responsible for the more than 14,000 trees growing there. A sort of Indiana Jones of plants, Mr. Kirkham also travels the world to hunt down exceptional specimens. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03download/03download-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03download/03download-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1427}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/03/sunday-review/03download/03download-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 431, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'super