 letters between characters based, both in temperament and biographical detail, on the writers Flannery O’Connor and Robert Lowell.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Flannery, I love you very much,” the poet Robert Lowell wrote to his friend Flannery O’Connor in 1954. He hastened to add parenthetically, “This isn’t a proposal, I have other eggs to fry.” In her own letter to “A.,” in 1956, O’Connor said of the troubled Lowell, “I feel almost too much about him to get to be able to get to the heart of it. He is a kind of grief to me.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/arts/jpbook/jpbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/04/arts/jpbook/jpbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 677}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/arts/jpbook/jpbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 909, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/arts/jpbook/jpbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1979, 'width': 1755, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/arts/jpbook/jpbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/04/arts/jpbook/jpbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/arts/jpbook/jpbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poetry, Love and Letters: A Romance Imagined', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Poetry, Love and Letters: A Romance Imagined', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'La Rocco, Claudia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lowell, Robert', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': "O'Connor, Flannery", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bauer, Carlene', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-03T20:07:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claudia La Rocco', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claudia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Rocco', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/63be5b93-9244-5781-9e10-8017b5ceaa34', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63be5b93-9244-5781-9e10-8017b5ceaa34'}, {'abstract': 'On opening day in M.L.S., Dallas took advantage of a goalkeeping miscue by Steward Ceus of Dallas in a 1-0 victory.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/opening-day-howler-in-m-l-s/', 'snippet': 'On opening day in M.L.S., Dallas took advantage of a goalkeeping miscue by Steward Ceus of Dallas in a 1-0 victory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saturday was opening day in Major League Soccer’s 2013 season, but it will take some doing to match a howler of epic proportions in Colorado’s 1-0 loss to Dallas in Frisco, Tex.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Opening Day Howler in M.L.S.', 'kicker': 'Goal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Major League Soccer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dallas (Tex)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-03T20:08:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Bell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6cdea84d-fb1c-53e0-ae74-054334da2ff7', 'word_count': 260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6cdea84d-fb1c-53e0-ae74-054334da2ff7'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/arts/television/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (History) THE BIBLE Mark Burnett, the reality television impresario, and his wife, the actress Roma Downey, tackle great biblical moments in this 10-hour mini-series, which Neil Genzlinger, writing in The New York Times, called “a rickety, often cheesy spectacle that is calculated to play well to a certain segment of the already enlisted choir but risks being ignored or scorned in other quarters.” Ms. Downey leads the cast, which also includes Diogo Morgaldo as Jesus, above center, being baptized by John (Daniel Percival). Alas, “Abraham, Moses, David, Daniel and the other great biblical figures aren’t really developed in a way that illuminates them or makes them linger in our minds,” Mr. Genzlinger said. “And those thinking that the ancient miracles might be better served by the special effects available in 2013 than they have been in previous versions should prepare for disappointment. The Red Sea parts no more convincingly here than it did for Charlton Heston in 1956.” (“All-Star Celebrity Apprentice,” of which Mr. Burnett is an executive producer, has its season premiere on NBC at 9.) “Vikings,” making its premiere at 10, fares somewhat better in Mr. Genzlinger’s opinion. It is “a mini-series about a band of professional pillagers with a disregard for human life and a relentless focus on gratifying material desires,” he wrote. “So it is somewhat surprising that it is also a refreshing study in restraint.” Created by Michael Hirst, whose previous projects include “Camelot,” on Starz, and “The Tudors,” on Showtime, “it has the same relatively high production values,” and “sometimes the cinematography is downright arresting,” he said. “But what really drives the series are the performances”; he cited in particular Travis Fimmel, Gabriel Byrne and Gustaf Skarsgard.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-03T20:08:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/9dd1e21a-f447-5bed-98a4-b737eaa92778', 'word_count': 699, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9dd1e21a-f447-5bed-98a4-b737eaa92778'}, {'abstract': 'The online retailer has become a symbol for everything that many Germans resent about American-style management and so-called Anglo-Saxon capitalism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/04/business/global/amazons-labor-relations-under-scrutiny-in-germany.html', 'snippet': 'The online retailer has become a symbol for everything that many Germans resent about American-style management and so-called Anglo-Saxon capitalism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAD HERSFELD, GERMANY — Manuel Sauer, a union activist, stood in front of Amazon’s colossal gray distribution center here last Thursday, masquerading as the company’s chief executive, Jeff Bezos, by holding up a cardboard cutout of a scowling Mr. Bezos. Television crews crowded in for the money shot. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/business/euamazon/euamazon-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/04/business/euamazon/euamazon-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/business/euamazon/euamazon-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/business/euamazon/euamazon-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/business/euamazon/euamazon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/04/business/euamazon/euamazon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/business/euamazon/euamazon-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': '