ey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Whitney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/391fedaf-6c91-51fe-ba33-777ba9f99b45', 'word_count': 639, 'uri': 'nyt://article/391fedaf-6c91-51fe-ba33-777ba9f99b45'}, {'abstract': 'The man who insisted that Western philosophy was based in confusion and wishful thinking is not popular among philosophers. But he should not be dismissed.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/03/was-wittgenstein-right/', 'snippet': 'The man who insisted that Western philosophy was based in confusion and wishful thinking is not popular among philosophers. But he should not be dismissed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The singular achievement of the controversial early 20th century philosopher Ludwig Wittgenstein was to have discerned the true nature of Western philosophy — what is special about its problems, where they come from, how they should and should not be addressed, and what can and cannot be accomplished by grappling with them. The uniquely insightful answers provided to these meta-questions are what give his treatments of specific issues within the subject — concerning language, experience, knowledge, mathematics, art and religion among them — a power of illumination that cannot be found in the work of others.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Was Wittgenstein Right?', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wittgenstein, Ludwig', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-04T01:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Horwich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horwich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/79bc454c-affc-5fe1-9864-bf51edcd1542', 'word_count': 1775, 'uri': 'nyt://article/79bc454c-affc-5fe1-9864-bf51edcd1542'}, {'abstract': 'Sixteen years after leaving ESPN, Keith Olbermann and his representatives have expressed an interest in his return to the employer that made him famous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/04/sports/keith-olbermann-expresses-interest-in-return-to-espn.html', 'snippet': 'Sixteen years after leaving ESPN, Keith Olbermann and his representatives have expressed an interest in his return to the employer that made him famous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Keith Olbermann was in Los Angeles on Friday being deposed for a reported $70 million lawsuit he filed against his most recent employer, Current TV, with the trial expected to begin in May. Whether the court rules in favor of Olbermann or the network, the verdict will put an official end to a one-year stint at Current that was supposed to last at least five. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/sports/YOLBERMANN/YOLBERMANN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 481, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/04/sports/YOLBERMANN/YOLBERMANN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 481}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/sports/YOLBERMANN/YOLBERMANN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 820, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/sports/YOLBERMANN/YOLBERMANN-superJumbo.jpg'