zation': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f056cbf4-764d-5cda-8a90-7d9c138f8df6', 'word_count': 413, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f056cbf4-764d-5cda-8a90-7d9c138f8df6'}, {'abstract': 'Political theater became life when the Russian immigration police interrupted a performance on Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/04/in-russia-political-theater-becomes-life-in-the-moscow-trials/', 'snippet': 'Political theater became life when the Russian immigration police interrupted a performance on Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — The press materials called it “political theater.” Last Friday through Sunday, three of the most important Russian political trials of the last decade were to be re-enacted in compressed form by people who had either taken part in them or might have taken part in them. Staged by the Swiss director Milo Rau, the “Moscow Trials” series was being performed at the Andrei Sakharov Museum and Public Center, itself a contested space. It would be filmed, for airing on television, presumably anywhere but in Russia, where a Western view of these trials could hardly be broadcast to a wide audience.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Pale Reflection of Reality', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pussy Riot', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Moscow (Russia)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-04T12:43:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Masha Gessen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Masha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gessen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': N