on today’s paper. Good luck!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Quiz | March 5, 2013', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-05T06:30:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anastasia Economides', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anastasia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Economides', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/366e88ad-a5c2-5154-b73c-92f9552607d5', 'word_count': 22, 'uri': 'nyt://article/366e88ad-a5c2-5154-b73c-92f9552607d5'}, {'abstract': 'Malaysian forces also led a ground assault on an armed group that has occupied parts of northern Borneo Island for weeks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/06/world/asia/malaysia-attacks-filipino-rebels-on-borneo-island.html', 'snippet': 'Malaysian forces also led a ground assault on an armed group that has occupied parts of northern Borneo Island for weeks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MANILA — Malaysia used airstrikes and mobilized thousands of troops on Tuesday to try to put an end to a monthlong quixotic incursion by a band of gunmen from the Philippines seeking to reclaim part of Borneo Island for a defunct sultanate.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Malaysia Attacks Filipino Rebels With Jets and Mortars', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Malaysia: Government Forces Strafe Filipino Gunmen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Borneo (Malaysia)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Philippines', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Malaysia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-05T06:55:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Floyd Whaley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Floyd', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Whaley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4ece036-2048-5017-8032-661a26f12b0e', 'word_count': 784, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4ece036-2048-5017-8032-661a26f12b0e'}, {'abstract': 'Bolstered by support from China, the United States introduced a draft resolution on Tuesday that would intensify sanctions on North Korea because of its nuclear test last month.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/06/world/asia/china-said-to-back-new-sanctions-against-north-korea.html', 'snippet': 'Bolstered by support from China, the United States introduced a draft resolution on Tuesday that would intensify sanctions on North Korea because of its nuclear test last month.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The United Nations Security Council moved closer on Tuesday to expanding sanctions on North Korea for its nuclear and ballistic missile activities. The United States and China introduced a resolution that would target North Korean bankers and overseas cash couriers, tighten inspections of suspect ship and air cargo, and subject the country’s diplomats to invasive scrutiny and increased risk of expulsion. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.N. Resolution to Aim at North Korean Banks and Diplomats', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.N. Resolution Aims At North Korean Banks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Economic Sanctions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Security Council (UN)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Tests', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-05T07:34:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc555d88-3782-51b4-abda-ad91ba3d6387', 'word_count': 879, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc555d88-3782-51b4-abda-ad91ba3d6387'}, {'abstract': 'Tensions rise in Iraq and Syria; Kenyans go to the polls; protesters object to the removal of the Berlin Wall; and baseball fever takes hold in Cuba.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/05/iht-quick-read-march-5/', 'snippet': 'Tensions rise in Iraq and Syria; Kenyans go to the polls; protesters object to the removal of the Berlin Wall; and baseball fever takes hold in Cuba.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWS Millions of Kenyans poured into polling stations on Monday to cast their ballots in a crucial, anxiously awaited presidential election, and as the voting proceeded relatively smoothly a real chance emerged that a candidate charged with crimes against humanity could win the race. Jeffrey Gettleman reports from Nairobi.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/05/world/05berlin1_span/05berlin1_span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 355,