'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-05T09:54:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rahul Joglekar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rahul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Joglekar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea5d777a-7ecf-5364-ae69-a8b9b4e79751', 'word_count': 632, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea5d777a-7ecf-5364-ae69-a8b9b4e79751'}, {'abstract': 'Your personal austerity program.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/magazine/how-economics-can-help-you-lose-weight.html', 'snippet': 'Your personal austerity program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last month, I visited the southern New Jersey plant of the Robard Corporation, a company that makes meal-replacement products for dieters. Robard’s president, Robert Schwartz, took me to the factory floor, where huge vats of powders — proteins, flavoring ingredients, vitamins — are poured into a 450-gallon V-shaped industrial mixer. The ingredients are then spun together and turned into protein shakes that sell for around $3.50 a serving. As I watched this process, it seemed as if Robard were in the world’s easiest business: take dirt-cheap raw materials, mix them together and sell them at a huge markup to America’s tens of millions of desperate dieters, like me.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/magazine/10economy/10economy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 778, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/10/magazine/10economy/10economy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 778}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201