03/05/facebook-news-feed-draws-more-criticism/', 'snippet': 'While Facebook responded to criticism that it was suppressing content in its news feed in order to highlight advertising posts, small businesses started voicing concerns about the company’s actions too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In my column this week, I wrote about how content I shared on Facebook saw a paltry amount of interaction unless I paid Facebook to highlight it on the company’s signature news feed. Other\xa0reporters\xa0and\xa0bloggers\xa0said they had seen a similar drop in connection.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/technology/bits-fb/bits-fb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 592, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/technology/bits-fb/bits-fb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 448, 'width': 592, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/technology/bits-fb/bits-fb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/04/technology/bits-fb/bits-fb-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/04/technology/bits-fb/bits-fb-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Facebook News Feed Draws More Criticism', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-05T18:33:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/918c3af0-391f-53cf-9ad5-fd9a7a1209d3', 'word_count': 478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/918c3af0-391f-53cf-9ad5-fd9a7a1209d3'}, {'abstract': 'John A. Catsimatidis, the supermarket magnate turned political upstart, is said to have paid to run the 30-second ad for two months.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/05/a-well-heeled-candidate-runs-political-seasons-first-radio-ad/', 'snippet': 'John A. Catsimatidis, the supermarket magnate turned political upstart, is said to have paid to run the 30-second ad for two months.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is a scrappy, somewhat corny political ad (click to listen) featuring a stilted exchange about a New York City mayoral candidate that ends with a woman asking, “How do you pronounce his name again?” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/05/nyregion/00jcats-cityroom/00jcats-cityroom-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 840, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/05/nyregion/00jcats-cityroom/00jcats-cityroom-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 840}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/05/nyregion/00jcats-cityroom/00jcats-cityroom-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 732, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/05/nyregion/00jcats-cityroom/00jcats-cityroom-superJumbo.jpg