 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Dominican Republic', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Galvan, Miguel', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Congress', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostitution', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-06T02:23:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frances Robles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frances', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5dc4164b-a90e-5a20-8115-9ff8ea748e6b', 'word_count': 584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5dc4164b-a90e-5a20-8115-9ff8ea748e6b'}, {'abstract': 'The idea that philosophy is purely descriptive, and should “leave the world as it is” falls short. It can play a more radical role.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/05/of-flies-and-philosophers-wittgenstein-and-philosophy/', 'snippet': 'The idea that philosophy is purely descriptive, and should “leave the world as it is” falls short. It can play a more radical role.', 'lead_paragraph': '“To show the fly the way out of the fly bottle ”— that, Wittgenstein once said, was the aim of his philosophy. While it is perhaps unclear whether anyone — philosopher or fly — should be flattered by this comparison, his overall point is clear enough, as Paul Horwich notes in his recent piece, “Was Wittgenstein Right?” When we get curious about philosophical problems we are drawn into puzzles by the promise of sweet enlightenment, only to find ourselves caught in frustration (and banging our heads against the same wall over and over again). What we need, Wittgenstein thinks, is liberation — liberation from the prison of pseudo-problems we have brought upon ourselves; liberation from traditional philosophy. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Of Flies and Philosophers: Wittgenstein and Philosophy', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philosophy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lynch, Michael P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-06T02:30:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael P. Lynch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'P.', 'lastname': 'Lynch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78c6f924-e312-5fae-a0e7-32f36e7ee1d7', 'word_count': 1219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78c6f924-e312-5fae-a0e7-32f36e7ee1d7'}, {'abstract': 'The first trip to Europe for Yankees pitcher Phil Hughes included a harrowing and interesting trip to an English soccer game in Manchester.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/06/sports/baseball/for-yankees-hughes-a-european-vacation-with-an-edge.html', 'snippet': 'The first trip to Europe for Yankees pitcher Phil Hughes included a harrowing and interesting trip to an English soccer game in Manchester.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TAMPA, Fla. — Phil Hughes has never sat in the bleachers at Yankee Stadium, but having warmed up in the bullpen below, he has a good sense of the raucous atmosphere there during some games.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/06/sports/YYANKEES/YYANKEES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 853, 'width': 512, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}},