:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/df5b08e5-0b95-530d-9c49-7598e476945b', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df5b08e5-0b95-530d-9c49-7598e476945b'}, {'abstract': 'Danny Burstein and Sarah Paulson star in the Roundabout’s revival of “Talley’s Folly,” Lanford Wilson’s romantic comedy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/06/theater/reviews/talleys-folly-revived-at-the-roundabout.html', 'snippet': 'Danny Burstein and Sarah Paulson star in the Roundabout’s revival of “Talley’s Folly,” Lanford Wilson’s romantic comedy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'That most seductive of notions, the dream that one enchanted evening can change the course of two lives, takes beguiling dramatic form in “Talley’s Folly,” the Pulitzer Prize-winning play by Lanford Wilson that has been lovingly revived by the Roundabout Theater Company at the Laura Pels Theater. Portraying a pair of mismatched mates trying to find a mutual rhythm for a waltz that at least one is hoping will last forever, Danny Burstein and Sarah Paulson infuse this gossamer two-character play with a warm, slightly spiky humanity that breathes vital life into material that might in lesser hands feel creaky or sentimental.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/06/arts/06TALLEY/06TALLEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/06/arts/06TALLEY/06TALLEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'capti