ew York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88c39698-dfb5-5ad6-b0e4-b077cf80dcce', 'word_count': 32, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88c39698-dfb5-5ad6-b0e4-b077cf80dcce'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Brantley on “Money: The Game Show” and a revival of “Privates on Parade,” starring Simon Russell Beale.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/06/london-theater-journal-follow-the-money/', 'snippet': 'Ben Brantley on “Money: The Game Show” and a revival of “Privates on Parade,” starring Simon Russell Beale.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — It felt like a good day to go to a funeral for the financial system that rules the Western world. My reading on the subway had been the Evening Standard (now a giveaway), which featured a lead story with blaring headlines about bankers’ bonuses and money laundering. (The next day I would awaken to a grim front-page essay in the Guardian on the same subject with the no-win headline “Too big to fail – and too big to manage.”)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/05/theater/05money/05money-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/05/theater/05money/05money-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/05/theater/05money/05money-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/05/theater/05money/05money-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/05/theater/05money/05money-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/05/theater/05money/05money-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/05/theater/05money/05money-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'London Theater Journal: Follow the Money', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beale, Simon Russell', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nichols, Peter', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bush Theater', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-06T13:00:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4428023f-9946-5aca-b1a2-545ee63879f4', 'word_count': 804, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4428023f-9946-5aca-b1a2-545ee63879f4'}, {'abstract': 'David Bowie’s new album, “The Next Day,” to be released on Columbia on Tuesday, is receiving acclaim as the artist’s strongest effort in decades.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/arts/music/the-singer-who-fell-to-earth.html', 'snippet': 'David Bowie’s new album, “The Next Day,” to be released on Columbia on Tuesday, is receiving acclaim as the artist’s strongest effort in decades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On “The Stars (Are Out Tonight),” the new single from David Bowie’s comeback album, “The Next Day,” one line jumps out: “We will never be rid of these stars.”\xa0In the video Mr. Bowie and the actress Tilda Swinton play an elderly couple persecuted by a pair of vampiric stars, who stalk them, invade their house and manipulate them like marionettes. But the song itself is less literal. It portrays celebrities as members of an overlord class who “burn you with their radiant smiles” but also as faintly pitiable creatures, jealous of the quiet, grounded lives of ordinary folk.\xa0“But I hope they live forever,” Mr. Bowie sings, a nod to the notion of fame as immortality, a compensation for all the damage and delusion that comes with the territory.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/arts/10BOWIE_SPAN/10BOWIE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/10/arts/10BOWIE_SPAN/10BOWIE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/arts/10BOWIE_SPAN/10BOWIE_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/arts/10BOWIE_SPAN/10BOWIE_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1362, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/arts/10BOWIE_SPAN/10BOWIE_SPAN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/