ction_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/daaca920-6f45-5771-b497-51c12240410f', 'word_count': 83, 'uri': 'nyt://article/daaca920-6f45-5771-b497-51c12240410f'}, {'abstract': 'Under pressure from their fellow cardinals, the Americans canceled their news briefing and shut down all communication with the news media.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/world/europe/roman-catholic-cardinals-discuss-hopes-and-expectations-of-new-pope.html', 'snippet': 'Under pressure from their fellow cardinals, the Americans canceled their news briefing and shut down all communication with the news media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VATICAN CITY — Journalists from around the world packed the daily briefings, relishing the rare opportunity to pose questions to the cardinals who will vote in the conclave to elect the next pope. The sessions, led by American cardinals, were a far cry from the elliptical approach favored by the Vatican. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/06/blogs/20130306POD-slide-0YES/20130306POD-slide-0YES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/06/blogs/20130306POD-slide-0YES/20130306POD-slide-0YES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/06/blogs/20130306POD-slide-0YES/20130306POD-slide-0YES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', '