'lead_paragraph': 'The latest evidence that Brooklyn is a hotbed of progressive design comes from Souda (pronounced SOH-duh), a Japanese word that translates to “oh, yeah” or “aha!” That’s the name of a design group started by three 2012 graduates of Parsons the New School for Design: Isaac Friedman-Helman, Shaun Kasperbauer and Luft Tanaka.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07LIGHTING/07CURR1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07LIGHTING/07CURR1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07LIGHTING/07CURR1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07LIGHTING/07CURR1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07LIGHTING/07CURR1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07LIGHTING/07CURR1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07LIGHTING/07CURR1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In Brooklyn, Tripping the Plastic Fantastic', 'kicker': 'Currents | Lighting', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Brooklyn, Tripping The Plastic Fantastic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hirst, Arlene', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lighting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Plastic Bottles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bushwick (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Souda', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lamps and Lampshades', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-06T23:28:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Arlene Hirst', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Arlene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hirst', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c14e5ee-8ce0-5849-89fa-64f08733637b', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c14e5ee-8ce0-5849-89fa-64f08733637b'}, {'abstract': 'For four-legged friends, a dog bed, and for the two-legged types, a bed skirt and pillow shams.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/garden/from-scalamandre-zebra-print-bedding-for-people-and-dogs.html', 'snippet': 'For four-legged friends, a dog bed, and for the two-legged types, a bed skirt and pillow shams.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since 1945, when Flora Scalamandré designed a red zebra wallpaper for the New York restaurant Gino of Capri, the pattern has migrated to umbrellas, china and tote bags. Now it can be found on bedding for people and pets alike. In December Scalamandré introduced a dog bed in the perky stripes, and next month it will follow with a zebra bed skirt and pillow shams for humans.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07BEDDING1/07BEDDING1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 540, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07BEDDING1/07BEDDING1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 540}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07BEDDING1/07BEDDING1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 922, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07BEDDING1/07BEDDING1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1594, 'width': 1770, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07BEDDING1/07BEDDING1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07BEDDING1/07BEDDING1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07BEDDING1/07BEDDING1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Basic Zebra Commands: Sit, Stay, Roll Over, Sleep', 'kicker': 'Currents | Bedding', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Basic Zebra Commands: Sit, Stay, Roll Over, Sleep', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Beds and Bedding', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Louie, Elaine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scalamandre', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shopping and Retail', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-06T23:29:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Louie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Louie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bfb4a320-faed-5afc-8ccc-0829f013e7f6', 'word_count': 148, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bfb4a320-faed-5afc-8ccc-0829f013e7f6'}, {'abstract': 'Cabinet knobs shaped like the heads of four birds: a finch, grosbeak, cardinal and woodpecker.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/garden/from-ted-boerner-jewelry-for-your-furniture.html', 'snippet': 'Cabinet knobs shaped like the heads of four birds: a finch, grosbeak, cardinal and woodpecker.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The San Francisco-based designer Ted Boerner has teamed up with Rocky Mountain Hardware to create four drawer pulls in the shape of bird heads: a finch, grosbeak, cardinal (shown) and woodpecker. Each one is cast in bronze at the Rocky Mountain foundry in Blackfoot, Idaho, using the centuries-old lost-wax method. (Wax molds are dipped into a liquid ceramic slurry multiple times and hardened. Then the wax is melted out of the shell, liquid bronze is poured in to replace it, and the ceramic shell is cracked off after the metal cools.)', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/garden/07HARDWARE_SPAN/07HARDWARE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'im