'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By John W. Fitzpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Fitzpatrick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/878ccea6-a22e-5a42-9e8f-51cc6e53bc26', 'word_count': 809, 'uri': 'nyt://article/878ccea6-a22e-5a42-9e8f-51cc6e53bc26'}, {'abstract': 'The Supreme Court appears ready to eviscerate the Voting Rights Act — and seriously harm itself in the process.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/06/a-big-new-power/', 'snippet': 'The Supreme Court appears ready to eviscerate the Voting Rights Act — and seriously harm itself in the process.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Years from now, when the Supreme Court has come to its senses, justices then sitting will look back on the spring of 2013 in bewilderment. On what basis, they will wonder, did five conservative justices, professed believers in judicial restraint, reach out to grab the authority that the framers of the post-Civil War 14th and 15th Amendments had vested in Congress nearly a century and a half earlier “to enforce, by appropriate legislation” the right to equal protection and the right to vote. How on earth did it come to pass that the Supreme Court ruled a major provision of the Voting Rights Act of 1965 unconstitutional?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘A Big New Power’', 'kicker': 'Linda Greenhouse', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitution (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting Rights Act (1965)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roberts, John G Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scalia, Antonin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Will, George F', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Shelby County (Ala)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T02:00:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56afd7a2-f9a0-55e2-8f80-d2b2b36986eb', 'word_count': 1478, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56afd7a2-f9a0-55e2-8f80-d2b2b36986eb'}, {'abstract': 'Alarmism erodes credibility, but real danger can still lurk.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/opinion/blow-cry-about-the-real-wolf.html', 'snippet': 'Alarmism erodes credibility, but real danger can still lurk.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The White House horribly botched its messaging on the sequester.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/charles-m-blow/charles-m-blow-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/charles-m-blow/charles-m-blow-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/charles-m-blow/charles-m-blow-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion/charles-m-blow/charles-m-blow-superJumbo.png', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name