aragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday Reading: Apps That Help With a Breakup', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T13:44:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d36bdc9f-f953-5fb2-9444-841c22d0a812', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d36bdc9f-f953-5fb2-9444-841c22d0a812'}, {'abstract': 'There is no issue more sensitive for the average Egyptian than the availability and price of diesel fuel.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/diesel-fuel-the-leading-concern-of-most-egyptians/', 'snippet': 'There is no issue more sensitive for the average Egyptian than the availability and price of diesel fuel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — The Egyptian pound is weakening at an alarming rate. Hard currency reserves are dwindling. Unemployment is up; investment is down. Imports are being restricted, and banks are no longer giving out loans. The list of worrisome indicators goes on, but there is no word that better encapsulates the severity of Egypt’s economic situation than “solar.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Solar Eclipse', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rationing and Allocation of Resources', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T13:46:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Issandr El Amrani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Issandr', 'middlename': 'El', 'lastname': 'Amrani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fedf9680-e6cb-5273-935d-8f5570c2c26a', 'word_count': 498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fedf9680-e6cb-5273-935d-8f5570c2c26a'}, {'abstract': 'The events will celebrate the Spanish poet Federico García Lorca, who lived in New York City in 1929 and 1930.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/lorca-celebration-set-for-spring-in-new-york/', 'snippet': 'The events will celebrate the Spanish poet Federico García Lorca, who lived in New York City in 1929 and 1930.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Spanish writer Federico García Lorca, who lived in New York in 1929 and 1930, and wrote about the experience in a book of verse, “Poet in New York,” will be the subject of a citywide festival anchored by an exhibition this spring.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Citywide Festival to Celebrate García Lorca’s Years in New York', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Garcia Lorca, Federico', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Public Library', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T14:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78334a17-5a53-5163-a118-2613c7376811', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78334a17-5a53-5163-a118-2613c7376811'}, {'abstract': 'The new FEED function bag, a messenger-like iteration on the brand’s familiar burlap theme, is being promoted with photos taken by Lauren Bush Lauren herself.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/the-multi-tasker/', 'snippet': 'The new FEED function bag, a messenger-like iteration on the brand’s familiar burlap theme, is being promoted with photos taken by Lauren Bush Lauren herself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her brief tenure on earth, 28-year-old Lauren Bush Lauren has already occupied many roles in the public sphere: presidential niece, model, wife of David Lauren, fashion designer, social entrepreneur, philanthropist — and now, photographer. Her latest venture with FEED Projects — her six-year-old company that sells utilitarian-chic, grain-sack-inspired bags and donates school meals to the world’s hungry children with each sale — is the FEED function bag, available now on the company’s Web site. It’s a messenger-like iteration of FEED’s familiar burlap theme, with a detachable shoulder strap, a faux suede bottom and an interior outfitted with pockets for today’s many gadgets. To promote it, Bush Lauren, who minored in photography in college, has shot portraits of a smattering of her many creative (and busy) friends, among them the photographer Henry Hargreaves (above), the Town and Country editor Amanda Hearst and Lehmann Maupin’s Carla Camacho, all pictured carrying the bag. “We wanted to show real people that I am inspired by, and how they function,” Bush Lauren says of the project, which can be viewed on FEED’s Web site. “Instead of hiring a photographer, I thought: I would love to do this.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Multi-Tasker', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Still Multitasking', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T14:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia Felsenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Felsenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7f516ac-3597-5790-81b3-120151fd2a4d', 'word_count': 265, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7f516ac-3597-5790-81b3-120151fd2a4d'}, {'abstract': 'The European Union fined Microsoft $732 million on Wednesday for failing to respect an antitrust settlement with regulators, but in a highly unusual admission, the union’s top antitrust regulator said his department bore some responsibility for situation that led to the fine.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/european-regulators-fine-microsoft-and-then-promise-to-do-better/', 'snippet': 'The European Union fined Microsoft $732 million on Wednesday for failing to respect an antitrust settlement with regulators, but in a highly unusual admission, the union’s top antitrust regulator said his department bore some responsibility for si...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The European Union fined Microsoft $732 million on Wednesday for failing to respect an antitrust settlement with regulators. But in a highly unusual mea culpa, the European Union’s top antitrust regulator said his department bore some responsibility for Microsoft’s failure to respect a settlement that caused the fine, James Kanter writes in The New York Times. Read more. »', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'European Regulators Fine Microsoft, and Then Promise to Do Better', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T14:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a665c69e-a00f-5a40-8985-21bd67183741', 'word_count': 59, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a665c69e-a00f-5a40-8985-21bd67183741'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama will have a bipartisan lunch on Thursday with Representative Paul D. Ryan, the Republican chairman of the House Budget Committee, and the panel’s senior Democrat, Representative Chris Van Hollen.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/obama-continues-congressional-outreach-with-white-house-lunch/', 'snippet': 'President Obama will have a bipartisan lunch on Thursday with Representative Paul D. Ryan, the Republican chairman of the House Budget Committee, and the panel’s senior Democrat, Representative Chris Van Hollen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Following on his unusual dinner on Wednesday with a dozen Republican senators, President Obama will have a bipartisan lunch on Thursday with Representative Paul D. Ryan, the Republican chairman of the House Budget Committee, and the panel’s senior Democrat, Representative Chris Van Hollen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Continues Congressional Outreach With White House Lunch', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T14:01:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jackie Calmes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jackie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Calmes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b813e803-a170-56f3-a220-1e935ccca292', 'word_count': 954, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b813e803-a170-56f3-a220-1e935ccca292'}, {'abstract': 'In oral arguments before the Supreme Court last week, Chief Justice John G. Roberts Jr. introduced a statistical claim that he took to imply that an important provision of the Voting Rights Act has become outmoded.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/in-supreme-court-debate-on-voting-rights-act-a-dubious-use-of-statistics/', 'snippet': 'In oral arguments before the Supreme Court last week, Chief Justice John G. Roberts Jr. introduced a statistical claim that he took to imply that an important provision of the Voting Rights Act has become outmoded.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In oral arguments before the Supreme Court last week, Chief Justice John G. Roberts Jr. introduced a statistical claim that he took to imply that an important provision of the Voting Rights Act has become outmoded.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Supreme Court Debate on Voting Rights Act, a Dubious Use of Statistics', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Justice’s Use Of Statistics, Viewed Skeptically', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Statistics', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting Rights Act (1965)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Roberts, John G Jr', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mississippi', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T14:02:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/170dbd68-4620-5174-8277-37af1cf42a2a', 'word_count': 1623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/170dbd68-4620-5174-8277-37af1cf42a2a'}, {'abstract': 'Time Warner will spin off its magazine titles, Facebook will revamp its News Feed and Google executives try to bring advertisers into social media.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/the-breakfast-meeting-time-warner-to-spin-off-time-inc-and-facebooks-new-look/', 'snippet': 'Time Warner will spin off its magazine titles, Facebook will revamp its News Feed and Google executives try to bring advertisers into social media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Time Warner announced Wednesday that it would spin off its Time Inc. magazine unit into a separate, publicly traded company, Amy Chozick writes. The announcement came hours after negotiations on a possible merger between Meredith Corporation and many Time Inc. titles fell through. The deal did not work out in part because of Time Inc.’s concern about flagship titles Time, Sports Illustrated, Fortune and Money, which Meredith had decided not to pursue. Time Warner found it made more economic sense to spin off all its titles rather than retain a few of them, a person with knowledge of the negotiations said. Time Inc. is the nation’s largest magazine publisher, and the breakdown in talks threw into sharp relief how once-glamorous magazines have become troubled assets.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Time Warner to Spin Off Time Inc., and Facebook’s New Look', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T14:10:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel E. Slotnik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Slotnik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec2e50c3-67c9-5517-abd7-90b5aec2c40d', 'word_count': 654, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec2e50c3-67c9-5517-abd7-90b5aec2c40d'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s Poetry Pairing matches “One Girl of Many,” a poem by Charlotte Anna Perkins Gilman, with “Taking the High Road” by Abby Goodnough.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/poetry-pairing-one-girl-of-many/', 'snippet': 'This week’s Poetry Pairing matches “One Girl of Many,” a poem by Charlotte Anna Perkins Gilman, with “Taking the High Road” by Abby Goodnough.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In honor of Women’s History Month, this week’s Poetry Pairing matches “One Girl of Many,” a poem by Charlotte Anna Perkins Gilman, with “Taking the High Road” by Abby Goodnough.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/22/education/22HUMANITY1-LN/22HUMANITY1-LN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 331, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/07/22/education/22HUMANITY1-LN/22HUMANITY1-LN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 331}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/07/22/education/22HUMANITY1-LN/22HUMANITY1-LN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 564, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'