xplosion in food allergies among children, many of whom react to several different foods? Some flummoxed families set out to bankroll the research themselves. – Patrick Farrell', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T14:54:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/58444a04-42e9-52f1-be26-8b5cedf8fae0', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58444a04-42e9-52f1-be26-8b5cedf8fae0'}, {'abstract': 'With his unconventional layouts and sensual use of materials, Ward Bennett attracted a clientele as rarefied as his designs. Meet the unsung hero of 1970s modernism.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/hidden-talent/', 'snippet': 'With his unconventional layouts and sensual use of materials, Ward Bennett attracted a clientele as rarefied as his designs. Meet the unsung hero of 1970s modernism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 1970s have always been a nebulous time for style, particularly interior design. An era — or was it a moment between two eras? — full of brazen promise at the beginning, but to which the harsh light of passing years has not always been kind. Especially when it comes to modernism. Picture conversation pits, exposed reel-to-reel stereo equipment or George Hamilton in a leather vest, shirtless. Of course, there are also the highlights, like Babe Paley in a caftan, or the town house rebuilt for Halston on 63rd Street by Paul Rudolph, snapshots of a moment whose fabulousness is undimmed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hidden Talent', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T15:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Netto', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Netto', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5bf3533-fd40-556b-af90-d2b9851f1346', 'word_count': 1277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5bf3533-fd40-556b-af90-d2b9851f1346'}, {'abstract': 'The Honda Civic is the first small car to earn the Insurance Institute for Highway Safety’s new Top Safety Pick+ award based on their performance in the new small overlap frontal crash test.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/more-vehicles-earn-insurance-institutes-top-safety-award/', 'snippet': 'The Honda Civic is the first small car to earn the Insurance Institute for Highway Safety’s new Top Safety Pick+ award based on their performance in the new small overlap frontal crash test.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Honda Civic two-door and four-door models are the first small cars to earn the Insurance Institute for Highway Safety’s new Top Safety Pick+ award based on their performance in the new small overlap frontal crash test.', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Vehicles Earn Insurance Institute’s Top Safety Award', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Vehicles Earn A Top Safety Award', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defects', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Cars (Compact, Subcompact and Microca