'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c5dfc07a-3b35-5fe3-91e5-a1603b427a64', 'word_count': 727, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c5dfc07a-3b35-5fe3-91e5-a1603b427a64'}, {'abstract': 'A long, deep look at the strategy and tactics of Bill McKibben, the ultramarathoner of climate campaigners.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/a-communications-scholar-analyzes-bill-mckibbens-path-on-climate/', 'snippet': 'A long, deep look at the strategy and tactics of Bill McKibben, the ultramarathoner of climate campaigners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bill McKibben and I have been on parallel but very different journeys related to human-driven global warming since the greenhouse effect first became front-page news back in the late 1980s (examples here and here). (Our video chat above was done in December for my Pace University blogging class.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Communications Scholar Analyzes Bill McKibben’s Path on Climate', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endowments', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Keystone Pipeline System', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T15:51:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/321ca38d-a43f-59b8-b572-ba7139c2a566', 'word_count': 357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/321ca38d-a43f-59b8-b572-ba7139c2a566'}, {'abstract': 'Kenya’s election was supposed to be the most modern in Africa. But almost everything that could go wrong did.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/in-kenyas-high-tech-election-almost-everything-that-could-have-gone-wrong-did/', 'snippet': 'Kenya’s election was supposed to be the most modern in Africa. But almost everything that could go wrong did.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NAIROBI — I’m thinking of calling it School Socket Syndrome. That’s my term for the screech heard when modern technology is suddenly grafted onto a developing state’s crumbling infrastructure.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'School Socket Syndrome', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voter Registration and Requirements', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kenya', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T15:56:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michela Wrong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wrong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e49e7d3-7376-5f0c-8db2-