t/', 'snippet': 'How will the likely next C.I.A. chief handle what is “by far the most thorough examination of how the United States came to use nudity, cold, sleep deprivation, stress positions, wall-slamming and waterboarding, methods it had long condemned as ab...', 'lead_paragraph': 'A 6,000-page Congressional report on the Central Intelligence Agency’s use of so-called enhanced interrogation techniques is “by far the most thorough examination of how the United States came to use nudity, cold, sleep deprivation, stress positions, wall-slamming and waterboarding, methods it had long condemned as abuse or torture,” reports Scott Shane, a national security correspondent. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'John Brennan and the Classified C.I.A. Torture Report', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interrogations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Torture', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waterboarding', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brennan, John O', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T17:59:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By The International Herald Tribune', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The International Herald Tribune'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d58006c-4a55-5e75-b964-8f10b13cae0f', 'word_count': 88, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d58006c-4a55-5e75-b964-8f10b13cae0f'}, {'abstract': 'The Times’s David E. Sanger on North Korea’s nuclear strike warnings as it faces a new round of sanctions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/world/asia/100000002105824/bold-threats.html', 'snippet': 'The Times’s David E. Sanger on North Korea’s nuclear strike warnings as it faces a new round of sanctions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Times’s David E. Sanger on North Korea’s nuclear strike warnings as it faces a new round of sanctions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/world/video-tc-130307-korea/video-tc-130307-korea-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/07/world/video-tc-130307-korea/video-tc-130307-korea-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/world/video-tc-130307-korea/video-tc-130307-korea-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/07/world/video-tc-130307-korea/video-tc-130307-korea-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/world/video-tc-130307-korea/video-tc-130307-korea-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bold Threats From North Korea', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Economic Sanctions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Tests', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T17:59:49+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'Ben Werschkul, Channon Hodge and Pedro Rafael Rosado', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werschkul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Channon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Pedro', 'middlename': 'Rafael', 'lastname': 'Rosado', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/7b79f41f-324c-53fe-99d3-e493270cc2fd', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/7b79f41f-324c-53fe-99d3-e493270cc2fd'}, {'abstract': 'Danh Vo cleverly assembles the personal effects of Robert McNamara, the former secretary of defense, for his new show at Marian Goodman Gallery.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/the-artist-as-curator/', 'snippet': 'Danh Vo cleverly assembles the personal effects of Robert McNamara, the former secretary of defense, for his new show at Marian Goodman Gallery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Born in Vietnam and raised in Denmark, the conceptualist Danh Vo’s rapid rise in the art world can be attributed to his incisive and enigmatic style. His most famous work to date involved “building” a to-scale model of the Statue of Liberty: the hundreds of discrete chunks were never actually assembled into a whole; instead they were scattered around the globe to represent the reach of democracy and American imperialism. Last year, Vo won the Guggenheim’s prestigious Hugo Boss Prize, a biennial award that comes with a solo show at the museum. Continuing his exploration of identity and heritage, Vo has filled his show, which opens on March 15, with artwork, kitschy Americana (above right) and Oriental knickknacks that belonged to the late painter Martin Wong. “I consider it a kind of collaboration,” he says. “And I don’t have any problems collaborating with dead people.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Artist as Curator', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vo, Danh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T18:00:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kevin McGarry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGarry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c827aff-61b0-52d5-83c9-afe6a2b7a1ef', 'word_count': 223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c827aff-61b0-52d5-83c9-afe6a2b7a1ef'}, {'abstract': 'Alaska Airlines, AirTran Airways and US Airways were the most punctual North American carriers last year, says a company that tracks air travel.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/on-the-ground-on-time/', 'snippet': 'Alaska Airlines, AirTran Airways and US Airways were the most punctual North American carriers last year, says a company that tracks air travel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FlightStats, a company that tracks air travel, released its annual On-time Performance Service awards for 2012. Alaska Airlines, AirTran Airways and US Airways were the top North American carriers last year; Japan Airlines, All Nippon Airways and Scandinavian Airlines were the top three international ones. The rankings were based on the percentage of the carriers’ flights that arrived within 15 minutes of their scheduled time.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Ground on Time', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Air: On the Ground on Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ALASKA AIRLINES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'US Airways Group Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T18:00:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanya Mohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0847357-86ee-5066-9855-3a2ab0ce8f15', 'word_count': 65, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0847357-86ee-5066-9855-3a2ab0ce8f15'}, {'abstract': 'Shortcomings continue particularly in discrimination against the Muslim Rohingya minority and abuses in northern Kachin State, the investigator said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/world/asia/myanmar-reforms-could-falter-un-investigator-says.html', 'snippet': 'Shortcomings continue particularly in discrimination against the Muslim Rohingya minority and abuses in northern Kachin State, the investigator said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GENEVA — Political reforms that are delivering greater freedom in Myanmar could falter if the authorities do not tackle some conspicuous failings, including continuing torture in prisons and discrimination against ethnic minorities, a United Nations investigator said in a report released on Thursday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Myanmar Reforms Could Falter, U.N. Investigator Says', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.N. Report\r\nCites Failings\r\nIn Myanmar', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cumming-Bruce, Nick', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Myanmar', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations Human Rights Council', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T18:03:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Cumming-Bruce', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cumming-Bruce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8613fb10-1b48-579b-8b55-6e46f671d021', 'word_count': 625, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8613fb10-1b48-579b-8b55-6e46f671d021'}, {'abstract': 'The officials are not the continent.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/bushifying-the-berlaymont/', 'snippet': 'The officials are not the continent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Busy day today, so no blogging until late. But a quick thought in passing: reading what the three tweeters of Brussels had to say, it struck me that they have a habit of mind that, to my mind, is more disturbing than a bit of incivility here and there. In fact, they sound remarkably like George W. Bush.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bushifying the Berlaymont', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T18:06:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organizati