rl': 'images/2013/03/08/arts/08ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1626, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/08/arts/08ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/08/arts/08ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/08/arts/08ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Romanov Legacy Finds New Life', 'kicker': 'Antiques', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Romanov Legacy Finds New Life', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIQUES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romanov Family', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T22:28:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eve M. Kahn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/506499bb-2bb5-58e1-a8d5-ae336bb5821e', 'word_count': 1141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/506499bb-2bb5-58e1-a8d5-ae336bb5821e'}, {'abstract': 'When The Atlantic published an article by Anne-Marie Slaughter with the title “Why Women Still Can’t Have It All,” the online response broke records for the magazine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'When The Atlantic published an article by Anne-Marie Slaughter with the title “Why Women Still Can’t Have It All,” the online response broke records for the magazine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the summer of 2012, when The Atlantic published an article by Anne-Marie Slaughter with the title “Why Women Still Can’t Have It All,” the online response broke records for the magazine. Slaughter had written about trying to juggle a senior position at the State Department while raising two sons; her frank admission of “how unexpectedly hard it was to do the kind of job I wanted to do as a high government official and be the kind of parent I wanted to be” made her both a figure and a flash point in a long-running conversation about working mothers.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Upfront/0310-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 951, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Upfront/0310-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 951}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Upfront/0310-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 646, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Upfront/0310-Upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1899, 'width': 1198, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Upfront/0310-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Upfront/0310-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Upfront/0310-Upfront-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Slaughter, Anne-Marie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T22:30:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f97523d-7479-5039-af63-140ebcc506d1', 'word_count': 307, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f97523d-7479-5039-af63-140ebcc506d1'}, {'abstract': 'In “Beyond the Hills,” by the Romanian director Cristian Mungiu, a girl who visits her friend at an isolated rural convent is suspected of being possessed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/movies/beyond-the-hills-directed-by-cristian-mungiu.html', 'snippet': 'In “Beyond the Hills,” by the Romanian director Cristian Mungiu, a girl who visits her friend at an isolated rural convent is suspected of being possessed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cristian Mungiu’s “Beyond the Hills” is a tough and engrossing work of realism, set in rural Romania in the dead of winter, but it also has some of the shadowy magic of an ancient folk tale. At its heart are two orphan girls — one dark, one fair — whose destinies are entwined even as their paths in life have diverged. One of them has fallen under a malign and powerful spell, or maybe both of them have. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Power of Faith, for Good and Ill', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Power of Faith, For Good and Ill', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mungiu, Cristian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Romania', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T22:32:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4816ca4a-3abf-5e06-87dc-36a98f954778', 'word_count': 782, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4816ca4a-3abf-5e06-87dc-36a98f954778'}, {'abstract': 'As theme parties go, it was a spirited hoedown, guests miming the moves and the scintillant garb of their favorite performers, Prince and Michael Jackson.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/07/michaeljackson-vs-prince-party-the-spark-is-still-there/', 'snippet': 'As theme parties go, it was a spirited hoedown, guests miming the moves and the scintillant garb of their favorite performers, Prince and Michael Jackson.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As theme parties go, it was a spirited hoedown, guests miming the moves and the scintillant garb of their favorite performers, Prince, that living avatar of ’80s glam, and the pop legend Michael Jackson. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Spark Is Still There', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Highline Ballroom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-07T22:32:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ruth La Ferla', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ruth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Ferla', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db661cd6-6dae-596f-a0b7-e67bfb191712', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db661cd6-6dae-596f-a0b7-e67bfb191712'}, {'abstract': 'The British photographer Bill Brandt, whose work is on view at MoMA, took pictures whose balance of art and humanity is frequently called strange, mysterious and irresistible.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/arts/design/bill-brandt-shadow-and-light-at-moma.html', 'snippet': 'The British photographer Bill Brandt, whose work is on view at MoMA, took pictures whose balance of art and humanity is frequently called strange, mysterious and irresistible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The pre-eminent British photographer of the 20th century, Bill Brandt, took pictures whose balance of art and humanity is frequently called strange, mysterious and irresistible. The best induce us to pore over them, exploring their psychology as much as their form, their implied narratives as much as their brooding blacks or parsimonious whites, their connections to the history of art as much as their documentary realism. Brandt himself wrote in 1948 that he admired photography’s power to make people see the world anew, to experience it with “a sense of wonder.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/07/arts/brandt_hp/brandt_hp-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 375, 'width': 600, 'subType':