weather future financial shocks, according to the results of the so-called stress tests that the Federal Reserve released on Thursday. But the big question raised by some analysts was whether the Fed was too lenient in the tests, which now pave the way for the healthiest institutions to return money to shareholders, DealBook’s Peter Eavis and Ben Protess write.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Easy ‘A’ on Bank Stress Tests?', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T12:44:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78468726-46e6-5bb7-a6f3-a5d7a06c06bf', 'word_count': 2059, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78468726-46e6-5bb7-a6f3-a5d7a06c06bf'}, {'abstract': 'Subaru recalls cars that could start themselves, keeping knee arthritis in check, a test run of Facebook’s new News Feed and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/friday-reading-subaru-recalls-cars-that-could-start-themselves/', 'snippet': 'Subaru recalls cars that could start themselves, keeping knee arthritis in check, a test run of Facebook’s new News Feed and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friday Reading: Subaru Recalls Cars That Could Start Themselves', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T12:53:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a91a0b6-bbcc-5690-98d6-15fd45fe399f', 'word_count': 100, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a91a0b6-bbcc-5690-98d6-15fd45fe399f'}, {'abstract': 'Sebastian Brajkovic deconstructs the historical elements of his tables, chairs and lamps while giving them a surreal, extruded quality that is only possible with high-tech machinery.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/seats-of-power/', 'snippet': 'Sebastian Brajkovic deconstructs the historical elements of his tables, chairs and lamps while giving them a surreal, extruded quality that is only possible with high-tech machinery.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Dutch designer Sebastian Brajkovic deconstructs the historical elements of his tables, chairs and lamps while giving them a surreal, extruded quality that is only possible with high-tech machinery. He was barely out of school when pieces from his Lathe furniture series began to appear at design fairs and in the collections of major museums. The furniture, inspired by Brajkovic’s childhood interest in things that turned, like reel-to-reel tape decks, is the subject of an exhibition that opens on March 18 on the European campus of the Savannah College of Art and Design in Lacoste, France. Several of his pieces are available for purchase through the Carpenters Workshop Gallery in London and Paris. ', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seats of Power', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Seats of Power', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interior Design and Furnishings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Savannah College of Art and Design', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T13:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pilar Viladas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pilar', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Viladas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50d53081-9b8d-5c19-a91b-5fbf09711e84', 'word_count': 114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50d53081-9b8d-5c19-a91b-5fbf09711e84'}, {'abstract': 'Feminism in India is grappling with new problems as vast economic and social changes sweep the country, while old mindsets steeped in patriarchy still prevail', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/where-is-indias-feminist-movement-headed/', 'snippet': 'Feminism in India is grappling with new problems as vast economic and social changes sweep the country, while old mindsets steeped in patriarchy still prevail', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the night of June 28, 2012, the sarpanch, or the elected head of the village council, of Singar village in rural Haryana, his nephew and two other men allegedly abducted a 15-year-old girl. According to the official complaint, The Hindu reported, the four men beat her up and then took turns repeatedly raping her.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where is India’s Feminist Movement Headed?', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's Rights", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court of India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Haryana (India)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'na