trying to make it possible for Web consumers to swap content on a grand scale, Time Warner’s spinoff of Time Inc. is the latest evidence of a strategy of divestment, and Facebook’s redesign strategy is already popular with advertisers.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/the-breakfast-meeting-new-ways-to-swap-e-content-and-time-warners-spinoff-trend/', 'snippet': 'Apple and Amazon are trying to make it possible for Web consumers to swap content on a grand scale, Time Warner’s spinoff of Time Inc. is the latest evidence of a strategy of divestment, and Facebook’s redesign strategy is already popular with adv...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apple and Amazon, the two largest purveyors of digital media, are working to create a kind of online swap meet where consumers can exchange music, movies or e-books, David Streitfeld writes. Buyers basically rent iTunes songs or Kindle books under the current system, with no way to transfer the content. The exchanges would allow users to trade content without reproducing it — when a song is traded, it disappears from its original owner’s library. Publishers and music companies are against this kind of trading, as they believe it could devalue their products, while libraries, which have not been able to dispense e-books very openly, are generally in favor.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: New Ways to Swap E-Content and Time Warner’s Spinoff Trend', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Books and Readers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerberg, Mark E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Time Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Time Warner Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Unilever NV', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T13:42:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel E. Slotnik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Slotnik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4377ddcc-d22e-594d-bec4-ddfbda7bf735', 'word_count': 513, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4377ddcc-d22e-594d-bec4-ddfbda7bf735'}, {'abstract': 'Wunderman, one of the largest agencies inside the WPP agency holding company empire, is being overhauled to better respond to the significant shifts in how marketers are seeking to reach consumers.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/wunderman-big-wpp-agency-gets-a-major-makeover/', 'snippet': 'Wunderman, one of the largest agencies inside the WPP agency holding company empire, is being overhauled to better respond to the significant shifts in how marketers are seeking to reach consumers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wunderman, one of the largest agencies inside the WPP agency holding company empire, is being overhauled to better respond, executives say, to the significant shifts in how marketers are seeking to reach consumers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wunderman, Big WPP Agency, Gets a Major Makeover', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chidester, Becky', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morel, Daniel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pearson, Stewart', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wunderman, Lester', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'WPP', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Y&R', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T14:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6e3ebd3-848a-55a9-9a98-4e20aedef6dc', 'word_count': 486, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6e3ebd3-848a-55a9-9a98-4e20aedef6dc'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/the-gun-report-march-8-2013/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Yesterday, the Senate Judiciary Committee passed a bill that would make it a felony for someone to illegally buy a gun on someone else’s behalf.\xa0Stunningly—at least to me—only one Republican senator supported the bill, Sen. Charles Grassley of Iowa.\xa0Here’s what I don’t understand: how can this bill be even remotely controversial?\xa0“Straw purchasing,” as it is called, is usually needed by people whose history—a criminal record, perhaps?—would make it impossible for them to pass a background check.\xa0Are Republicans really taking the position that convicted felons should be able to buy guns through intermediaries?\xa0What am I missing here?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: March 8, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Daytona