 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jeremiah’s Vanishing New York: Big meatballs, you say? At least one change-resistant New Yorker has a beef with Carbone, the new restaurant from the Torrisi Italian Specialties team. — Jeff Gordinier ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T14:53:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ccf1030b-52a5-5676-94c4-88962e5e031c', 'word_count': 397, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ccf1030b-52a5-5676-94c4-88962e5e031c'}, {'abstract': 'Times critics on “Oz the Great and Powerful,” “Beyond the Hills” and “The We and the I.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/this-weeks-movies-march-8/', 'snippet': 'Times critics on “Oz the Great and Powerful,” “Beyond the Hills” and “The We and the I.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this week’s video, Times critics share their thoughts on on “Oz the Great and Powerful,” “Beyond the Hills” and “The We and the I.” Read all of this week’s reviews here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week’s Movies: March 8', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T14:59:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b92476d0-d8ca-592a-a6c7-076dc7ee1666', 'word_count': 32, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b92476d0-d8ca-592a-a6c7-076dc7ee1666'}, {'abstract': 'Alice Waters, founder and owner of the famed restaurant in Berkeley, Calif., says no one was injured and the cause is unknown.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/fire-breaks-out-at-chez-panisse/', 'snippet': 'Alice Waters, founder and owner of the famed restaurant in Berkeley, Calif., says no one was injured and the cause is unknown.', 'lead_paragraph': '3:12 p.m. | Updated  ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fire Damages Chez Panisse', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fires and Firefighters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waters, Alice', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chez Panisse', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berkeley (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T15:01:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Weinstein and Carol Pogash', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weinstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogash', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e8ace1a-4aab-57fd-9091-e1f857078864', 'word_count': 587, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e8ace1a-4aab-57fd-9091-e1f857078864'}, {'abstract': 'The so-called revolving door between private sector and government posts has several positive implications, and it means that law enforcement officials have some first-hand knowledge of how the industry they regulate works, the author writes.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/at-the-s-e-c-a-revolving-door-that-helps-the-public/', 'snippet': 'The so-called revolving door between private sector and government posts has several positive implications, and it means that law enforcement officials have some first-hand knowledge of how the industry they regulate works, the author writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Zaring is assistant professor of legal studies at the Wharton School of Business at the University of Pennsylvania.Both Mary Jo White, President Obama’s nominee for chairwoman of the Securities and Exchange Commission, and her likely enforcement deputy, Andrew J. Ceresney, had lucrative careers as white-collar criminal defense lawyers after leaving their jobs as federal prosecutors in Manhattan. Now that it appears that they are likely to rejoin the government, people have wondered, here and elsewhere, whether we should worry about picking law enforcement officials from the ranks of those whose résumés include stints prosecuting and defending the same sorts of people.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At the S.E.C., a Revolving Door That Helps the Public', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T15:08:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Zaring', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zaring', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03d5a653-5d55-5685-a86a-fe96b61ebc22', 'word_count': 681, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03d5a653-5d55-5685-a86a-fe96b61ebc22'}, {'abstract': 'Some sectarian attacks in Pakistan get media attention, others don’t, and the difference means getting a political reaction, or not.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/the-pakistani-medias-uneven-treatment-of-sectarian-attacks/', 'snippet': 'Some sectarian attacks in Pakistan get media attention, others don’t, and the difference means getting a political reaction, or not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — On Sunday a bombing in Abbas Town, a predominantly Shiite neighborhood of Karachi, killed 48 people and injured almost 200. Although it was just the latest incident in a sinister trend of sectarian violence in Pakistan, the attack prompted a swifter political response.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Unequal Time Rule', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Karachi (Pakistan)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T15:19:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Huma Yusuf', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Huma', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yusuf', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a3f7acd-a634-5ccf-852e-e772c6859b75', 'word_count': 606, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a3f7acd-a634-5ccf-852e-e772c6859b75'}, {'abstract': 'Solar cells under development by a Silicon Valley start-up, Alta Devices, could give smartphone batteries a boost of as much as 80 percent of a battery’s power. The company uses hybrid gallium arsenide solar cells, which are better at capturing energy than silicon solar cells.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/will-gallium-arsenide-solar-cells-boost-smartphone-batteries/', 'snippet': 'Solar cells under development by a Silicon Valley start-up, Alta Devices, could give smartphone batteries a boost of as much as 80 percent of a battery’s power. The company uses hybrid gallium arsenide solar cells, which are better at capturing en...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Why don’t power-thirsty smartphones incorporate solar cells, to reduce the reliance on batteries? Because in general, the kind of solar cell that can be fabricated in a lightweight, flexible and durable form does not capture enough energy per square inch to make it worthwhile.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/08/technology/08bits-cells/08bits-cells-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 320, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/08/technology/08bits-cells/08bits-cells-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 320}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/08/technology/08bits-cells/08bits-cells-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 414, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/08/technology/08bits-cells/08bits-cells-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 414, 'width': 776, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/08/technology/08bits-cells/08bits-cells-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/08/technology/08bits-cells/08bits-cells-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/08/technology/08bits-cells/08bits-cells-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width':