{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T15:52:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alex Mar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alex', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7a3950b-ade6-5202-baca-16746c5f131a', 'word_count': 1275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7a3950b-ade6-5202-baca-16746c5f131a'}, {'abstract': 'Fifty years ago, the Book Review was shuttered during a four-month newspaper strike — but the best-seller list rolled on.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Fifty years ago, the Book Review was shuttered during a four-month newspaper strike — but the best-seller list rolled on.', 'lead_paragraph': 'STRIKE I: Fifty years ago this week, there was no New York Times Book Review — there was, indeed, no proper New York Times at all, as the 1963 newspaper strike was limping to the end of its four-month run. When the Book Review returned on April 7 of that year, its front cover featured a cartoon of a despondent bearded man slumped in an armchair, his concerned wife soothing him: “Cheer up, Harry. You’re not the only author who came out during the strike.” And he wasn’t: the first quarter of 1963 saw the arrival of big books from Günter Grass (“The Tin Drum”), Betty Friedan (“The Feminine Mystique”), Anthony Burgess (“A Clockwork Orange”) and Alexander Solzhenitsyn (“One Day in the Life of Ivan Denisovich”), among many others. The Book Review did what it could to catch up — all four of those books received respectful reviews in the April 7 issue, and both “The Tin Drum” and “The Feminine Mystique” went on to become hardcover best sellers. But Burgess and Solzhenitsyn had to defer their gratification. “A Clockwork Orange” finally turned up on the paperback fiction list in 1972, after Stanley Kubrick had turned it into a hit movie. And Solzhenitsyn (like Grass) eventually won the Nobel Prize in Literature.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/10list-img/10list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 381, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/10list-img/10list-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 381, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/10list-img/10list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/10list-img/10list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/10list-img/10list-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T15:53:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6eb5cfd6-6dfb-5f88-9d51-79bbb68b99de', 'word_count': 596, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6eb5cfd6-6dfb-5f88-9d51-79bbb68b99de'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'STICKS AND STONES: Defeating the Culture of Bullying and Rediscovering the Power of Character and Empathy,  by Emily Bazelon. (Random House, $27.) This intelligent study presents profiles of affected children and synthesizes current scholarship.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T15:53:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf0294fb-f4c1-5bd1-9e81-4b17386e54ae', 'word_count': 275, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf0294fb-f4c1-5bd1-9e81-4b17386e54ae'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE MAN WITHIN MY HEAD,  by Pico Iyer. (Vintage, $15.) This is a deeply felt meditation on the dual influences Iyer’s father and Graham Greene exerted upon him. Iyer, a novelist, essayist and travel writer, follows Greene’s trail from his first novel, “The Man Within,” to classics like “The Quiet American,” exploring ideas of love, faith and divided identity.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T15:53:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2db8631-8e8f-5f25-b37e-7294328dc6b5', 'word_count': 514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2db8631-8e8f-5f25-b37e-7294328dc6b5'}, {'abstract': 'If baseball fans can agree that Mariano Rivera is the best closer in baseball history and therefore the best reliever in baseball history, can one make the case that he is the best pitcher in history?', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/08/in-praise-of-rivera-whats-the-limit/', 'snippet': 'If baseball fans can agree that Mariano Rivera is the best closer in baseball history and therefore the best reliever in baseball history, can one make the case that he is the best pitcher in history?', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Tyler Kepner writes of Yankees closer Mariano Rivera, who is expected to announce his retirement on Saturday, it is the postseason that has distinguished him from every other reliever, before or since: “His regular-season earned run average is 2.21. His postseason E.R.A. — in 96 games against the best competition, under the most pressure — is 0.70.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Praise of Rivera, What’s the Limit?', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rivera, Mariano', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Torre, Joe', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T15:54:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e926c6f3-3b40-5ca3-8b1f-be2774bdc87e', 'word_count': 191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e926c6f3-3b40-5ca3-8b1f-be2774bdc87e'}, {'abstract': 'In two middle-grade novels, boys pursue a passion for show business.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/books/review/better-nate-than-ever-and-i-represent-sean-rosen.html', 'snippet': 'In two middle-grade novels, boys pursue a passion for show business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At first glance the protagonists of these two middle-grade novels, “Better Nate Than Ever” and “I Represent Sean Rosen,” have a lot in common: they’re both 13-year-old boys; they both have blue-collar parents and white-collar aspirations; they both have intelligent and original voices; and they’re both in the process of doggedly pursing their life’s passion to be in show business. And yet. . . .', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310BALABAN/0310BALABAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 967, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310BALABAN/0310BALABAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 967}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310BALABAN/0310BALABAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 636, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310BALABAN/0310BALABAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1271, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310BALABAN/0310BALABAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310BALABAN/0310BALABAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310BALABAN/0310BALABAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rising Stars', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rising Stars', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Federle, Tim (1980- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Baron, Jeff', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T15:56:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bob Balaban', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Balaban', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3d184b3-654b-5177-8b4f-e430eed14039', 'word_count': 714, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3d184b3-654b-5177-8b4f-e430eed14039'}, {'abstract': 'This novel’s title characters are an unlikely couple.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/books/review/eleanor-park-by-rainbow-rowell.html', 'snippet': 'This novel’s title characters are an unlikely couple.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I began reading contemporary fiction in high school, I remember feeling that each book was an absolute revelation. Whether I was reading Michael Crichton or Amy Tan or Tom Robbins, there had never been anything like it before in my life. The novel’s novelty passes, of course. I’m 35 now. I’ve read a dozen “we brought back the dinosaurs and they are mad” books. I’ve seen the conventions, and I’ve seen them interrogated.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Against the World', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two Against the World', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Green, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rowell, Rainbow', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T15:56:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Green', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Green', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/aaa6c969-4efb-54fb-988f-ed0562c7331d', 'word_count': 733, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aaa6c969-4efb-54fb-988f-ed0562c7331d'}, {'abstract': 'A man vanishes in Chicago, and his family struggles to survive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/books/review/hold-fast-by-blue-balliett.html', 'snippet': 'A man vanishes in Chicago, and his family struggles to survive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Langston Hughes’s poem “Dreams” inspired the title of Blue Balliett’s “Hold Fast,” which opens with clear signals of the distress to come — and of the effort people require to stay hopeful through hardship. Before the novel even begins, Balliett offers dictionary definitions of the words “home,” “lost” and “time,” as well as a brief, sobering statement about the thousands of homeless children in Chicago. She also describes a 2003 diamond heist, at the time the biggest in history, in which the thieves were caught but the gems never found. All these elements play a part in the story that lies ahead.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Side Story', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Side Story', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Balliett, Blue', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T15:56:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abby McGanney Nolan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abby', 'middlename': 'Mcganney', 'lastname': 'Nolan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4b2565c-5069-595f-a1b7-d26ea41d2a71', 'word_count': 704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4b2565c-5069-595f-a1b7-d26ea41d2a71'}, {'abstract': 'A search for hidden organisms finds some that thrive in extreme conditions, and some that may live only in the imagination.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/books/review/weird-life-by-david-toomey.html', 'snippet': 'A search for hidden organisms finds some that thrive in extreme conditions, and some that may live only in the imagination.', 'lead_paragraph': "It's life, Jim, but not as we know it. The familiar phrase, apocryphally attributed to “Star Trek,” encapsulates David Toomey’s study of the search for species flourishing in places where life was thought to be insupportable. “Weird Life” is a breakneck tour through natural history, encounters of an impossible kind, researchers as weird as the organisms they pursue that leads the reader to wonder where science ends and fantasy begins.", 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Fortey/0310-Fortey-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 738, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Fortey/0310-Fortey-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 738}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Fortey/0310-Fortey-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 833, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Fortey/0310-Fortey-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1665, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Fortey/0310-Fortey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Fortey/0310-Fortey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Fortey/0310-Fortey-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'That’s Life?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'That’s Life?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Toomey, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Extraterrestrial Life', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T15:57:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Fortey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fortey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8d99108-9d0c-515a-92f2-f43562463ac4', 'word_count': 1124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8d99108-9d0c-515a-92f2-f43562463ac4'}, {'abstract': 'Three picture books explore the theme of individuality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/books/review/snippet-the-early-riser-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Three picture books explore the theme of individuality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How odd, I sometimes think, that so many picture books aimed at our youngest readers emphasize the same theme. “You are special,” they inform us in voices loud and soft. “Celebrate your individuality, your uniqueness, your youness!” The message is a fine one, I suppose, but I can’t help wondering if the constant refrain contains a wee bit of adult projection. After all, the problem among young children is rarely that of mindless conformity. As all parents know, toddlers and early readers do not conform nearly enough, certainly not at mealtime. Or bath time. Or, really, any time.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Black01/0310-Black01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 498, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Black01/0310-Black01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 498}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Black01/0310-Black01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 850, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Black01/0310-Black01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 891, 'width': 1073, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Black01/0310-Black01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Black01/0310-Black01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310-Black01/0310-Black01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'You’re Special', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'You’re Special', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 