9c5d177c', 'word_count': 231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5279e8e4-70b8-52d9-bf28-5ab99c5d177c'}, {'abstract': 'On a trans-Atlantic cruise, a former psychic examines the tricky deceptions of magic, relationships and language itself.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/books/review/the-blue-book-by-a-l-kennedy.html', 'snippet': 'On a trans-Atlantic cruise, a former psychic examines the tricky deceptions of magic, relationships and language itself.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We don’t, in America, have any writers quite like the Scottish novelist A. L. Kennedy. Her nearest local relative, perhaps, is Richard Ford in his Bascombe novels, with that funny, dark, vituperative voice that serves equally well for tragic and comic moments. Ford, though, possesses an underlying conviction about continuity — of character, of place, of history — while in Kennedy’s world everything is up for grabs, all the time. One might view her work as surreal, if that didn’t imply a divorce from our own felt reality; one might call her sensibility ironic, except she’s profoundly attached to what she describes. Kennedy’s novels repeatedly take on dark subjects (alcoholism, familial despair, sexual betrayal, loneliness), but they do so in a chatty, engaging manner that can sometimes make you laugh out loud. I wasn’t surprised to learn that she has a sideline as a stand-up comic — though, again, this isn’t something a serious American novelist would do. And A. L. Kennedy is as serious as they get.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310LESSER/0310LESSER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310LESSER/0310LESSER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 676}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'cap