main': 'Image of the Day: March 8', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T16:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6b94702-58ad-595a-81fd-bd406f96d402', 'word_count': 1, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6b94702-58ad-595a-81fd-bd406f96d402'}, {'abstract': 'The collected letters — many previously unpublished — of the great comic writer P. G. Wodehouse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/books/review/p-g-wodehouse-a-life-in-letters.html', 'snippet': 'The collected letters — many previously unpublished — of the great comic writer P. G. Wodehouse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By most measures, P. G. Wodehouse, arguably the greatest writer of comic prose ever, hardly had a life. That was what enabled him to be so productive. In the course of his very long career, he published close to 100 books, worked on more than 30 Broadway shows, put in a couple of highly paid stints as a Hollywood screenwriter and corresponded with practically anyone who sent him mail. Writing and doting over his beloved Pekingese lapdogs was pretty much all he did.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310MCGRATH/0310MCGRATH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 333, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310MCGRATH/0310MCGRATH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 333}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310MCGRATH/0310MCGRATH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 553, 'width': 996, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310MCGRATH/0310MCGRATH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 553, 'width': 996, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310MCGRATH/0310MCGRATH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310MCGRATH/0310MCGRATH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Well, Cheerio, Old Bean', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Well, Cheerio, Old Bean', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGrath, Charles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wodehouse, P G', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ratcliffe, Sophie', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Letters', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T16:00:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0dd02978-4bd8-5054-85b8-dee920a726e1', 'word_count': 1292, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0dd02978-4bd8-5054-85b8-dee920a726e1'}, {'abstract': 'In Ann Leary’s novel, an alcoholic real estate agent pretends to be sober, with disastrous consequences.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/books/review/the-good-house-by-ann-leary.html', 'snippet': 'In Ann Leary’s novel, an alcoholic real estate agent pretends to be sober, with disastrous consequences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Wendover, Mass., a “regular” is a coffee with cream and two sugars. “Wicked” generally means “very.” And the word “townie” is not an insult, but a badge of honor. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310SULLIVAN/0310SULLIVAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 549, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310SULLIVAN/0310SULLIVAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 549}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310SULLIVAN/0310SULLIVAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 937, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310SULLIVAN/0310SULLIVAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1874, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310SULLIVAN/0310SULLIVAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310SULLIVAN/0310SULLIVAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books/review/0310SULLIVAN/0310SULLIVAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shaky Foundations', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shaky Foundations', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sullivan, J Courtney', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leary, Ann', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-08T16:01:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By J. Courtney Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'J.', 'middlename': 'Courtney', 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/17284358-efbe-5f31-8b25-0bab1d3c079e', 'word_count': 603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17284358-efbe-5f31-8b25-0bab1d3c079e'}, {'abstract': 'In Herman Koch’s novel, two Dutch couples struggle with the hardest decision of their lives — over the course of one meal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/books/review/the-dinner-by-herman-koch.html', 'snippet': 'In Herman Koch’s novel, two Dutch couples struggle with the hardest decision of their lives — over the course of one meal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'North American readers care inordinately that fictional characters be likable. This preference is strange, given that few real people are thoroughly nice and that those few aren’t interesting. Surely what actually matters is that characters clear this vital hurdle: that they be interesting. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/books