two biggest museums, ending a long-simmering feud, combining two arts collections, and rescuing one of them, the Museum of Contemporary Art, from a spasm of personnel turmoil and fiscal decline. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/09/us/LACMA-1/LACMA-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/09/us/LACMA-1/LACMA-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 413}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/09/us/LACMA-1/LACMA-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 705, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/09/us/LACMA-1/LACMA-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1410, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/09/us/LACMA-1/LACMA-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/09/us/LACMA-1/LACMA-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/09/us/LACMA-1/LACMA-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Cautious Approval for a Plan to Merge a City’s Museums', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cautious Approval For a Plan to Merge A City’s Museums', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles County Museum of Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles Museum of Contemporary Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Broad, Eli', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Govan, Michael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-09T02:18:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Nagourney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nagourney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/240d4239-fd7b-5fb0-8513-8109da423ffa', 'word_count': 1045, 'uri': 'nyt://article/240d4239-fd7b-5fb0-8513-8109da423ffa'}, {'abstract': 'One way to appreciate the conceptual oddness of daylight saving time is to imagine it as a new idea.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/09/opinion/daylight-saving-re-envisioned.html', 'snippet': 'One way to appreciate the conceptual oddness of daylight saving time is to imagine it as a new idea.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One way to appreciate the conceptual oddness of daylight saving time is to imagine it as a new idea just cooked up by the Obama administration. An individual mandate to change your clocks twice a year, every year, to enable a government redistribution of sunshine. Or, put another way, the feds’ confiscation of more than 300 million privately held American sleep hours, taken for eight months without interest payments or a penalty. All to make the nation healthier, wealthier and wiser, and for somewhat dubious energy savings. House Republicans would not be pleased — would you? Would you reset the clock radio, or would you demand that the gover