ews_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brett Bull', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brett', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bull', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec0a36b2-f437-59aa-b108-c86702ebe0c3', 'word_count': 866, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec0a36b2-f437-59aa-b108-c86702ebe0c3'}, {'abstract': 'He’s coaxing the Republican Party to move past the Bush-era foreign policy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/opinion/sunday/douthat-what-hath-rand-paul-wrought.html', 'snippet': 'He’s coaxing the Republican Party to move past the Bush-era foreign policy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE Republican Party built an advantage on foreign policy across generations, and then began demolishing it 10 years ago this month. What the cold war made, the invasion of Iraq largely unmade: beginning in 2003, a party that had long promised — and mostly delivered — peace through strength became identified with an intelligence fiasco, a botched occupation and the squandering of American resources, credibility and lives.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-articleLarge.png', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/03/opinion/ross-douthat/ross-douthat-jumbo.png', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',