age', 'url': 'images/2013/03/09/opinion/morozov/morozov-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 739, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/09/opinion/morozov/morozov-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1477, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/09/opinion/morozov/morozov-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/09/opinion/morozov/morozov-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/09/opinion/morozov/morozov-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Keep Calm and Carry On... Buying', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Guest Columnist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Keep Calm And Carry On ...Buying', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morozov, Evgeny', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMAZON.COM INC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Artificial Intelligence', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-09T19:58:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evgeny Morozov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evgeny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morozov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1cb0b8d-38b1-5b21-9a37-db9e06e6514d', 'word_count': 794, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1cb0b8d-38b1-5b21-9a37-db9e06e6514d'}, {'abstract': 'A dark night of the soul, heartache, remorse, guilt, desire, God-hunger — surely this, and not obstructed airways, is the stuff of poetry.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/09/insomnia-and-the-poet/', 'snippet': 'A dark night of the soul, heartache, remorse, guilt, desire, God-hunger — surely this, and not obstructed airways, is the stuff of poetry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Insomnia is never far from my thoughts, especially when I am writing. A poor sleeper even as a girl, I spent many nights scribbling diary entries in a notebook on the bathroom floor after our crowded household had gone to bed. As an adult, with three spirited children and a full-time job, I often sought the desk in the wee hours by necessity. Those liminal hours between dark and dawn continue to haunt my praxis even now that my nest is empty. At night the smallest noises, including the surprising veracity of my own breathing, are more apparent and seem to command an acute attention. For me, there is nothing as inimitably private and attuned as nocturnal writing. As I type away at this piece, for example, I find myself grateful that a lifelong habit of insomnia churned me awake at 5 a.m. from my usual choppy spell of semiconsciousness, affording at least a couple of deliciously quiet hours to think and work before even the dog will rouse and the winter windows lighten.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Insomnia and the Poet', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insomnia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-09T20:01:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa Russ Spaar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'Russ', 'lastname': 'Spaar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/722bd22a-bb58-52d6-8eef-2f893fd3ee0d', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/722bd22a-bb58-52d6-8eef-2f893fd3ee0d'}, {'abstract': 'After trying repeatedly to get a new microwave oven in working order — and then asking the company for a refund — a Whirlpool customer turned to the Haggler to intervene.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/your-money/a-whirlpool-microwave-and-a-customer-service-problem.html', 'snippet': 'After trying repeatedly to get a new microwave oven in working order — and then asking the company for a refund — a Whirlpool customer turned to the Haggler to intervene.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SOME companies resolve consumer complaints in ways that are both efficient and empathetic, demonstrating not just a grasp of what defines good service but also the ways that brand loyalty is nurtured. Then there is Whirlpool.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/business/10-HAGGLER/10-HAGGLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 503, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/10/business/10-HAGGLER/10-HAGGLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 503}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/10/business/10-HAGGLER/10-HAGGLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 8