osition party ended a congress over the weekend with the party’s leader, Daw Aung San Suu Kyi, calling for a “good relationship” with the powerful military. She vowed to infuse new blood into the party, which is still recovering from more than two decades of persecution under military rule.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Myanmar Opposition Leader Vows Party Reform', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Myanmar Opposition Leader Vows Party Reform', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fuller, Thomas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National League for Democracy (Myanmar)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aung San Suu Kyi', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Myanmar', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-10T13:03:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Fuller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fuller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfd42a64-ff1d-5cc5-bdd0-c7909721c832', 'word_count': 352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfd42a64-ff1d-5cc5-bdd0-c7909721c832'}, {'abstract': 'Sleep-deprived solvers head into Patrick Berry’s puzzle 7.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/a-c-p-t-2013-the-last-waltz/', 'snippet': 'Sleep-deprived solvers head into Patrick Berry’s puzzle 7.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SPECIAL POST — As if a whirlwind weekend of puzzling and late night socializing wasn’t enough, Daylight Savings Time has robbed the solvers of an hour’s sleep. There are more coffee cups on the tables of the ballroom than I remember in recent years.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A.C.P.T. 2013: The Last Waltz', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-10T13:27:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/247e63d4-36b7-5b90-bcdc-657e30693d2b', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/247e63d4-36b7-5b90-bcdc-657e30693d2b'}, {'abstract': 'When a classmate mocked a character in our reading as “uneducated,” I told the class I’d never graduated high school. Then I wished I’d kept my mouth shut.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/a-military-mom-goes-back-to-school/', 'snippet': 'When a classmate mocked a character in our reading as “uneducated,” I told the class I’d never graduated high school. Then I wished I’d kept my mouth shut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On my first day of school I cried, “Mommy, what if they’re all smarter than me?” I wasn’t a first grader. I was a 40-year-old Army wife, a mother of four and a new student. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Military Mom Goes Back to School', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-10T13:50:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sharisse T. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sharisse', 'middlename': 'T.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ef7ee77-4c96-523b-b992-c5a6d4ee14fb', 'word_count': 655, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ef7ee77-4c96-523b-b992-c5a6d4ee14fb'}, {'abstract': 'The United Nations Human Rights Council will meet Monday to consider calls for an international inquiry into possible crimes\xa0against humanity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/11/world/asia/north-korea-faces-pressure-from-un-on-human-rights.html', 'snippet': 'The United Nations Human Rights Council will meet Monday to consider calls for an international inquiry into possible crimes\xa0against humanity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GENEVA — Already angry over tougher sanctions imposed last week to punish its nuclear tests, North Korea faces renewed pressure over its human rights record as the United Nations Human Rights Council meets Monday to consider calls for an international inquiry into possible crimes against humanity.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'North Korea Faces Pressure From U.N. on Human Rights', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'North Korea Faces Pressure From U.N. on Human Rights', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'North Korea', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embargoes and Economic Sanctions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-10T13:52:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Cumming-Bruce and Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cumming-Bruce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/afa9435d-f9b3-5ebe-af11-819b6f780772', 'word_count': 931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/afa9435d-f9b3-5ebe-af11-819b6f780772'}, {'abstract': 'London’s Royal Opera House and the Ultimate Travel Company offer privileged access to some of the world’s best operas, ballets and symphonies.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/the-very-grand-tour/', 'snippet': 'London’s Royal Opera House and the Ultimate Travel Company offer privileged access to some of the world’s best operas, ballets and symphonies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Culturati who’ve had enough of art-fair globe-trotting can now move on to music and dance. London’s Royal Opera House and the Ultimate Travel Company offer privileged access to some of the world’s best operas, ballets and symphonies. Each journey begins in London with a performance and private dinner at the Royal Opera House. Then maybe it’s off to St. Petersburg for a show at the Mariinsky Ballet followed by a backstage tour led by the company’s director. Past trips have included V.I.P. treatment at the Salzburg Easter Festival in Austria, a visit to ballet schools, lectures, private concerts and a chance to meet virtuosos like the South African tenor Johan Botha. From $4,640 per person; theultimatetravelcompany.co.uk/roh. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The (Very) Grand Tour', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-10T14:00:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rocky Casale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rocky', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Casale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7176d06-d47e-50f6-9575-b606f75d7f6f', 'word_count': 117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7176d06-d47e-50f6-9575-b606f75d7f6f'}, {'abstract': 'Still shambling.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/10/attack-of-the-trust-fund-zombie/', 'snippet': 'Still shambling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So, I’ve Stephanopoulized, along with Senator Ron Johnson, who began by declaring that we need to agree about the facts — then promptly rolled out the old trust fund zombie. I’ve explained this one over and over again, for example here. And I have to say, it’s extremely telling that conservative Republicans don’t seem able to make their case without resorting, right from the beginning, to obviously dumb fallacies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Attack of the Trust Fund Zombie', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-10T14:42:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0fc9ec4-1d05-50e7-a0a4-4de9055987f5', 'word_count': 69, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0fc9ec4-1d05-50e7-a0a4-4de9055987f5'}, {'abstract': 'Police say two officers shot and killed the teenage boy in Brooklyn after he pointed a handgun at them.', 'web_url': 'https:/