nk': 2}, {'firstname': 'Pedro', 'middlename': 'Rafael', 'lastname': 'Rosado', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/ecbf7594-d3e8-5b5c-ae40-24ed92bea5b1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/ecbf7594-d3e8-5b5c-ae40-24ed92bea5b1'}, {'abstract': 'The tennis player talks to T about her collaboration with the first lady, Michelle Obama, and the Let’s Move! Active Schools program.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/11/talking-talent-serena-williams/', 'snippet': 'The tennis player talks to T about her collaboration with the first lady, Michelle Obama, and the Let’s Move! Active Schools program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At 31 years old, Serena Williams is ranked the No. 1 female tennis player in the world, for the sixth time. Williams is arguably one of the most exciting athletes to ever grace tennis — with her lightning speed serve, her festive fashion and her fierce determination, Williams defines power. Although her life is virtually a hamster wheel of 24/7 tennis, Williams joined the Nike president and C.E.O., Mark Parker, and the first lady, Michelle Obama, on Feb. 28 in Chicago to announce Nike’s investment of $50 million in the United States to increase the physical activity of children in schools and communities, a portion of which will benefit the Let’s Move! Active Schools program. En route to the airport following the event, Williams discussed the first lady, the Miami Dolphins and her Chris Evert fantasy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Talking Talent | Serena Williams', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-11T14:53:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lauren Tabach-Bank', 'person': [{'firstname': 