6:57:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Totilo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Totilo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed4c542d-95fa-59c0-aeed-891f71b06bcb', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed4c542d-95fa-59c0-aeed-891f71b06bcb'}, {'abstract': 'This week, Rich Ricci, the head of Barclays investment banking unit, is entering several horses in the Cheltenham Festival, including “Fat Cat in the Hat.”', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/03/11/a-barclays-banker-bets-on-a-fat-cat/', 'snippet': 'This week, Rich Ricci, the head of Barclays investment banking unit, is entering several horses in the Cheltenham Festival, including “Fat Cat in the Hat.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama irked Wall Street bankers a few years ago by calling them “fat cats.” But one banker has embraced the name.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Barclays Banker Bets on a ‘Fat Cat’', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executive Compensation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horse Racing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barclays PLC', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-11T16:57:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04fcb63c-e824-53de-a698-1bde814ca931', 'word_count': 314, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04fcb63c-e824-53de-a698-1bde814ca931'}, {'abstract': 'How Theodora Allen, an MFA candidate in California, played a part in Saint Laurent’s Paris show.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/11/painterly-fashion/', 'snippet': 'How Theodora Allen, an MFA candidate in California, played a part in Saint Laurent’s Paris show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For his second women’s collection at Saint Laurent in Paris last week, Hedi Slimane sent out invitations in the form of an artist book called “49 Paintings” by Theodora Allen. Fashion houses teaming up with contemporary artists is nothing particularly new — there were actual Yayoi Kusama mannequins in Louis Vuitton’s windows when those two paired off — but to tap someone still in graduate school feels the riskier proposition. Allen is currently in her second year at U.C.L.A.’s M.F.A. program and has no gallery representation. “I’m looking at various 19th- and 20th-century bohemian countercultures — from the Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood, to the early California Arts and Crafts movement, to singer-songwriters living in Laurel Canyon in the late 1960s,” she said of her inspiration.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Painterly Fashion', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Slimane, Hedi', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yves Saint Laurent', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-11T16:58:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Powers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Powers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fba5315b-c2b8-505b-ab01-4a03b2f3ae9d', 'word_count': 174, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fba5315b-c2b8-505b-ab01-4a03b2f3ae9d'}, {'abstract': 'Rupert Everett stars as Oscar Wilde in “The Judas Kiss” and Rowan Atkinson plays a teacher of English in “Quartermaine’s Terms.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/11/london-theater-journal-well-seated-performers/', 'snippet': 'Rupert Everett stars as Oscar Wilde in “The Judas Kiss” and Rowan Atkinson plays a teacher of English in “Quartermaine’s Terms.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Rupert Everett and Rowan Atkinson are very good at sitting. This is more challenging and less boring than it sounds. I, for example, sat for nearly five hours watching these actors sitting in different West End revivals one day recently. But I seriously doubt that anyone focusing on me, or on any of my fellow audience members, during those hours would have been held in thrall.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/11/arts/11artsbeat-rowan1/11artsbeat-rowan1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 361, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/11/arts/11artsbeat-rowan1/11artsbeat-rowan1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 361}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/11/arts/11artsbeat-rowan1/11artsbeat-rowan1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 616, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/11/arts/11artsbeat-rowan1/11artsbeat-rowan1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 711, 'width': 1181, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/11/arts/11artsbeat-ro