etball Assn', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of North Carolina', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-11T18:48:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve McPherson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McPherson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34b71076-1602-5be2-9783-90a27505cb01', 'word_count': 635, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34b71076-1602-5be2-9783-90a27505cb01'}, {'abstract': 'The French and the Anglo-Saxons have found themselves caught in a clash of culinary cultures with the widening food scandal that is “horsegate.”', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/11/lumiere-they-eat-horses-dont-they-ready-for-copy/', 'snippet': 'The French and the Anglo-Saxons have found themselves caught in a clash of culinary cultures with the widening food scandal that is “horsegate.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last October, the super-starred French chef Alain Ducasse invited Dan Barber, of the Blue Hill restaurants in New York, to the Plaza Athenée to cook for some special guests. American Ambassador Charles H. Rivkin and his wife, Susan Tolson, showed up, as did dozens of Paris luminaries. The Americans were wowed by Barber’s inventiveness, including his use of charcoal made from pig bones to roast a whole pig. The French, by contrast, were horrified.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lumière | They Eat Horses, Don’t They?', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Horses', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-11T19:00:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Sciolino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sciolino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8865720-a494-506a-9cba-b5032512a1ec', 'word_count': 1061, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8865720-a494-506a-9cba-b5032512a1ec'}, {'abstract': 'Yes, money will flow to other betting markets and bookmakers who offer odds on the election. But the billions I am referring to will be wagered somewhere else: Wall Street.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/11/even-without-intrade-billions-will-be-bet-on-2016-race/', 'snippet': 'Yes, money will flow to other betting markets and bookmakers who offer odds on the election. But the billions I am referring to will be wagered somewhere else: Wall Street.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The popular prediction market Intrade, which in November shut down its markets to United States residents after a complaint from the Commodity Futures Trading Commission, announced on Sunday that it was closing down entirely.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Even Without Intrade, Billions Will Be Bet on 2016 Race', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-11T19:04:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/261b43ba-ea2a-52