tners Andrew and Adam Mariani talk to T about winemaking with family and drinking on the job.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/11/asked-and-answered-the-terroir-two/', 'snippet': 'Young vintners Andrew and Adam Mariani talk to T about winemaking with family and drinking on the job.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Andrew Mariani, the 30-year-old founder of the artisanal winery Scribe, in Sonoma, Calif., is paving his way into wine culture via a dusty, palm-tree-lined path that winds up to a shabby chic hacienda, two farmhouses and a 200-acre vineyard estate, all of which he and his younger brother, Adam, acquired, renovated and unveiled to the public in 2007.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Asked and Answered | The Terroir Two', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Asked And Answered: The Terroir Two', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wines', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-11T22:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeanine Celeste Pang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeanine', 'middlename': 'Celeste', 'lastname': 'Pang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6979e80f-e09b-5b1f-85e4-72b93cdfec04', 'word_count': 681, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6979e80f-e09b-5b1f-85e4-72b93cdfec04'}, {'abstract': 'The three historians commissioned by the Vienna Philharmonic to carry out an independent study of its Nazi past announced their findings on Sunday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/12/arts/music/historians-issue-report-on-vienna-philharmonic.html', 'snippet': 'The three historians commissioned by the Vienna Philharmonic to carry out an independent study of its Nazi past announced their findings on Sunday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The three historians commissioned by the Vienna Philharmonic to carry out an independent study of its Nazi past announced their findings on Sunday. In preliminary materials furnished to The New York Times last month, Oliver Rathkolb, the lead historian, said he had learned, from a “highly trustworthy and well-informed witness,” the identity of a shadowy presence and promised to reveal it with the entire study. That has now been posted on the orchestra’s Web site, in German, with a promise of an English translation to follow. The person served as an emissary, official or not, of the orchestra in delivering a replacement of the orchestra’s ring of honor that had been bestowed on Baldur von Schirach in 1942. Schirach, the Nazi governor of Vienna who lost the ring after World War II, spent 20 years in prison for his part in deporting tens of thousands of Viennese Jews to extermination camps in Nazi-occupied Poland. In late 1966 or early 1967, after he was freed, the replacement ring was tendered by — it is now revealed — Helmut Wobisch, a trumpeter in the orchestra. Wobisch joined the Nazi party and the SS early on, spied on and denounced fellow musicians, was fired by the orchestra after the war, rejoined it in 1947, became its executive director in 1953 and played in it until 1968.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Historians Issue Report on Vienna Philharmonic', 'kicker': 'Arts, Briefly', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Historians Issue Report On Vienna Philharmonic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vienna Philharmonic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-11T22:03:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By James R. Oestreich', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Oestreich', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/58a65868-9b83-53f2-aa48-9dc07fc07e83', 'word_count': 223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/58a65868-9b83-53f2-aa48-9dc07fc07e83'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Taylor recombines and juxtapose dances, like the new “To Make Crops Grow” alongside the very different “Le Sacre du Printemps (The Rehearsal).”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/12/arts/dance/paul-taylor-company-at-the-koch-theater.html', 'snippet': 'Paul Taylor recombines and juxtapose dances, like the new “To Make Crops Grow” alongside the very different “Le Sacre du Printemps (The Rehearsal).”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The foyer at the David H. Koch Theater, happily stripped of recent clutter, is one of this city’s great interiors. Large, calm and elegant, with its vast window space filtered by pearl-rope curtains, it opens onto a balcony that faces over Lincoln Center’s plaza. Now, during the Paul Taylor Dance Company’s annual three-week season at the Koch, this space proves the perfect place in which to let the dances add up after the curtain falls, and to mull over the connections between one work and another. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/12/arts/TAYLOR/TAYLOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/12/arts/TAYLOR/TAYLOR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 