 'headline': {'main': 'Selection of New Pope Cloaked in Secrecy and Tradition', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-12T08:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eee81f89-5336-598b-934d-1b9616307c5f', 'word_count': 135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eee81f89-5336-598b-934d-1b9616307c5f'}, {'abstract': 'Why do so many shows with central female characters equate “headstrong” with “head case”?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/magazine/tvs-new-wave-of-women-smart-strong-borderline-insane.html', 'snippet': 'Why do so many shows with central female characters equate “headstrong” with “head case”?', 'lead_paragraph': 'At first glance, this looks like a great moment for women on television. Many smart and confident female characters have paraded onto the small screen over the past few years. But I’m bothered by one persistent caveat: that the more astute and capable many of these women are, the more likely it is that they’re also completely nuts.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/magazine/17riff/17riff-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 90, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/17/magazine/17riff/17riff-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 90}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/magazine/17riff/17riff-jumbo.jpg'