eaders: articles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/11/blogs/nyt5-article-design/nyt5-article-design-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/11/blogs/nyt5-article-design/nyt5-article-design-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/11/blogs/nyt5-article-design/nyt5-article-design-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/11/blogs/nyt5-article-design/nyt5-article-design-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1299, 'width': 1299, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/11/blogs/nyt5-article-design/nyt5-article-design-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/11/blogs/nyt5-article-design/nyt5-article-design-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/11/blogs/nyt5-article-design/nyt5-article-design-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Introducing a New Article Experience', 'kicker': 'First Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Times', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-12T15:31:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Adelman and Rob Larson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adelman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Larson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6cc43a0-647e-5d70-b95d-bece2b789681', 'word_count': 485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6cc43a0-647e-5d70-b95d-bece2b789681'}, {'abstract': 'With new hotels and restorations of landmarks on the horizon, West Berlin is experiencing a revival.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/12/the-other-berlin/', 'snippet': 'With new hotels and restorations of landmarks on the horizon, West Berlin is experiencing a revival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the last decade, as East Berlin has risen to become Europe’s most fertile breeding ground of cool, West Berlin has languished. By the mid-2000s, once-bustling squares and boulevards were shadows of their glamorous former selves. But the winds of change have shifted. Construction cranes now swing over Breitscheidplatz, and the streets buzz with shoppers and diners. “The city is reorienting itself to the west,” says Stefanie Hering, who will open a flagship store for her porcelain design company, Hering Berlin, in the new Waldorf-Astoria hotel building this spring. The Waldorf, occupying a new high-rise steps from the Zoo station, is only the beginning. Recently opened on the main boulevard Kurfürstendamm, Haus Cumberland is a restored hotel from the early 1900s that houses several welcome arrivals, among them Grosz — a coffeehouse and restaurant evoking 1920s Berlin.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Other Berlin', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hotels and Travel Lodgings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-12T15:35:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kimberly Bradley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kimberly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bradley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5fb5bfa9-cc8f-515d-8d9c-4545f5ee9636', 'word_count': 229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5fb5bfa9-cc8f-515d-8d9c-4545f5ee9636'}, {'abstract': 'Queen Latifah will perform George Gershwin’s “The Man I Love” and Mr. Stoltzman is set to perform the “Interlude” from André Previn’s “Sonata for Clarinet and Piano” and Leonard Bernstein’s “Sonata for Clarinet and Piano.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/12/queen-latifah-and-richard-stoltzman-to-perform-at-new-york-city-ballet-gala/', 'snippet': 'Queen Latifah will perform George Gershwin’s “The Man I Love” and Mr. Stoltzman is set to perform the “Interlude” from André Previn’s “Sonata for Clarinet and Piano” and Leonard Bernstein’s “Sonata for Clarinet and Piano.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Queen Latifah and Richard Stoltzman, the clarinetist, will be the guest artists at New York City Ballet’s 2013 spring gala, to be held May 8 at the David H. Koch Theater at Lincoln Center. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Queen Latifah and Richard Stoltzman to Perform at New York City Ballet Gala', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York City Ballet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'