value': "Jacob's Pillow Dance Festival", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-12T18:35:41+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/94431712-1ae4-5632-a286-8c37ac143bac', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/94431712-1ae4-5632-a286-8c37ac143bac'}, {'abstract': 'Findings from NASA’s Mars rover show that the planet may have once been warm and wet and a pleasant place where microbes could have thrived.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/13/science/space/mars-could-have-supported-life-nasa-says.html', 'snippet': 'Findings from NASA’s Mars rover show that the planet may have once been warm and wet and a pleasant place where microbes could have thrived.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Several billion years ago, Mars may well have been a pleasant place for tiny microbes to live, with plenty of water as well as minerals that could have served as food, NASA scientists said Tuesday at a news conference on the latest findings from their Mars rover. But they have yet to find signs that actual microbes did live in that oasis.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/13/science/13mars/13mars-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 266, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/13/science/13mars/13mars-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 266}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/13/science/13mars/13mars-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 454, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/13/science/1