print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Jason Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-12T19:18:53+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Theater', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/40200696-2215-555e-8f41-a3191c00d339', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/40200696-2215-555e-8f41-a3191c00d339'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Ryan, same as he ever was.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/12/flimflam-forever/', 'snippet': 'Paul Ryan, same as he ever was.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I took Paul Ryan’s measure two and a half years ago. All the Very Serious People were very angry with me — Ryan was the Serious, Honest Conservative, the guy centrists demonstrated their centrism by praising. But he was an obvious phony. His “plan” was all smoke (I couldn’t even find any mirrors), with all the alleged deficit reduction coming from closing tax loopholes he refused to specify plus projected reductions in discretionary spending that he also refused to specify. Meanwhile, he was pursuing radical redistribution away from the needy to the wealthy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Flimflam Forever', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-12T19:27:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e1267e7-678a-5662-813d-27e7c4424b56', 'word_count': 136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e1267e7-678a-5662-813d-27e7c4424b56'}, {'abstract': 'Looking behind the scenes at Wesley Allsbrook’s illustrations for our article on a physicist who was duped by a “model” he met online.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/12/illustrating-an-online-romance-gone-wrong/', 'snippet': 'Looking behind the scenes at Wesley Allsbrook’s illustrations for our article on a physicist who was duped by a “model” he met online.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the magazine’s most recent issue, Maxine Swann recounts how an online romance led to incarceration in an Argentine jail for the physicist Paul Frampton. But because her article, “The Professor, the Bikini Model and the Suitcase Full of Trouble,” includes crucial elements that could not be photographed — from Frampton’s life at the University of North Carolina to his online encounters with someone he believed to be the model Denise Milani to his trip to South America to meet her to his eventual arrest on drug charges — we commissioned Wesley Allsbrook to supply the article’s graphic elements. Allsbrook is a Brooklyn-based illustrator whose work accompanied another hard-to-photograph article in the magazine, Jeffrey Gettleman’s 2011 article about pirate kidnappings off the coast of Somalia.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/11/magazine/11-framptonspread2/11-framptonspread2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/11/magazine/11-framptonspread2/11-framptonspread2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/11/magazine/11-framptonspread2/11-framptonspread2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 677, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}},