}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Syrian Army', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-12T22:54:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Barnard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37485aa3-50d5-5ecc-8f94-316d088c4951', 'word_count': 1229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37485aa3-50d5-5ecc-8f94-316d088c4951'}, {'abstract': 'In sharp contrast to earlier generations, many people retire and then unretire, or continue working well after they were supposed to have retired.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/13/business/retirementspecial/pushing-back-retirement-and-not-always-for-money.html', 'snippet': 'In sharp contrast to earlier generations, many people retire and then unretire, or continue working well after they were supposed to have retired.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT’S not your grandfather’s retirement, not nearly as cut and dried. It’s not the simple picture of stopping work at 60 or 65, moving to Florida and devoting oneself to golf and bridge — not that it was ever totally that.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/13/business/retirementspecial/SUB-WORK/SUB-WORK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 754, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/13/business/retirementspecial/SUB-WORK/SUB-WORK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 754}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/13/business/retirementspecial/SUB-WORK/SUB-WORK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 816, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/13/business/retirementspecial/SUB-WORK/SUB-WORK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1631, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/13/business/retirementspecial/SUB-WORK/SUB-WORK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/13/business/retirementspecial/SUB-WORK/SUB-WORK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/13/business/retirementspecial/SUB-WORK/SUB-WORK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pushing Back Retirement, and Not Always for Money', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In and Out, Off and On', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Retirement', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenhouse, Steven', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Longevity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-12T22:54:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Retirement', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Greenhouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenhouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/48489386-d365-5d3f-98ce-564b2d2f8017', 'word_count': 2436, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48489386-d365-5d3f-98ce-564b2d2f8017'}, {'abstract': 'Low interest rates mean retirees must save more money to produce the same income or invest in assets with higher yields but possibly higher risk.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/13/business/retirementspecial/finding-the-right-mix-of-assets-for-retirement-savings.html', 'snippet': 'Low interest rates mean retirees must save more money to produce the same income or invest in assets with higher yields but possibly higher risk.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BUILDING an investment portfolio that produces enough income to live on is one of the main goals of retirement planning, but lately the goal posts have shifted. With interest rates stuck near record lows, retirees must have more saved up to produce the same income through the usual high-quality investments, like Treasury bonds, or else they must venture into assets with higher yields but potentially higher risk', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Choice for Tight Times: Save More or More Risk', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Choice for Tight Times: Save More or Take On More Risk', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Retirement', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-12T22:55:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Retirement', 'byline': {'original': 'By Conrad De Aenlle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Conrad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'De Aenlle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba36b064-de27-5f89-b702-7b9de0ce6282', 'word_count': 931, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba36b064-de27-5f89-b702-7b9de0ce6282'}, {'abstract': 'Some older adults are taking jobs that they never trained for as young people, like recording audiobooks, refereeing sports and bartending.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/13/business/retirementspecial/older-adults-start-new-chapters-in-their-work-lives.html', 'snippet': 'Some older adults are taking jobs that they never trained for as young people, like recording audiobooks, refereeing sports and bartending.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR most of my working life, I have never had to commute or see the inside of a cubicle. As a television critic, I was “forced” to watch the tube, and as a sportswriter, I inhabited stadiums and press boxes rather than dingy offices.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/13/business/retirementspecial/PRERETIRE/PRERETIRE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/13/business/retirementspecial/PRERETIRE/PRERETIRE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 