y.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it comes to one of the oldest corporate takeover defenses around, more and more companies are saying no.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Companies Holding Annual Board Elections, Group Reports', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boards of Directors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shareholder Rights and Activism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bebchuk, Lucian A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lipton, Martin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harvard Law School', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-13T12:23:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael J. de la Merced', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'de la Merced', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3530d95-9077-5f97-b3b0-a3c56b233f52', 'word_count': 351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3530d95-9077-5f97-b3b0-a3c56b233f52'}, {'abstract': 'When exercise stresses you out, a college student and frat brother at age 65, a tool to better predict commute times and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/13/wednesday-reading-when-exercise-stresses-you-out/', 'snippet': 'When exercise stresses you out, a college student and frat brother at age 65, a tool to better predict commute times and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wednesday Reading: When Exercise Stresses You Out', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-13T12:24:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e7c13f0-dc88-54b4-9672-7d0bc855317f', 'word_count': 97, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e7c13f0-dc88-54b4-9672-7d0bc855317f'}, {'abstract': 'They can’t let the confidence fairy go.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/13/night-of-the-living-alesina/', 'snippet': 'They can’t let the confidence fairy go.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ah, remember the good old days of expansionary austerity? On both sides of the Atlantic, austerians seized on academic work by Alberto Alesina and Silvia Ardagna claiming that fiscal consolidation, if focused on spending cuts, would if anything lead to economic expansion. It wasn’t because the paper was especially compelling — even a quick look suggested that the methodology for identifying austerity was seriously flawed. But A-A told people what they wanted to hear, and they went with it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Night of the Living Alesina', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-13T12:26:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b89e4979-145a-5f7a-a70a-35c9dcfd4225', 'word_count': 351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b89e4979-145a-5f7a-a70a-35c9dcfd4225'}, {'abstract': 'If you hear Facebook making sounds when your friends post updates, your notifications settings are set to include audio alerts.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/13/qa-quieting-a-noisy-facebook/', 'snippet': 'If you hear Facebook making sounds when your friends post updates, your notifications settings are set to include audio alerts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Quieting a Noisy Facebook', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quickly Quieting\r\nA Noisy Facebook', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-13T12:32:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc94273e-7820-5ca4-8b92-a550d081287a', 'word_count': 168, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc94273e-7820-5ca4-8b92-a550d081287a'}, {'abstract': 'The dearth of prosecutions since the financial crisis is again stirring controversy, but there is still time for political leaders to hold corporate executives responsible when a company engages in misconduct.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/03/13/after-financial-crisis-prosecutors-navigate-tricky-waters/', 'snippet': 'The dearth of prosecutions since the financial crisis is again stirring controversy, but there is still time for political leaders to hold corporate executives responsible when a company engages in misconduct.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The question of whether large financial institutions can ever be held fully responsible for criminal conduct has again become a political controversy. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Financial Crisis, Prosecutors Navigate Tricky Waters', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Libor (London Interbank Offered Rate)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Money Laundering', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Securities and Commodities Violations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, David S', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Holder, Eric H Jr', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Powell, Jerome H', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Arthur Andersen LLP', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Treasury Department', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-13T12:33:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter J. Henning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Henning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fa3f1f8-bc78-582e-bac9-30ae0b1f1e25', 'word_count': 1011, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fa3f1f8-bc78-582e-bac9-30ae0b1f1e25'}, {'abstract': 'Anna Wintour becomes Condé Nast’s artistic director, NPR reaches out to a younger audience and extensive police failings allowed Jimmy Savile to escape investigation for decades of sexual abuse.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/13/the-breakfast-meeting-anna-wintours-new-role-and-npr-appeals-to-the-tweet-set/', 'snippet': 'Anna Wintour becomes Condé Nast’s artistic director, NPR reaches out to a younger audience and extensive police failings allowed Jimmy Savile to escape investigation for decades of sexual abuse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Condé Nast plans to announce Wednesday that Anna Wintour, editor of Vogue for nearly a quarter-century, will take on the additional role of artistic director for the company, Eric Wilson reports. The move into Condé Nast’s executive ranks ends speculation that Ms. Wintour might leave the company, and it makes her one of the most powerful women in magazine publishing. Ms. Wintour’s new role will assume some of the responsibility from S.I. Newhouse Jr., who has controlled editorial management as Condé Nast’s chairman for more than three decades. Ms. Wintour described the position as “almost like being a one-person consulting firm” for other editors on directing their brands.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Anna Wintour’s New Role and NPR Appeals to the Tweet Set', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-13T12:43:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel E. Slotnik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Slotnik'