tions', 'value': 'Office of the Comptroller of the Currency', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-13T18:25:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Silver-Greenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver-Greenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b29c0cbd-2d1f-5613-9969-2e68dc587f3d', 'word_count': 636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b29c0cbd-2d1f-5613-9969-2e68dc587f3d'}, {'abstract': 'In this lesson, we’ll help students investigate the science and myths about added sugars. How unhealthy are they? What happens to sugar when ingested? And how much sugar do we actually consume?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/13/investigating-science-and-health-questions-about-sugar-and-our-bodies/', 'snippet': 'In this lesson, we’ll help students investigate the science and myths about added sugars. How unhealthy are they? What happens to sugar when ingested? And how much sugar do we actually consume?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sugar and corn syrup have become a large part of the American diet. On average, teenagers get 17 percent of their daily calories from added sugar. Meanwhile, public health officials have taken note that more and more studies suggest a link between sugar consumption and health issues like obesity and diabetes. Some go so far as to say that sugar is toxic to our bodies, and as dangerous to our health as cigarettes. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/22/us/SODA-LN/SODA-LN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'lega