hmir and Cambodia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Photos from West Bank, Syria, Kashmir and Cambodia.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of the Day: West Bank and Elsewhere', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cambodia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kashmir and Jammu (India)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-13T20:32:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/003e6699-16f3-548a-8808-c3620ee126ad', 'word_count': 13, 'uri': 'nyt://article/003e6699-16f3-548a-8808-c3620ee126ad'}, {'abstract': 'James Gandre will assume the post in May, succeeding Robert Sirota.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/13/manhattan-school-of-music-names-new-president/', 'snippet': 'James Gandre will assume the post in May, succeeding Robert Sirota.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Manhattan School of Music has appointed James Gandre as president, effective May 6. He succeeds the composer Robert Sirota, who stepped down last fall. Mr. Gandre is currently the provost and executive vice president of Roosevelt University, in Chicago, but he has worked at the Manhattan School before, as dean of enrollment and alumni from 1995 to 2000. He joined Roosevelt University in 2000, and held positions there as interim dean of the College of Education, and dean of the Chicago College of Performing Arts.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Manhattan School of Music Names New President', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Manhattan Music School Names President', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sirota, Robert', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Manhattan School of Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-13T20:32:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa1009e3-c119-5f49-97a1-0c1183dfc60b', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa1009e3-c119-5f49-97a1-0c1183dfc60b'}, {'abstract': '“The Drawer Boy” by Michael Healey looks in on the lives of two aging friends who share a farmhouse in the Canadian countryside, and a stranger who comes to live with them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/14/theater/reviews/the-drawer-boy-at-abingdon-theater-arts-complex.html', 'snippet': '“The Drawer Boy” by Michael Healey looks in on the lives of two aging friends who share a farmhouse in the Canadian countryside, and a stranger who comes to live with them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Actors must be thrilled when handed Michael Healey’s play “The Drawer Boy,” no matter which of the three characters they’ll be tackling. It’s such a beautifully written piece, humorous and heart-wrenching but never overwrought, that all you have to do is ride the vehicle the author has provided. You can concentrate on your performance, not on dredging humor out of unfunny jokes or tears out of unconvincing moments. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/DRAWER/DRAWER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 302, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/DRAWER/DRAWER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 302}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/DRAWER/DRAWER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 516, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/DRAWER/DRAWER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1031, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/DRAWER/DRAWER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/DRAWER/DRAWER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/DRAWER/DRAWER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two’s Company, Three’s a Tale', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Two’s Company, Three’s a Tale', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oberon Theater Ensemble', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dinelaris, Alexander', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-13T20:32:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/65a6cf60-8c0d-59b4-a7f0-39ff93c5f42b', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65a6cf60-8c0d-59b4-a7f0-39ff93c5f42b'}, {'abstract': 'In “Present Shock,” Douglas Rushkoff analyzes the breathless impact of today’s technologies, including their consequences for information processing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/14/books/present-shock-by-douglas-rushkoff.html', 'snippet': 'In “Present Shock,” Douglas Rushkoff analyzes the breathless impact of today’s technologies, including their consequences for information processing.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Present Shock” is one of those invaluable books that make sense of what we already half-know. Playing on the title of Alvin Toffler’s influential 1970 “Future Shock,” which sounded an alarm about what Mr. Toffler called “a personal perception of too much change in too short a period of time,” Douglas Rushkoff analyzes a very different phenomenon. The future arrived a little while ago, he posits — maybe with Y2K, maybe with Sept. 11. Now it’s here. And we are stuck with “a diminishment of everything that isn’t happening right now — and the onslaught of everything that supposedly is.” Mr. Toffler warned that we would be unready for this onslaught. Mr. Rushkoff is more analytical than alarmist. He divides his thoughts into five sections addressing five kinds of profound change, and his biggest illustration of present shock has to do with the actual book itself. Because the present is more full of interruptions than the past was, it took him extra time to write. Because its ideas aren’t glib, he says, “here I am writing opera when the people are listening to singles.” And he realizes that data-swamped readers may take longer to finish books now. Coming from him the phrase “thanks for your time” has new meaning.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/14BOOKRUSHKOFF/14BOOKRUSHKOFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 858, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/14BOOKRUSHKOFF/14BOOKRUSHKOFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 858}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/14BOOKRUSHKOFF/14BOOKRUSHKOFF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 716, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/14BOOKRUSHKOFF/14BOOKRUSHKOFF-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1425, 'width': 996, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/14BOOKRUSHKOFF/14BOOKRUSHKOFF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/14BOOKRUSHKOFF/14BOOKRUSHKOFF-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/arts/14BOOKRUSHKOFF/14BOOKRUSHKOFF-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Out of Time: The Sins of Immediacy', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Out of Time: The Sins of Immediacy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rushkoff, Douglas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-13T20:34:56+000