ations', 'section_name': 'Great Homes & Destinations', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/0c804e88-a00f-5a91-bdd6-c86c0d861a7a', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/0c804e88-a00f-5a91-bdd6-c86c0d861a7a'}, {'abstract': 'An outpost of a Williamsburg design shop comes to the Pacific Heights neighborhood of San Francisco.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/14/garden/the-future-perfect-expands-to-san-francisco.html', 'snippet': 'An outpost of a Williamsburg design shop comes to the Pacific Heights neighborhood of San Francisco.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s been a decade since the Future Perfect was founded in Williamsburg as an early champion of the Brooklyn design scene; six years later, a second outpost opened in NoHo. But David Alhadeff, the owner, had always eyed the West Coast. Last week he opened his third shop, in the Pacific Heights neighborhood of San Francisco. “I’ve split my time between New York and San Francisco since 2011, and became very aware of the market here,” Mr. Alhadeff said of the Bay Area. “It’s very progressive and smart.” Among his items are the Future Perfect’s own line of furniture, as well as pieces by Piet Hein Eek and Russell Pinch; lighting by Lindsey Adelman and Charles de Lisle; and accessories from Lobmeyr, Artel, Michele Quan and Fort Standard. The Future Perfect is at 3085 Sacramento Street (Baker Street), (415) 932-6508, thefutureperfect.com.\xa0', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/garden/14OPEN_SPAN/14CURR1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 263, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/14/garden/14OPEN_SPAN/14CURR1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 263}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': Non