ist at War', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Trade Center (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hussein, Saddam', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maass, Peter', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Foreign Policy (Magazine)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-14T14:25:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Maass', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maass', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/521ac181-99b5-57ac-9d5e-782c9057d0d0', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/521ac181-99b5-57ac-9d5e-782c9057d0d0'}, {'abstract': 'Flimflamming away.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/14/night-of-the-living-alesina-continued/', 'snippet': 'Flimflamming away.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So, while I was dealing with real life yesterday — sorry about the blog silence, but sometimes other things are more important — Paul Ryan weighed in. Now, Ryan’s status among the Very Serious People has taken a Very Steep Plunge; at this point Dana Milbank sounds a lot like, well, me back in 2010. (But that won’t stop the VSPs from considering me unsound; I was prematurely anti-flimflam, you know.) Still, it’s worth spending a few seconds parsing his current position.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Night of the Living Alesina, Continued', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-14T14:46:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7d61263-1b9c-5c93-8ed7-be722771f1ac', 'word_count': 325, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7d61263-1b9c-5c93-8ed7-be722771f1ac'}, {'abstract': 'Representatives from the United States Conference of Catholic Bishops and DignityUSA, a Catholic group supporting G.L.B.T. rights, talk about what they want from Pope Francis.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/us/100000002117646/papal-expectations.html', 'snippet': 'Representatives from the United States Conference of Catholic Bishops and DignityUSA, a Catholic group supporting G.L.B.T. rights, talk about what they want from Pope Francis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Representatives from the United States Conference of Catholic Bishops and DignityUSA, a Catholic group supporting G.L.B.T. rights, talk about what they want from Pope Francis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/us/video-tc-130314/video-tc-130314-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/14/us/video-tc-130314/video-tc-130314-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/us/video-tc-130314/video-tc-130314-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 507, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/us/video-tc-130314/video-tc-130314-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 507, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/us/video-tc-130314/video-tc-130314-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/14/us/video-tc-130314/video-tc-130314-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/14/us/video-tc-130314/video-tc-130314-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Papal Expectations', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Conference of Catholic Bishops', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-14T14:47:25+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'Ben Werschkul, Channon Hodge, Pedro Rafael Rosado, Erica Berenstein, Alyssa Kim and Marcus Mabry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Werschkul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Channon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Pedro', 'middlename': 'Rafael', 'lastname': 'Rosado', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'Erica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berenstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}, {'firstname': 'Alyssa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 5}, {'firstname': 'Marcus', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mabry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 6}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/a9a3f572-ec6c-57d8-8309-04fd129eb85b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/a9a3f572-ec6c-57d8-8309-04fd129eb85b'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/14/the-gun-report-march-14-2013/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gun advocates call it DGU. The initials stand for Defensive Gun Use. Gun proponents claim that such incidents – homeowners using guns to protect themselves against intruders – are extremely common, and that we don’t work hard enough to include them in the Gun Report. (A reminder: we compile this daily report by typing “shooting” into Google News and looking at gun injuries and deaths from the past 24 hours. We don’t “favor” any form of gun violence over any other form.) In any case, today we have three examples of DGU, along with one example of a 3-year-old shooting himself by accident. It is unclear whether the gun the child used was owned by someone in his home, but we have our suspicions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: March 14, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALABAMA', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kentucky', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Louisiana', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'St Louis (Mo)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-14T14:50:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/709eb55e-eb48-50ca-a14f-0b5bc5ed13ae', 'word_count': 691, 'uri': 'nyt://article/709eb55e-eb48-50ca-a14f-0b5bc5ed13ae'}, {'abstract': 'Banks are now adding “photo bill payment” features to their mobile banking apps.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/14/next-in-mobile-banking-photo-bill-payments/', 'snippet': 'Banks are now adding “photo bill payment” features to their mobile banking apps.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Now that many banking customers are used to depositing paper checks by snapping a photo with their cellphones, some banks are adding another feature: photo bill pay.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Next in Mobile Banking: Photo Bill Payments', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Commerce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Commerce', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'US Bancorp', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-14T14:54:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3829a36a-8db8-5ea8-b4f0-c054607a1194', 'word_count': 398, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3829a36a-8db8-5ea8-b4f0-c054607a1194'}, {'abstract': 'Is she a poet? No one quite knows for sure.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/magazine/the-inscrutable-brilliance-of-anne-carson.html', 'snippet': 'Is she a poet? No one quite knows for sure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anne Carson was uncomfortable with the idea of a traditional profile: a journalist following her around for a few days, like a private detective, noting her outfits and mannerisms, shadowing her on errands, making lists of furniture and wall decorations and pets, quizzing her students, standing behind her holding his breath while she tried to write in her journal. Carson is a private person. She prefers to be alone. (When her husband is traveling, Carson will call and tell him, “I miss you, but I’m having a great time.”) Her book jackets have no author photo. Her back-flap biography — “Anne Carson was born in Canada and teaches ancient Greek for a living” — is so minimalist that it sounds like a parody of a back-flap biography. Carson told me that, years ago, she had a bad experience with the private-detective model of journalism and would prefer never to do it again. It took her publisher a couple of weeks to wear her down to the point that she would agree, even in a limited way, to participate in a profile. Carson later described those weeks as akin to water torture.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/magazine/17carson1/17carson1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 723, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/17/magazine/17carson1/17carson1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 723}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/magazine/17carson1/17carson1-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/magazine/17carson1/17carson1-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/magazine/17carson1/17carson1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/17/magazine/17carson1/17carson1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/magazine/17carson1/17carson1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Inscrutable Brilliance of Anne Carson', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "'The Most Exciting Thing To Do WIth Your Head'", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carson, Anne', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Red Doc (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-14T14:55:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e653ee49-fb74-5b6c-a1e0-9624b444359b', 'word_count': 3748, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e653ee49-fb74-5b6c-a1e0-9624b444359b'}, {'abstract': 'Wednesday’s rout of the Knicks should leave little doubt that the Nuggets have become the superior team just two years after Carmelo Anthony forced a trade to New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/15/sports/basketball/nuggets-have-sped-past-the-knicks-harvey-araton.html', 'snippet': 'Wednesday’s rout of the Knicks should leave little doubt that the Nuggets have become the superior team just two years after Carmelo Anthony forced a trade to New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DENVER', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/15/sports/jpARATON/jpARATON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 912, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/15/sports/jpARATON/jpARATON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 912}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/15/sports/jpARATON/jpARATON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 674, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url'