ge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Today’s Scuttlebot: Swarming Robots and Competing Taxis', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-14T22:58:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd338b7e-3ac5-5206-b6b5-d45e3631595f', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd338b7e-3ac5-5206-b6b5-d45e3631595f'}, {'abstract': 'The Yankees, who brought their C team, were drubbed by the Blue Jays, the off-season champions in the American League East.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/14/fortunately-for-yankees-this-one-didnt-count/', 'snippet': 'The Yankees, who brought their C team, were drubbed by the Blue Jays, the off-season champions in the American League East.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DUNEDIN, Fla. — The off-season champions in the American League East flexed their new-found muscles Thursday, hammering the Yankees, 17-5. Although the victory was earned by one of the Toronto Blue Jays’ key winter acquisitions, the starting pitcher Josh Johnson, it was still far too early to regard the game as a harbinger of change in what has traditionally been baseball’s toughest division.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fortunately for Yankees, This One Didn’t Count', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Toronto Blue Jays', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-14T23:07:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_na