58-3ade492e5036', 'word_count': 202, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3990a0c-19b4-52a3-9d58-3ade492e5036'}, {'abstract': 'Representatives from Britain and Germany, two of the largest members of the European Union, abstained from the voting, which left the proposal in limbo.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/16/business/global/europe-vote-on-bee-harming-pesticides-is-inconclusive.html', 'snippet': 'Representatives from Britain and Germany, two of the largest members of the European Union, abstained from the voting, which left the proposal in limbo.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A proposed measure to restrict the use of pesticides that have been implicated in bee die-offs around the world was left in limbo on Friday, after representatives from Britain and Germany, two of the largest members of the European Union, abstained from the voting in Brussels. The move left the committee without the needed qualified majority, which gives larger countries greater weight than smaller ones.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/business/Beevote/Beevote-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 443, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/16/business/Beevote/Beevote-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 443}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/business/Beevote/Beevote-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 756, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/business/Beevote/Beevote-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1512, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/business/Beevote/Beevote-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/16/business/Beevote/Beevote-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/business/Beevote/Beevote-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Decision on Bee-Harming Pesticides in Europe', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Decision on Bee-Harming Pesticides In Europe', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Commission', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jolly, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pesticides', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T15:39:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/960fa342-e394-5fbf-92e9-379a94531381', 'word_count': 370, 'uri': 'nyt://article/960fa342-e394-5fbf-92e9-379a94531381'}, {'abstract': 'They have made a desert and called it a “delicate balance”.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/15/delusions-at-the-european-commission/', 'snippet': 'They have made a desert and called it a “delicate balance”.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I see that Vox has posted a self-justifying piece on fiscal austerity from the European Commission, declaring that the Commission is pursuing a “delicate balance”. Actually, that’s kind of awesome: how does that “delicate balance” feel in countries with 15, 20, 25 percent unemployment?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Delusions at the European Commission', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T15:41:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f9054ad-c2bf-5d59-b57f-7c80d0cb8203', 'word_count': 301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f9054ad-c2bf-5d59-b57f-7c80d0cb8203'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney defied predictions that he might get a cold shoulder from a grass-roots conservative crowd, some of whom had their doubts about him as the Republican nominee.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/15/at-cpac-mcconnell-urges-party-to-fight-health-law/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney defied predictions that he might get a cold shoulder from a grass-roots conservative crowd, some of whom had their doubts about him as the Republican nominee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OXON HILL, Md. – Mitt Romney received an overwhelmingly warm welcome here at the Conservative Political Action Conference, known as CPAC, defying predictions that he might get the cold shoulder from a grass-roots crowd, some of whom had their doubts about him as the Republican nominee.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney’s Warm Reunion, and Other CPAC Updates', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McConnell, Mitch', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Conservative Political Action Conference', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T15:43:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Rutenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rutenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbff9649-df4b-5d88-86d7-d3167dd0302e', 'word_count': 1076, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbff9649-df4b-5d88-86d7-d3167dd0302e'}, {'abstract': 'Nearly 70 percent of Americans ages 18 to 64 said they had chatted on their phones while driving in the past 30 days, and about 30 percent said they had sent text messages while behind the wheel, far more than drivers in seven European countries.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/15/the-distracted-american-driver/', 'snippet': 'Nearly 70 percent of Americans ages 18 to 64 said they had chatted on their phones while driving in the past 30 days, and about 30 percent said they had sent text messages while behind the wheel, far more than drivers in seven European countries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Despite increasing media attention and laws forbidding the practice, more Americans than ever are using their cellphones to talk and send text messages while driving, a new study shows.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/science/15wellphone/15wellphone-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 387, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/16/science/15wellphone/15wellphone-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 387}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/science/15wellphone/15wellphone-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 660, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/science/15wellphone/15wellphone-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1320, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/science/15wellphone/15wellphone-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/16/science/15wellphone/15wellphone-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/science/15wellphone/15wellphone-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Distracted American Driver', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Distracted American Drivers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drunken and Reckless Driving', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Roads and Traffic', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Text Messaging', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T15:45:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': "By Anahad O'Connor", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/78f72877-9b8a-518e-b7fa-30a9abc1ad96', 'word_count': 529, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78f72877-9b8a-518e-b7fa-30a9abc1ad96'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of letters between Paul Auster and J. M. Coetzee, in which the writers attempt to “strike sparks off each other.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/books/review/here-and-now-by-paul-auster-and-j-m-coetzee.html', 'snippet': 'A collection of letters between Paul Auster and J. M. Coetzee, in which the writers attempt to “strike sparks off each other.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s an odd moment for literary letters as a genre. The proliferation of letter volumes published over the past few years (Beckett, Bellow, Wodehouse, Gaddis, with Calvino and Wallace on the way) might be considered post-mortem in more ways than one, putting to bed a form that is already effectively dead, along with its authors. For while the \xade-\xadrevolution means changes in how we read any sort of book, the move from epistles to \xade-\xadpistles is, for better or worse, a fundamental redefinition of the genre itself. And while there are probably plenty of contemporary writers saving up their best e-mails on external hard drives for later publication, it’s difficult to imagine those looking or acting like a volume of letters. Since you will read these collected e-mails on your electronic device, there’s a fairly good chance they will look like, well, your in-box.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/17riker-img/17riker-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 364, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/17riker-img/17riker-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 364}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/17riker-img/17riker-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 622, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/17riker-img/17riker-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 1219, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/17riker-img/17riker-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/17riker-img/17riker-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/17riker-img/17riker-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pen Pals', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pen Pals', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Auster, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coetzee, J M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T15:48:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Riker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Riker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e227d936-d189-5f44-956b-348643218dc7', 'word_count': 1206, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e227d936-d189-5f44-956b-348643218dc7'}, {'abstract': 'Forget fictionalized memoirs. More troubling are the countless novels tainted by elements of truth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/books/review/true-lies.html', 'snippet': 'Forget fictionalized memoirs. More troubling are the countless novels tainted by elements of truth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In January, two California men, a political consultant and a professional chef, filed suit against Lance Armstrong and his publishers after his interview with Oprah Winfrey, in which he confessed to blood doping and using other performance-enhancing drugs during his Tour de France championship spree. In their complaint, the litigants claimed they would not have purchased Armstrong’s autobiographies “It’s Not About the Bike” (2000) and “Every Second Counts” (2003) had they known that the books were built on a foundation of lies. Any inspirational messages contained within, they alleged, were communicated with a flagrant disregard for truth. Armstrong’s stories of training vigilantly and succeeding as a result grossly misrepresented actual circumstance. Moreover, the lawsuit suggested, the two books were categorically dishonest: they were marketed as nonfiction when they were, it turns out, fictitious.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Greenman/0317-Greenman-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1239, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Greenman/0317-Greenman-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1239}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Greenman/0317-Greenman-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 496, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Greenman/0317-Greenman-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1919, 'width': 929, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Greenman/0317-Greenman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Greenman/0317-Greenman-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Greenman/0317-Greenman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'True Lies', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'True Lies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T15:49:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Greenman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Greenman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d06b4f05-6651-548e-a7fc-1771cc646b42', 'word_count': 1210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d06b4f05-6651-548e-a7fc-1771cc646b42'}, {'abstract': 'In this memoir, Emily Rapp describes the months after learning of her young son’s fatal genetic disorder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/books/review/emily-rapps-still-point-of-the-turning-world.html', 'snippet': 'In this memoir, Emily Rapp describes the months after learning of her young son’s fatal genetic disorder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The “grief memoir” is by now a well-established subgenre of autobiography, the array of recent books about dead loved ones a veritable graveyard. The best of these — Jamaica Kincaid’s “My Brother” and Peter Handke’s “Sorrow Beyond Dreams” — aren’t just sad stories; they’re attempts to write one’s way out of the crisis. So it is with Emily Rapp’s “Still Point of the Turning World,” a memoir in which a mother’s grief occasions a brilliant study of the wages of mortal love.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Manguso/0317-Manguso-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 807, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Manguso/0317-Manguso-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 807}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Manguso/0317-Manguso-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 762, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Manguso/0317-Manguso-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1851, 'width': 1377, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 's