 'word_count': 255, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8565e66e-d392-54d7-8b64-c93a2d74bf9b'}, {'abstract': 'Pat Summitt, the renowned women’s basketball coach who won eight national championships at the University of Tennessee, is now a No. 1 best-selling author, with the memoir “Sum It Up.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Pat Summitt, the renowned women’s basketball coach who won eight national championships at the University of Tennessee, is now a No. 1 best-selling author, with the memoir “Sum It Up.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A FISH CALLED GENO: Pat Summitt, the renowned women’s basketball coach from the University of Tennessee, is used to being No. 1: with 1,098 career victories, eight national championships and a win-loss ratio of .840, she is by some measures the most successful coach in N.C.A.A. history. So it makes a poetic kind of sense that her memoir — the wincingly titled “Sum It Up,” written with Sally Jenkins — would shoot straight to the top in its first week on the hardcover nonfiction list. The book is tumultuous and engaging (“one long roar of acceleration,” as Summitt describes her undefeated 1997-98 season), and unafraid to throw a good-natured elbow or two.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24list-img/24list-img-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 467, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24list-img/24list-img-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 467, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24list-img/24list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24list-img/24list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24list-img/24list-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T15:51:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83459f16-dade-528c-9101-b4585e6a5703', 'word_count': 601, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83459f16-dade-528c-9101-b4585e6a5703'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Moss reveals how food companies use science to maximize our cravings for their products.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/books/review/salt-sugar-fat-by-michael-moss.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Moss reveals how food companies use science to maximize our cravings for their products.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is a certain enlightened segment of America that relishes a good gastro-scolding, whether delivered gently by a Michael Pollan (“Eat food. Not too much. Mostly plants”) or more vituperatively by a Mark Bittman (“In the time it takes to go into a McDonald’s, stand in line, order, wait, pay and leave, you could make oatmeal for four while taking your vitamins, brushing your teeth and half-unloading the dishwasher”). But there is a much larger segment of America whose members heedlessly eat processed foods that make them overweight and unwell. Michael Moss, a dogged investigative reporter who neither scolds nor proselytizes, is here for them.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Kamp/0317-Kamp-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 587, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Kamp/0317-Kamp-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 587}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Kamp/0317-Kamp-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1002, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Kamp/0317-Kamp-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2005, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Kamp/0317-Kamp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Kamp/0317-Kamp-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Kamp/0317-Kamp-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Sweet It Is', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': 