'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9917136-eeb8-5faf-8b0f-af97cc7ce168', 'word_count': 872, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9917136-eeb8-5faf-8b0f-af97cc7ce168'}, {'abstract': 'A summer home both shelters and isolates the family in Elizabeth Graver’s multigenerational novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/books/review/the-end-of-the-point-by-elizabeth-graver.html', 'snippet': 'A summer home both shelters and isolates the family in Elizabeth Graver’s multigenerational novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Late in Elizabeth Graver’s eloquent new novel, a therapist warns a very troubled young man against what he calls the “geographic” cure: “You can’t fix yourself by going somewhere else. . . . You’ll always take yourself along.” The young man, convinced that his family’s summer home on the coast of Massachusetts is the only place he has ever been happy, instantly bristles. “Ashaunt,” Charlie insists, isn’t “somewhere else.” By now, having occupied this windswept spit of land along with the Porters from World War II through Vietnam, Graver’s readers will be inclined to agree. It is indeed special, sometimes almost magical. But we also realize, having come to know the other lives entangled with Charlie’s, that Ashaunt’s spell can involve something much more complex than a dearly held childhood attachment, that a house like this, where “everyone could be together and so tight,” can also feel “vise tight, tight enough to blow a fuse.” The therapist may be right. Wherever they go, Graver’s characters will carry Ashaunt along, but its look and feel will be different for each of them, colored by the emotions they inhabit just as intimately as they do its drafty rooms and sandy footpaths.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317BECKER/0317BECKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 753, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317BECKER/0317BECKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 753}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317BECKER/0317BECKER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 816, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317BECKER/0317BECKER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1632, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317BECKER/0317BECKER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317BECKER/0317BECKER-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317BECKER/0317BECKER-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Full House', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Full House', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Graver, Elizabeth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T15:53:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alida Becker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alida', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Becker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/168a2073-420a-59b5-87aa-67db76f51c65', 'word_count': 1173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/168a2073-420a-59b5-87aa-67db76f51c65'}, {'abstract': 'In Renata Adler’s reissued novels, vignette follows vignette.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/books/review/speedboat-and-pitch-dark-by-renata-adler.html', 'snippet': 'In Renata Adler’s reissued novels, vignette follows vignette.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was a time when her name would provoke admiration, anger and, not infrequently, fear. Renata Adler was a purveyor of definitive judgments, of arguments that seemed to march inexorably from a firmly held point of view. In the late ’60s, during a 14-month stint as a film critic for The New York Times, she eviscerated a good deal of what Hollywood had to offer. (Her loathing for John Wayne’s hyper-\xadpatriotic “Green Berets” — “so unspeakable, so stupid, so rotten and false in every detail” — was entered into the Congressional Record by Strom Thurmond, who cited it as definitive proof “that this must be one of the most admirable movies of our generation.”) In 1980, she wrote an 8,000-word essay in The New York Review of Books that denounced the work of Pauline Kael as “piece by piece, line by line, and without interruption, worthless.” Throughout it all, she was a writer for The New Yorker, and her memoir of nearly 30 years there was similarly unsparing, showing that her category of “old friend” wasn’t necessarily distinct from that of “fair game.” She seemed dissatisfied with merely cutting something down to size; whatever offended her had to be put out of its miserable existence, and she wielded her hatchet with methodical and merciless strokes.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317SZALAI/0317SZALAI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317SZALAI/0317SZALAI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 740}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317SZALAI/0317SZALAI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 830, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317SZALAI/0317SZALAI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1660, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317SZALAI/0317SZALAI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317SZALAI/0317SZALAI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317SZALAI/0317SZALAI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jump-Cuts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jump-Cuts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Szalai, Jennifer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ADLER, RENATA', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T15:53:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Szalai', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Szalai', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a478ba2-3b95-5cab-b707-325d4ed52de3', 'word_count': 1244, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a478ba2-3b95-5cab-b707-325d4ed52de3'}, {'abstract': 'Alex Berenson’s superspy tries to rescue four relief workers kidnapped by a Somali warlord.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/books/review/the-night-ranger-by-alex-berenson.html', 'snippet': 'Alex Berenson’s superspy tries to rescue four relief workers kidnapped by a Somali warlord.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is the seventh outing for Alex Berenson’s central character, John Wells, and the mileage is starting to show. Although “The Night Ranger” is skillfully engineered, its action hero feels distinctly weary.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'International Aid', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'International Aid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berenson, Alex', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T15:54:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam LeBor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'LeBor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7a7ca791-1d24-545f-b236-df1195792341', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7a7ca791-1d24-545f-b236-df1195792341'}, {'abstract': 'Clive Davis reviews his career as a hit-maker.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/books/review/the-soundtrack-of-my-life-by-clive-davis.html', 'snippet': 'Clive Davis reviews his career as a hit-maker.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No mere vulgar showbiz reminiscence — and that’s kind of a pity — Clive Davis’s autobiography has the ceremonious heft we associate with presidential memoirs. At 586 dense pages, “The Soundtrack of My Life” is dwarfed by Bill Clinton’s herculean “My Life.” But it leaves the shrimpy 497 pages of George W. Bush’s “Decision Points” looking like an amuse-bouche. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317CARSON/0317CARSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317CARSON/0317CARSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 740}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317CARSON/0317CARSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 830, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317CARSON/0317CARSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1660, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317CARSON/0317CARSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317CARSON/0317CARSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': '