'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy Kline', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kline', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee1c9e5d-84b2-5183-b5a6-9a87d26ef908', 'word_count': 595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee1c9e5d-84b2-5183-b5a6-9a87d26ef908'}, {'abstract': 'Lucy Ellmann’s novel follows the romantic escapades of a New York plastic surgeon who discovers his inner feminist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/books/review/mimi-by-lucy-ellmann.html', 'snippet': 'Lucy Ellmann’s novel follows the romantic escapades of a New York plastic surgeon who discovers his inner feminist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lucy Ellmann’s sixth novel, “Mimi,” starts off promisingly, and there are passages that mark her as a talented and erudite writer. But that Ellmann appears to share a word processor with another Lucy Ellmann, who insists on aggressively cloying tics: exclamation marks, Capital Letters and italics. She deploys these mannerisms with such remorseless, in-your-face frequency that the reader is left wondering whether Ellmann is attempting some elusive gestalt. Elusive, that is, unless gestalt also means “self-indulgent” in German.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Buckley/0317-Buckley-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 574, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Buckley/0317-Buckley-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 574}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Buckley/0317-Buckley-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 980, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Buckley/0317-Buckley-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1349, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Buckley/0317-Buckley-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Buckley/0317-Buckley-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317-Buckley/0317-Buckley-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Carnal Knowledge', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Carnal Knowledge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buckley, Christopher', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ellmann, Lucy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T15:55:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf90098f-8902-5a1d-b6c3-a85c143568df', 'word_count': 1290, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf90098f-8902-5a1d-b6c3-a85c143568df'}, {'abstract': 'Constance Wilde was more than just a long-suffering wife.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/books/review/constance-by-franny-moyle.html', 'snippet': 'Constance Wilde was more than just a long-suffering wife.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No one was ever more suitably named, at birth and by marriage, than Constance Wilde. Her first name conveys her near-endless loyalty to her irresponsible, genius husband, Oscar. Even after the worst of the humiliations — after he had taken up with the pretty young Lord Alfred Douglas and been sent to prison for the affair, after her household goods had been auctioned to pay the high-living Oscar’s debts, after she had fled to the Continent and changed the family name to protect their two small sons — she could still refer to him as a “poor, poor fellow” and write in a letter, “What a tragedy for him who is so gifted!”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317JAMES/0317JAMES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 527, 'width': 351, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317JAMES/0317JAMES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 527, 'width': 351, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317JAMES/0317JAMES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317JAMES/0317JAMES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317JAMES/0317JAMES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Woman of Importance', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Woman of Importance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moyle, Franny', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilde, Constance', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T15:55:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caryn James', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'James', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/da9f7b75-d6bc-5ab5-bf07-e9ebc674b2f9', 'word_count': 615, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da9f7b75-d6bc-5ab5-bf07-e9ebc674b2f9'}, {'abstract': 'Philip Hensher on the fading art of writing longhand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/books/review/the-missing-ink-by-philip-hensher.html', 'snippet': 'Philip Hensher on the fading art of writing longhand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In an era when the comment “You’re all thumbs” can be taken as high praise of texting prowess, the notion of hand writing a note, a business letter or even a grocery list seems quaint — like studying elocution or fastening shoes with mother-of-pearl buttons. Bemoaning the decline of the written hand smacks of fogyism, but the British novelist Philip Hensher, who is also a columnist for The Independent and an arts critic for The Spectator, enlivens his musings about penmanship’s demise with sharp insights and wry wit. In “The Missing Ink,” he argues that handwriting fills a human need: “It involves us in a relationship with the written word which is sensuous, immediate and individual. It opens our personality out to the world, and gives us a means of reading other people.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317MEISEL/0317MEISEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 287, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317MEISEL/0317MEISEL-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 287}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317MEISEL/0317MEISEL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 489, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317MEISEL/0317MEISEL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 803, 'width': 1680, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317MEISEL/0317MEISEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317MEISEL/0317MEISEL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/books/review/0317MEISEL/0317MEISEL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lost Letters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lost Letters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meisel, Abigail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'v