ews_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/35530796-0563-5e21-a0f2-a1f98a1b3caa', 'word_count': 13, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35530796-0563-5e21-a0f2-a1f98a1b3caa'}, {'abstract': 'An increasing number of high-profile Republicans, like Senator Rob Portman of Ohio, have recently announced support for same-sex marriage. But polling of rank-and-file Republicans shows they are still largely opposed.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/15/among-g-o-p-voters-little-support-for-same-sex-marriage/', 'snippet': 'An increasing number of high-profile Republicans, like Senator Rob Portman of Ohio, have recently announced support for same-sex marriage. But polling of rank-and-file Republicans shows they are still largely opposed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The decision by the Senator Rob Portman, a Republican from Ohio, to announce his support for same-sex marriage may come to be seen as a watershed moment for gay rights advocates. Mr. Portman’s announcement, which he said he made in part because his son is gay, has so far yielded relatively little pushback from Republicans on blogs and social media, or from other Republican office-holders. Instead, gay rights advocates are increasingly finding support from influential Republicans.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Among G.O.P. Voters, Little Support for Same-Sex Marriage', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T20:14:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organiz