ning', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'David Frank', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frank', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/a95ad077-d6e0-5f54-a860-d68cba438a37', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/a95ad077-d6e0-5f54-a860-d68cba438a37'}, {'abstract': 'About 3,000 seats in the upper bowl will be lowered to $50 per game, bringing the total to 9,000 seats, or about one-third of the upper bowl, that will be priced at this level.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/15/jets-cheap-seats-get-a-little-cheaper/', 'snippet': 'About 3,000 seats in the upper bowl will be lowered to $50 per game, bringing the total to 9,000 seats, or about one-third of the upper bowl, that will be priced at this level.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After winning just six games last season, their worst showing since 2007, the Jets said Friday that they were trimming the price of some of their cheapest tickets and not increasing the price of the other 80,000 seats in MetLife Stadium.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jets’ Cheap Seats Get a Little Cheaper', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jets Lower Ticket Prices', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Jets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T20:19:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0744b4aa-6c5c-56aa-ab02-356845fe09f7', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0744b4aa-6c5c-56aa-ab02-356845fe09f7'}, {'abstract': 'A salad inspired by the Seder staple haroseth, and infused with Manischewitz Concord grape wine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/20/dining/a-salad-with-a-passover-connection.html', 'snippet': 'A salad inspired by the Seder staple haroseth, and infused with Manischewitz Concord grape wine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OTHER THAN HAROSETH and Caribbean black cake, there are not a lot of recipes calling for Manischewitz Concord grape wine. I know this because I’ve had the same bottle for what might be decades. I unearth the dusty thing during Passover to anoint my haroseth, then return the bottle to the depths of the liquor cabinet. It’s been a challenge to use up.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Secret at the Back of the Liquor Cabinet', 'kicker': 'A Good Appetite', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Secret at the Back of the Liquor Cabinet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Melissa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Passover', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salads', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Liquor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T20:20:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melissa Clark', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melissa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Clark', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56822305-a95f-5d30-ab95-a38dc565dbfb', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56822305-a95f-5d30-ab95-a38dc565dbfb'}, {'abstract': 'Jason Giambi, who said his leaked testimony about using steroids was freeing, was a managerial candidate over the winter and is now a respected veteran in Indians camp.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/16/sports/baseball/jason-giambi-reinvents-himself-and-baseball-is-intrigued.html', 'snippet': 'Jason Giambi, who said his leaked testimony about using steroids was freeing, was a managerial candidate over the winter and is now a respected veteran in Indians camp.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GOODYEAR, Ariz. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/sports/subKEPNER/subKEPNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/16/sports/subKEPNER/subKEPNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 389}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/sports/subKEPNER/subKEPNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 664, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/sports/subKEPNER/subKEPNER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1202, 'width': 1853, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/sports/subKEPNER/subKEPNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/16/sports/subKEPNER/subKEPNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/16/sports/subKEPNER/subKEPNER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Giambi Reinvents Himself, and Baseball Is Intrigued', 'kicker': 'On Baseball', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New Role for Older, Wiser Giambi', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kepner, Tyler', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Giambi, Jason', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cleveland Indians', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Steroids', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Colorado Rockies', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T20:22:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fbafde87-0c72-5586-bda8-2f67d9849196', 'word_count': 1180, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fbafde87-0c72-5586-bda8-2f67d9849196'}, {'abstract': 'You’re supposed to think about them, too.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/15/other-peoples-children/', 'snippet': 'You’re supposed to think about them, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Matthew Yglesias beats me to a point I was planning to make. Sen. Rob Portman has made headlines by declaring his support for gay marriage after learning that his own son is gay, and apparently we’re supposed to praise him for his new enlightenment. But while enlightenment is good, wouldn’t it have been a lot more praiseworthy if he had shown some flexibility on the issue before he knew that his own family would benefit?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Other People’s Children', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T20:23:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d717bc04-de30-528d-8892-3c312ec3bfd4', 'word_count': 195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d717bc04-de30-528d-8892-3c312ec3bfd4'}, {'abstract': 'A fifth-grade class has a sleep-away field trip. What do do with a child who doesn’t want to go?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/15/should-we-force-children-into-sleep-aways/', 'snippet': 'A fifth-grade class has a sleep-away field trip. What do do with a child who doesn’t want to go?', 'lead_paragraph': 'A reader’s child has a mandatory school sleep-away activity coming up. The fifth-grade class at her child’s (public) school traditionally spends a night away on a particular field trip, and the reader’s child doesn’t want to go.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Should We Force Children into Sleep-Aways?', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-15T20:24:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 're