'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zach Schonbrun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zach', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schonbrun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd8cae3b-d882-5298-88c7-3be073139b58', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd8cae3b-d882-5298-88c7-3be073139b58'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/16/arts/television/whats-on-saturday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (HBO) THE BEST EXOTIC MARIGOLD HOTEL (2012) Seven financially distressed British retirees (Judi Dench, Bill Nighy, Penelope Wilton, Celia Imrie, Ronald Pickup, Tom Wilkinson and Maggie Smith) are lured by invitation to “outsource” themselves for a stay at what is billed as a luxurious retreat “for the elderly and beautiful” in Jaipur, India, in this film directed by John Madden. But upon arrival, they discover that the place is far from what was advertised by their host (Dev Patel, above right with Tena Desae), a wildly disorganized but enthusiastic young man who neglected to mention the lack of working telephones and doors on the rooms. “This leisurely paced two-hour movie is a reasonably tasty banquet for the same Anglophiles who embrace ‘Downton Abbey,’\xa0” Stephen Holden wrote in The New York Times. “Although it’s not as tidy or comfy as that self-congratulating fantasy of British noblesse oblige or as elevated as the Merchant-Ivory films set in India, it’ll do nicely.” He added, “Besides its sterling cast, its ace in the hole is its pungent depiction of Jaipur’s teeming streets, which give an otherwise well-mannered movie a blinding splash of color.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Saturday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-16T05:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/000d74bf-b55b-5804-838e-f2062963f8c2', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/000d74bf-b55b-5804-838e-f2062963f8c2'}, {'abstract': 'Seeking better results, the city divided Adlai E. Stevenson High School in the Bronx into nine theme-based institutions; today the Stevenson Campus has the most schools under one roof anywhere in the city.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/nyregion/at-the-stevenson-campus-nine-high-schools-one-roof.html', 'snippet': 'Seeking better results, the city divided Adlai E. Stevenson High School in the Bronx into nine theme-based institutions; today the Stevenson Campus has the most schools under one roof anywhere in the city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When teachers heard footsteps thundering in the hallways at Adlai E. Stevenson High School in the Bronx, they threw down iron gates to stop student gangs from rioting, even as other students tried to deconstruct literature behind locked classroom doors. A decade ago, Stevenson, in the borough’s Castle Hill section, was one of the city’s most dangerous, overcrowded schools, barely containing its 3,054 students — when they showed up for class.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/17/nyregion/17STEVENSON2/17STEVENSON2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'c