, 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bruni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c875660d-304d-541f-a607-459166b2ff8f', 'word_count': 1111, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c875660d-304d-541f-a607-459166b2ff8f'}, {'abstract': 'When doctors and nurses don’t get along, it’s the patient who suffers.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/16/healing-the-hospital-hierarchy/', 'snippet': 'When doctors and nurses don’t get along, it’s the patient who suffers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A hospital is, by its nature, the scene of constant life-or-death situations. It’s the work we nurses, doctors and other health professionals do; we chose it. The threat of harm can jazz you up or bring you down, but what it should demand, always, is the highest possible level of professionalism. Who’s at risk when that doesn’t occur?', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Healing the Hospital Hierarchy', 'kicker': 'Bedside', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Healing the Hospital Hierarchy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nursing and Nurses', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-16T17:45:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Theresa Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Theresa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36c14fe7-f87d-5c0e-99f8-78f0eb88a52c