the inevitable breakdown and disappearance of authentic equipment. And beyond the indie-rock circuit, it’s pretty unlikely that that totally 1960s-style psychedelia would become a commercial force. But against those odds, young bands keep doing the 1960s better.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'SXSW Music: The 1960s, Getting Better With Age', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'South by Southwest Music and Media Conference', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-16T18:37:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee256e51-0b08-5bf7-9117-9b3912652a39', 'word_count': 311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee256e51-0b08-5bf7-9117-9b3912652a39'}, {'abstract': 'The furor over New York Road Runners’ handling of its marathon last year has died down, but questions about the group’s future persist, including whether its dominance has started to erode because of rival race organizers and disaffected runners.', 'web_url': 'https://marathon.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/16/road-runners-facing-difficulty-months-after-marathon-uproar/', 'snippet': 'The furor over New York Road Runners’ handling of its marathon last year has died down, but questions about the group’s future persist, including whether its dominance has started to erode because of rival race organizers and disaffected runners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The furor over New York Road Runners’ handling of its marathon last year has died down, but questions about the group’s future persist, including whether its dominance has started to erode because of rival race organizers and disaffected runners. Read full story here. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Road Runners Facing Difficulty Months After Marathon Uproar', 'kicker': 'On the Run', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York City Marathon', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Running', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Road Runners Club', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-16T18:49:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1093f441-dcf2-5bbc-957e-2667835297cc', 'word_count': 44, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1093f441-dcf2-5bbc-957e-2667835297cc'}, {'abstract': 'The statement was the latest in a series in which North Korea has appeared to harden its position on its nuclear weapons program.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/world/asia/north-korea-says-nuclear-arms-arent-a-ploy.html', 'snippet': 'The statement was the latest in a series in which North Korea has appeared to harden its position on its nuclear weapons program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — North Korea said Saturday that its nuclear weapons were not a bargaining chip to trade for economic concessions, warning that it would never negotiate with the United States as long as Washington maintained its hostile policy toward the North.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Trading Nuclear Arms for Aid, North Korea Says', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Trading Nuclear Arms For Aid, North Korea Says', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None