ocument_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90344ad4-0f22-5327-90c0-a2b95530bd72', 'word_count': 706, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90344ad4-0f22-5327-90c0-a2b95530bd72'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/arts/television/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '10 P.M. (E!) PLAYING WITH FIRE The former “Top Chef” competitor Candice Kumai; the night-life impresarios turned food entrepreneurs Derek and Daniel Koch; the brand ambassador Anna Boiardi; the pastry chef Julie Elkind; the restaurateur Todd English; and the actress and bakery owner Jennifer Esposito turn their lives into open cookbooks as they storm the New York culinary scene in this new reality series. In this premiere episode, the Koch twins prepare for the grand opening of Toy, a restaurant inside the Gansevoort Hotel. Ms. Boiardi, meanwhile, finds that her mother disapproves of her Bolognese-cooking technique. And Mr. English celebrates his birthday and the 10th anniversary of his restaurant Olives. Above, a scene from the show.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-17T16:07:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/09397d1c-f0d1-5f78-b133-925802f124d0', 'word_count': 655, 'uri': 'nyt://article/09397d1c-f0d1-5f78-b133-925802f124d0'}, {'abstract': '“Oz the Great and Powerful” took in an estimated $42.2 million at North American theaters over the weekend for first place at the box office.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/17/disneys-oz-keeps-lead-at-box-office/', 'snippet': '“Oz the Great and Powerful” took in an estimated $42.2 million at North American theaters over the weekend for first place at the box office.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Oz the Great and Powerful” continued to cast a spell on moviegoers over the weekend, while another magician — “The Incredible Burt Wonderstone” — essentially arrived to a chorus of boos. “Oz” (Disney) took in an estimated $42.2 million at theaters in the United States and Canada, for a strong two-week total of $145 million, according to Hollywood.com, which compiles box-office data. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Disney’s ‘Oz’ Keeps Lead at Box Office', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Gold for Disney Along Yellow-Brick Road', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hollywood.com', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Walt Disney Company', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-17T16:08:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooks Barnes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barnes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec364428-8e4e-5507-b3f0-6159387441c2', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec3