hostakovich cycle at Lincoln Center on Sunday afternoon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Shostakovich’s career, pockmarked with forced commissions, attempts to placate Soviet officials and humiliating bouts of censorship, his string quartets occupy a special place. Many critics have likened them to a confessional into which the composer confided the fears and hopes he had to suppress in public. But most of all, the 15 quartets represent the music Shostakovich wanted to write at a given moment.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Moments of Lyricism in a Purely Russian Story', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Moments of Lyricism in a Purely Russian Story', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'da Fonseca-Wollheim, Corinna', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chamber Music Society of Lincoln Center', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Jerusalem Quartet', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shostakovich, Dmitri', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kam, Ori', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bresler, Sergei', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zlotnikov, Kyril', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pavlovsky, Alexander', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-18T21:47:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corinna da Fonseca-Wollheim', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1e67345-fb6c-528d-8e48-f4d688bf3e3b', 'word_count': 504, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1e67345-fb6c-528d-8e48-f4d688bf3e3b'}, {'abstract': '“Bell” by Yasuko Yokoshi recasts a Kabuki play into a dance mixing Eastern and Western elements.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/19/arts/dance/yasuko-yokoshis-bell-at-new-york-live-arts.html', 'snippet': '“Bell” by Yasuko Yokoshi recasts a Kabuki play into a dance mixing Eastern and Western elements.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Rudyard Kipling ballad starts with the refrain “Oh, East is East, and West is West, and never the twain shall meet.” In the choreographer Yasuko Yokoshi’s “Bell,” which had its world premiere on Saturday at New York Live Arts, East and West indeed don’t meet. But they share the stage; they overlap; they coincide. The result, unlike any production I have seen, runs a gamut: It’s peculiar, admirable, a bit long-winded and sometimes perplexing. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/arts/19YOKOSHI/19YOKOSHI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/19/arts/19YOKOSHI/19YOKOSHI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/arts/19YOKOSHI/19YOKOSHI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 625, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/arts/19YOKOSHI/19YOKOSHI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1250, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/arts/19YOKOSHI/19YOKOSHI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/19/arts/19YOKOSHI/19YOKOSHI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/arts/19YOKOSHI/19YOKOSHI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Giselle’ Meets Kabuki in a Tale of Love, Madness and Toxic Breath', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Giselle’ Meets Kabuki in a Tale of Love, Madness and Toxic Breath', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yokoshi, Yasuko', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Live Arts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kabuki (Japanese Theater)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-18T21:54:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9077d795-c7d7-5fa1-b98f-2de17020b20d', 'word_count': 784, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9077d795-c7d7-5fa1-b98f-2de17020b20d'}, {'abstract': 'Playing a pop goddess in “The Bodyguard,” the new musical based on the 1992 movie that starred Whitney Houston and Kevin Costner, Heather Headley gives great diva.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/19/theater/reviews/the-bodyguard-with-heather-headley-in-london.html', 'snippet': 'Playing a pop goddess in “The Bodyguard,” the new musical based on the 1992 movie that starred Whitney Houston and Kevin Costner, Heather Headley gives great diva.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — Playing a pop goddess in “The Bodyguard,” the new musical based on the 1992 movie that starred Whitney Houston and Kevin Costner, Heather Headley gives great diva. Ms. Headley, who won (and deserved) a Tony as the title character in Disney’s “Aida,” scores another personal triumph and once again transcends her material as the celebrity in jeopardy in Alexander Dinelaris’s lumpy adaptation of Lawrence Kasdan’s original screenplay. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/arts/jpbodyguard/jpbodyguard-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 349, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/19/arts/jpbodyguard/jpbodyguard-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 349}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/arts/jpbodyguard/jpbodyguard-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 595, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/arts/jpbodyguard/jpbodyguard-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1190, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/arts/jpbodyguard/jpbodyguard-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/19/arts/jpbodyguard/jpbodyguard-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None,