'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Salman Masood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Salman', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Masood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/93da8742-97b6-583a-a898-2bf0734ad388', 'word_count': 680, 'uri': 'nyt://article/93da8742-97b6-583a-a898-2bf0734ad388'}, {'abstract': 'Two co-founders of BlackBerry have started a $100 million fund to invest in companies using quantum physics. They plan to help create ultrapowerful sensors and motors, building on esoteric discoveries at research labs they’ve financed in Ontario.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/19/creating-canadas-quantum-valley/', 'snippet': 'Two co-founders of BlackBerry have started a $100 million fund to invest in companies using quantum physics. They plan to help create ultrapowerful sensors and motors, building on esoteric discoveries at research labs they’ve financed in Ontario.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Showing how quickly an esoteric technology can become mainstream, Mike Lazaridis and Doug Fregin, who together founded BlackBerry, are establishing a $100 million venture fund for technologies that employ practical applications of quantum physics. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/technology/19bits-quantum/19bits-quantum-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/19/technology/19bits-quantum/19bits-quantum-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 407}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/technology/19bits-quantum/19bits-quantum-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 694, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name'