lo by Mairi Dorman-Phaneuf; the arrangement is by Rob Berman, the show’s musical director. “Passion” continues through April 19.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Performance: Judy Kuhn of ‘Passion’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kuhn, Judy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lapine, James', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sondheim, Stephen', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Classic Stage Company', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-19T12:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Erik Piepenburg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Piepenburg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cc0dae52-463a-54a7-bc0f-dcee2039d7a5', 'word_count': 172, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cc0dae52-463a-54a7-bc0f-dcee2039d7a5'}, {'abstract': 'Granted, the subjunctive mood is a bit more arcane than some of our other common grammar problems, like subject-verb agreement. Still, it’s a sign of polish and precision to get this right. The subjunctive is most often used in contrary-to-fact conditions and for wishes and other hypothetical expressions.', 'web_url': 'https://afterdeadline.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/19/favorite-grammar-gaffes-the-subjunctive/', 'snippet': 'Granted, the subjunctive mood is a bit more arcane than some of our other common grammar problems, like subject-verb agreement. Still, it’s a sign of polish and precision to get this right. The subjunctive is most often used in contrary-to-fact co...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The subjunctive mood is a bit more arcane than other entries on our list of favorite grammar gaffes, like subject-verb agreement. Still, it’s a sign of polish and precision to get this right. The subjunctive is most often used in contrary-to-fact conditions and for wishes and other hypothetical expressions.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Subjunctivitis', 'kicker': 'After Deadline', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-19T12:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Times Topics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip B. Corbett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f652107e-df22-5b86-abb6-1d48f9a056f9', 'word_count': 1338, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f652107e-df22-5b86-abb6-1d48f9a056f9'}, {'abstract': 'A plan to tax bank depositors in Cyprus sets off alarms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/19/opinion/global/taxing-deposits-in-cyprus.html', 'snippet': 'A plan to tax bank depositors in Cyprus sets off alarms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A plan to tax bank depositors in Cyprus sets off alarms.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/opinion/global/19iht-edchappatte19/19iht-edchappatte19-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 452, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/19/opinion/global/19iht-edchappatte19/19iht-edchappatte19-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 452}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/opinion/global/19iht-edchappatte19/19iht-edchappatte19-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 490, 'width': 650, 'sub