 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3a8d1cc-0829-56ed-bed1-152ba35025a2', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3a8d1cc-0829-56ed-bed1-152ba35025a2'}, {'abstract': 'Astor Piazzolla’s “María de Buenos Aires” is an intense cocktail of poetry, tango music and dance.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/20/arts/music/maria-de-buenos-aires-by-piazzolla-at-le-poisson-rouge.html', 'snippet': 'Astor Piazzolla’s “María de Buenos Aires” is an intense cocktail of poetry, tango music and dance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was no piano in Astor Piazzolla’s vacation home in Parque del Plata, Uruguay, where he spent the summer of 1968 working with the poet Horacio Ferrer on an operita, a “little opera,” so he composed on the bandoneón. The resulting work, “María de Buenos Aires,” is an intense cocktail of poetry, tango music and dance that is performed by a folk contralto, an operatic baritone and one male actor. But it’s the fourth voice — the bandoneón, the soulful South American accordion — that calls the shots and, to borrow Ferrer’s words, “burns in the back of your throat.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/20/arts/maria/maria-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 361, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/20/arts/maria/maria-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 361}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/20/arts/maria/maria-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 616, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/20/arts/maria/maria-superJumbo.jpg', 'height':