 raising taxes on high incomes. California voters addressed their state’s school budget troubles with a surcharge on big incomes. And now Albany has decided to raise revenue with a high income tax rate for people with seven-figure incomes.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Deal in Albany Would Extend Higher Taxes on Top Earners', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Deal in Albany To Extend Tax On Big Earners', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaplan, Thomas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Tax', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Net Worth Individuals', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-20T00:33:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/233002d2-8d2e-512d-8b86-1a0ac66a0d93', 'word_count': 1300, 'uri': 'nyt://article/233002d2-8d2e-512d-8b86-1a0ac66a0d93'}, {'abstract': 'The Yankees argued that the store was within 1,500 feet of the stadium, violating a state law that bans tickets from being resold that close to a venue.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/19/stubhub-barred-near-yankee-stadium-for-now/', 'snippet': 'The Yankees argued that the store was within 1,500 feet of the stadium, violating a state law that bans tickets from being resold that close to a venue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Yankees won a court order Tuesday temporarily preventing StubHub from opening a store near Yankee Stadium that is designed to allow customers to pick up their tickets before games.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'StubHub Barred Near Yankee Stadium, for Now', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'StubHub Barred Near Yankee Stadium', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'StubHub', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yankee Stadium (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-20T00:39:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Belson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Belson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f191cee1-6b02-51fd-9989-110f967fc330', 'word_count': 394, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f191cee1-6b02-51fd-9989-110f967fc330'}, {'abstract': 'Thomas Lane, 18, was given three life sentences for a 2012 shooting in a school cafeteria that left three students dead and two others injured.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/19/teenage-gunman-in-ohio-mocks-victims-families/', 'snippet': 'Thomas Lane, 18, was given three life sentences for a 2012 shooting in a school cafeteria that left three students dead and two others injured.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thomas Lane wore a T-shirt with KILLER scrawled in black ink during his sentencing Tuesday in an Ohio courtroom for shooting and killing three students in a high school cafeteria and wounding two others last year. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Teenage Gunman in Ohio Mocks Victims’ Families', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'School Shootings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lane, T J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chardon High School (Chardon, Ohio)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-20T00:41:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Preston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Preston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d5ad8263-8dfd-57a9-84e4-d6ae640079b7', 'word_count': 549, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d5ad8263-8dfd-57a9-84e4-d6ae640079b7'}, {'abstract': 'The reverberations of the American-led invasion are still being felt in Iraq, which remains a deeply divided, violence-plagued country that some fear may be headed for a full-blown civil war.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/19/a-decade-later-stability-eludes-iraq/', 'snippet': 'The reverberations of the American-led invasion are still being felt in Iraq, which remains a deeply divided, violence-plagued country that some fear may be headed for a full-blown civil war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first American bombs and missiles to hit Baghdad were four 2,000-pound bunker-busters dropped by two F-117 Nighthawk fighter-bombers and 40 Tomahawk cruise missiles, a thunderous volley aimed at killing Saddam Hussein and his two sons in a secret underground redoubt beside the Tigris River known as Dora Farms. The impacts, some missing their targets, came soon after first light on March 19, 2003. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Decade Later, Stability Eludes Iraq', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'IRAQ', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hussein, Saddam', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maliki, Nuri Kamal al-', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wolfowitz, Paul D', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-20T00:50:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04c62329-47d3-57bc-8a97-7bf68a954e14', 'word_count': 1640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04c62329-47d3-57bc-8a97-7bf68a954e14'}, {'abstract': 'Bank customers will be allowed to stop withdrawals for high-interest payday loans and close their accounts sooner.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2013/03/19/jpmorgan-reining-in-payday-lenders/', 'snippet': 'Bank customers will be allowed to stop withdrawals for high-interest payday loans and close their accounts sooner.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JPMorgan Chase will make changes to protect consumers who have borrowed money from a rising power on the Internet — payday lenders offering short-term loans with interest rates that can exceed 500 percent.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'JPMorgan Chase Is Reining In Payday Lenders', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'JPMorgan Reining In Payday Lenders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-20T00:50:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Silver-Greenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver-Greenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9cd8cd56-a983-5d6f-b7b1-f14f335af303', 'word_count': 1022, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9cd8cd56-a983-5d6f-b7b1-f14f335af303'}, {'abstract': 'What’s “capricious” about the Board of Health doing its job where it could?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/19/banning-the-big-gulp-ban/', 'snippet': 'What’s “capricious” about the Board of Health doing its job where it could?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Neither I nor anyone else I’ve read on last week’s judicial stay of New York City’s “Big Gulp Ban” is a lawyer. (We call it the Big Gulp Ban, though in fact it famously would not have banned 7-Eleven’s massive drinks.) But I doubt that our Board of Health was being “capricious” or “arbitrary” — those were the words of the ruling justice, Milton A. Tingling — when it decided last September, by a vote of 8 to 0 (you read that right), to forbid the sale, where it could, of super-size sugary drinks. (From this point on I’ll just call them “soda.”)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Banning the Big Gulp Ban', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Obesity', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'},