ar. But Which Ones?', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hadley, Stephen J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cheney, Dick', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gates, Robert M', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-20T10:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By David E. Sanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Sanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bccd823-050d-583c-86e6-d52e60e1e98a', 'word_count': 1187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bccd823-050d-583c-86e6-d52e60e1e98a'}, {'abstract': 'The Portuguese government’s policy of merging public schools into large clusters has been a slow-moving disaster.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/20/lowering-the-education-tab-in-portugal-comes-at-a-human-cost/', 'snippet': 'The Portuguese government’s policy of merging public schools into large clusters has been a slow-moving disaster.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LISBON — Education policy isn’t the Portuguese government’s strong suit. There have been 28 education ministers since 1974