, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-20T17:36:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jason Rider', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jason', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rider', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/899a462f-f97d-522f-a50b-e158e53651eb', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/899a462f-f97d-522f-a50b-e158e53651eb'}, {'abstract': 'During Argentina’s “Dirty War,” people thought to be enemies of the state were bundled into Ford Falcons without license plates and disappeared. Would the “war” have been less dirty if the Roman Catholic church had stood up to its perpetrators?', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/20/what-argentine-priests-knew-about-the-dirty-war/', 'snippet': 'During Argentina’s “Dirty War,” people thought to be enemies of the state were bundled into Ford Falcons without license plates and disappeared. Would the “war” have been less dirty if the Roman Catholic church had stood up to its perpetrators?', 'lead_paragraph': ' In 1975, I watched churches in Buenos Aires fill with distraught mothers praying futilely for news of missing sons and daughters. Troubled police officers frequented the confessionals. Most Argentines suspected that President Isabel Perón and her Rasputin, José López Rega, were behind official death squads that made so many people disappear. Priests knew the details.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/20/global-home/ihtrdv-0320-argentina-photo/ihtrdv-0320-argentina-photo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1120, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/20/global-home/ihtrdv-0320-argentina-photo/ihtrdv-0320-argentina-photo-articleLarge.jpg'