'2013-03-20T18:53:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Hartman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ffeeabce-734a-5419-bff7-deaf293c7340', 'word_count': 799, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ffeeabce-734a-5419-bff7-deaf293c7340'}, {'abstract': 'An economists’ affliction.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/20/stimulus-derangement-syndrome/', 'snippet': 'An economists’ affliction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Miles Kimball goes after John Taylor’s latest, and in the process reminds us of an earlier Taylor episode, in which JT argued that low interest rates are actually contractionary — a conclusion he reached by confusing the Fed’s setting of an interest rate target, which is achieved by buying bonds, with a rent-control-type price ceiling enforced by simply banning above-target transactions. It was an amazing thing for a highly credentialed macroeconomist to say. But it wasn’t unique; just offhand I can think of multiple comparable flubs ever since we began monetary and fiscal stimulus to fight the Great Recession.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stimulus Derangement Syndrome', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-20T18:54:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae69bc15-b5d2-591a-88c8-3084e60f8304', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae69bc15-b5d2-591a-88c8-3084e60f8304'}, {'abstract': 'Spotify and Rdio, two popular streaming music apps, are duking it out for the loyalty of streaming music listeners. But Rdio’s beautiful design, recently improved in its mobile app, gives it an edge.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/20/between-rdio-and-spotify-im-with-team-rdio/', 'snippet': 'Spotify and Rdio, two popular streaming music apps, are duking it out for the loyalty of streaming music listeners. But Rdio’s beautiful design, recently improved in its mobile app, gives it an edge.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Talking about Spotify versus Rdio, two competing music streaming services, can be like arguing with someone in a bar over two baseball teams: People passionately love one or the other and will happily share their disdain for the rival. But Spotify often wins with its might and size, touting millions more users than Rdio.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/technology/bits-rdio/bits-rdio-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 592, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/technology/bits-rdio/bits-rdio-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 592, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/technology/bits-rdio/bits-rdio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/19/technology/bits-rdio/bits-rdio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/19/technology/bits-rdio/bits-rdio-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Test Run: Rdio vs. Spotify for Streaming Music', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rdio', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Spotify', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-20T19:05:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/26d6ce84-9c75-5e95-8384-2f5f33d82dcb', 'word_count': 446, 'uri': 'nyt://article/26d6ce84-9c75-5e95-8384-2f5f33d82dcb'}, {'abstract': 'The technology reporters and editors of The New York Times scour the Web for important and peculiar items. For Wednesday, selections include Amazon setting up the Kindle as a place to store online articles for reading later, how the explosion of cellphones has put drone technology into the public domain and within the budgets of hobbyists, and Apple’s great “I’m a Mac and I’m a PC’ ads collected on YouTube.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/20/todays-scuttlebot-amazon-c-i-a-deal-and-googling-for-gifs/', 'snippet': 'The technology reporters and editors of The New York Times scour the Web for important and peculiar items. For Wednesday, selections include Amazon setting up the Kindle as a place to store online articles for reading later, how the explosion of c...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here are some of the more interesting items that the tech reporters and editors of The New York Times found on the Web recently. More Scuttlebot can be found here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'r