nwich Village.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/21/garden/antik-reopens-as-hostler-burrows.html', 'snippet': 'Antik, which sold antiques in TriBeCa, reopens as Hostler Burrows in Greenwich Village.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Antik, which sold Scandinavian antiques in TriBeCa until May, has re-emerged in Greenwich Village as Hostler Burrows. When the shop opened in the late ’90s, “people knew Hans Wegner, Georg Jensen, Jens Risom — three Danish designers,” said Kim Hostler, an owner with Juliet Burrows. The women have since introduced other 20th-century Scandinavians. Shown at top, the work of five Swedish designers: 1940s blue-gray chairs by Otto Schulz ($14,000 a pair), 1950s ivory chairs by Kerstin Horlin-Holmqvist ($12,500 a pair) and a table by Axel Einar Hjorth ($16,000). Above, 1950s porcelain vases by Berndt Friberg (from $2,200 each) and a 1939 rug by Sigvard Bernadotte ($28,000): (212) 343-0471, hostlerburrows.com. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/21/garden/21OPEN_SPAN/21OPEN_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 407, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/21/garden/21OPEN_SPAN/21OPEN_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 407}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/21/garden/21OPEN_SPAN/21OPEN_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 694, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/21/garden/21OPEN_SPAN/21OPEN_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1388, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/21/garden/21OPEN_SPAN/21OPEN_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/21/garden/21OPEN_SPAN/21OPEN_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/21/garden/21OPEN_SPAN/21OPEN_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pushing the Boundaries of Scandinavian Design', 'kicker': 'Currents | Open', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pushing the Boundaries\r\nOf Scandinavian Design', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Louie, Elaine', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIQUES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hostler Burrows (Manhattan, NY, Retail Store)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Antik (Manhattan, NY, Retailer)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-20T22:36:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Louie', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Louie', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef93002e-8c35-54fd-809b-f5d8998f30f9', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef93002e-8c35-54fd-809b-f5d8998f30f9'}, {'abstract': 'Gregg Klewicki, a woodworker on Long Island, carves bears, gulls and other objects from stumps, many of them from trees brought down by Hurricane Sandy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/21/garden/from-tree-stump-to-sculpture.html', 'snippet': 'Gregg Klewicki, a woodworker on Long Island, carves bears, gulls and other objects from stumps, many of them from trees brought down by Hurricane Sandy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most of the thousands of tree limbs and stumps left by Hurricane Sandy were sent to composting facilities and trash heaps. But some homeowners have embraced the wreckage by turning their downed trees into sculpture. Gregg Klewicki, a 45-year-old woodcarver in Huntington, N.Y., began his craft when he was 20. His first project, he said, was a giant nose that he placed on his front lawn. Within a few weeks, someone knocked on his door willing to pay $300 for it, so he started carving on the weekends. A few years later, Mr. Klewicki had enough commissions to quit his day job driving a beer truck. Last week, he laid down his chain saw long enough to talk to a reporter.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/21/garden/21QNA1_SPAN/21QNA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/21/garden/21QNA1_SPAN/21QNA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/21/garden/21QNA1_SPAN/21QNA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/21/garden/21QNA1_SPAN/21QNA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/21/garden/21QNA1_SPAN/21QNA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail':