inal': 'By Harvey Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6dad50c-a52b-5a8c-9e0c-d55b8c5f0bc3', 'word_count': 664, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6dad50c-a52b-5a8c-9e0c-d55b8c5f0bc3'}, {'abstract': 'Brandon Friedman served in Iraq and Afghanistan as an infantry officer with the 101st Airborne Division. But when people ask him about the war in Iraq now, he feels like he’s going through the motions, reciting lines long memorized–almost as if he’s telling someone else’s tale instead of his own.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/21/the-end-of-war-stories/', 'snippet': 'Brandon Friedman served in Iraq and Afghanistan as an infantry officer with the 101st Airborne Division. But when people ask him about the war in Iraq now, he feels like he’s going through the motions, reciting lines long memorized–almost as if he...', 'lead_paragraph': 'I can’t remember the last time I told a real war story — the kind you recount with waving arms and an animated voice, impersonating friends you haven’t talked to in years. The kind you really get into.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The End of War Stories', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Loyd, Anthony', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-21T13:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brandon Friedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brandon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Friedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d64de64-4b93-51ef-9b28-2875db203631', 'word_count': 734, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d64de64-4b93-51ef-9b28-2875db203631'}, {'abstract': 'The Most Rev. Justin Welby, the new archbishop, spoke in sympathetic terms of same-sex relationships, but reaffirmed his commitment to opposing gay weddings in church.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/22/world/europe/new-archbishop-of-canterbury-to-be-enthroned.html', 'snippet': 'The Most Rev. Justin Welby, the new archbishop, spoke in sympathetic terms of same-sex relationships, but reaffirmed his commitment to opposing gay weddings in church.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — At his installation as archbishop of Canterbury on Thursday, Justin Welby, a former oil executive who made an unusually rapid rise to the leadership of the Anglican Church, used the ceremony in Canterbury’s nine-century-old cathedral to set themes of simplicity, modesty and innovation that echoed the tone Pope Francis has set for his week-old papacy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/world/0322BRITAINsub/0322BRITAINsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/22/world/0322BRITAINsub/0322BRITAINsub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 395}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/world/0322BRITAINsub/0322BRITAINsub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/world/0322BRITAINsub/0322BRITAINsub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1347, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/world/0322BRITAINsub/0322BRITAINsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/22/world/0322BRITAINsub/0322BRITAINsub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/world/0322BRITAINsub/0322BRITAINsub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Setting Themes of Humility, a New Archbishop of Canterbury Is Installed ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Setting Themes of Humility, a New Archbishop of Canterbury Is Installed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Welby, Justin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burns, John F', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Church of England', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANGLICAN CHURCHES', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-21T13:09:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By John F. Burns and Alan Cowell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Burns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2e1032c-ca3b-5613-8a5e-e155dab3f81d', 'word_count': 1045, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2e1032c-ca3b-5613-8a5e-e155dab3f81d'}, {'abstract': 'NBC is planning for Jimmy Fallon to take over the “Tonight” show from Jay Leno, a new campaign seeks to diminish the digital divide in the United States, and the new Big East conference kicked off at News Corporation’s headquarters.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/21/the-breakfast-meeting-fallon-to-become-host-of-tonight-and-ads-aim-to-close-digital-divide/', 'snippet': 'NBC is planning for Jimmy Fallon to take over the “Tonight” show from Jay Leno, a new campaign seeks to diminish the digital divide in the United States, and the new Big East conference kicked off at News Corporation’s headquarters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NBC has made a commitment to make Jimmy Fallon, the 38-year-old host of its “Late Night” show, the host of the “Tonight” show and to move the show from Burbank, Calif., back to New York, Bill Carter reports. Mr. Fallon would succeed Jay Leno, who will turn 63 next month and whose show still regularly leads the late-night ratings. The change would by fall 2014 at the latest. NBC has been desperate to avoid acrimony that often surrounds one of the biggest changes in late-night television; their attempt to replace Mr. Leno with Conan O’Brien three years ago ended with recriminations and a reversal, as Mr. Leno was reinstated and NBC endured weeks of negative coverage.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Fallon to Become Host of ‘Tonight’ and Ads Aim to Close Digital Divide', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-21T13:11:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel E. Slotnik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Slotnik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/20a2cc1c-a150-5081-985a-095403143469', 'word_count': 622, 'uri': 'nyt://article/20a2cc1c-a150-5081-985a-095403143469'}, {'abstract': 'Publication of the report on government data requests places Microsoft alongside Google, Twitter and other Web businesses in releasing regular transparency reports.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/22/technology/microsoft-releases-report-on-law-enforcement-requests.html', 'snippet': 'Publication of the report on government data requests places Microsoft alongside Google, Twitter and other Web businesses in releasing regular transparency reports.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Microsoft disclosed for the first time on Thursday the number of requests it had received from government law enforcement agencies for data on its hundreds of millions of customers around the world, joining the ranks of Google, Twitter and other Web businesses that publish so-called transparency reports.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Microsoft Releases Report on Law Enforcement Requests', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Microsoft Releases Report on Law Enforcement Requests', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-21T13:18:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': "By Kevin J. O'Brien", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kevin', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': "O'Brien", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72098064-3a0f-5536-947f-949333ef746a', 'word_count': 935, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72098064-3a0f-5536-947f-949333ef746a'}, {'abstract': '“House of Cards”, this time with no Zoe Barnes, no Slugline, no journalism. Ashley Parker loved the episode and David Carr gave to to thumbs “meh.”', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/21/house-of-cards-recap-episode-8-you-can-go-home-again-it-just-might-get-complicated/', 'snippet': '“House of Cards”, this time with no Zoe Barnes, no Slugline, no journalism. Ashley Parker loved the episode and David Carr gave to to thumbs “meh.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Deep into “House of Cards” and wondering what it all means? You are not the only one. Ashley Parker and David Carr read between the lines of episode eight in search of a deeper message. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘House of Cards’ Recap, Episode 8: You Can Go Home Again, It Just Might Get Complicated', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosin, Hanna', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Virginia Military Institute', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-21T13:22:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Carr and Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2865c45-9125-5d03-9d4f-40c6bcc08ab3', 'word_count': 970, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2865c45-9125-5d03-9d4f-40c6bcc08ab3'}, {'abstract': 'Analysts of global environmental trends seek to topple the three pillars of sustainable development.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/21/scientists-propose-a-new-architecture-for-sustainable-development/', 'snippet': 'Analysts of global environmental trends seek to topple the three pillars of sustainable development.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a United Nations working group negotiates a set of “sustainable development goals,” 10 scientists and development analysts, in a commentary published today in Nature, have proposed a fundamentally different way to frame this concept. (Click here for relevant Dot Earth posts.)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/21/blogs/dotsdg/dotsdg-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/21/blogs/dotsdg/dotsdg-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 598}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/21/blogs/dotsdg/dotsdg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1021, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/21/b