e, tempestuous marriage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/22/books/the-force-of-things-by-alexander-stille.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Force of Things: A Marriage in War and Peace,” the journalist Alexander Stille tells the story of his parents’ passionate, tempestuous marriage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Elizabeth and Misha, Alexander Stille’s mother and father, met in May 1948 at a party for Truman Capote. She came to the party with her husband at the time, Bob, and left with Misha. Within a week, she’d moved out of the apartment she’d shared with Bob, and begun an intense romance with Misha. The pair would get married and stay married for more than four decades, but the marriage would be a fiercely tempestuous one, fueled by epic fights and threats of divorce — a marriage that their son describes as a toxic clash of civilizations. When he was a teenager, he says, it reminded him of Sartre’s line “Hell is other people.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22BOOKSTILLE/JPBOOKSTILLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 990, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22BOOKSTILLE/JPBOOKSTILLE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 990}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22BOOKSTILLE/JPBOOKSTILLE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 621, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22BOOKSTILLE/JPBOOKSTILLE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2004, 'width': 1214, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22BOOKSTILLE/JPBOOKSTILLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22BOOKSTILLE/JPBOOKSTILLE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22BOOKSTILLE/JPBOOKSTILLE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mom and Dad, Midwest and Milan', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mom and Dad, Midwest and Milan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stille, Alexander', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-21T20:12:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8887232-d48b-55eb-a900-ef1707e3bfef', 'word_count': 1239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8887232-d48b-55eb-a900-ef1707e3bfef'}, {'abstract': 'China, with $2.7 billion in ticket sales last year, passed Japan to become the second-biggest market for films, according to industry statistics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/22/business/media/global-ticket-sales-for-movies-rise.html', 'snippet': 'China, with $2.7 billion in ticket sales last year, passed Japan to become the second-biggest market for films, according to industry statistics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Movie ticket sales around the world rose 6 percent last year, to $34.7 billion from $32.6 billion in 2011, as China became the world’s second-biggest market for theatrical films after the United States, according to statistics released by the Motion Picture Association of America on Thursday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Global Ticket Sales for Movies Rise 6%', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Global Ticket Sales for Movies Rise 6%', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Motion Picture Assn of America', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-21T20:15:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/316ff084-c3a2-504e-b6c0-bf10c7d5417e', 'word_count': 454, 'uri': 'nyt://article/316ff084-c3a2-504e-b6c0-bf10c7d5417e'}, {'abstract': 'Even the recent reduction in carbon-dioxide emissions in the United States is not nearly sharp enough to avoid reaching what climate scientists consider the prudent limit in global warming within decades.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/21/the-hard-math-on-fossil-fuels/', 'snippet': 'Even the recent reduction in carbon-dioxide emissions in the United States is not nearly sharp enough to avoid reaching what climate scientists consider the prudent limit in global warming within decades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How close is the world to devastating climate upheaval? My column on Wednesday argued that the natural gas boom is helping the United States reduce its emissions of heat-trapping carbon into the air, encouraging power generators to switch to gas from the much dirtier coal. American CO2 emissions last year totaled about 5.25 billion tons — almost 13 percent less than in 2007.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Hard Math on Fossil Fuels', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Temperature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Energy Agency', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-21T20:16:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eduardo Porter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eduardo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Porter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/db545681-6767-5fc2-a25b-388e70155622', 'word_count': 429, 'uri': 'nyt://article/db545681-6767-5fc2-a25b-388e70155622'}, {'abstract': 'An early Pollock and an André Derain portrait are among the most notable works coming to auction. The Metropolitan Museum of Art will open on Mondays starting July 1.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/22/arts/design/pollock-and-andre-derain-at-auction-met-expands-hours.html', 'snippet': 'An early Pollock and an André Derain portrait are among the most notable works coming to auction. The Metropolitan Museum of Art will open on Mondays starting July 1.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With deadlines for the spring auctions fast approaching, experts at Sotheby’s and Christie’s are scrambling to get top material for their sales. It’s been a tough couple of months. No major estates are up for grabs, and most collectors are reluctant to sell a work unless they need cash. Slowly, however, a few significant works are beginning to come on the market for the sales, among them “The Blue Unconscious,” an early abstract canvas that Jackson Pollock painted in 1946.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22VOGEL2/VOGEL2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 910, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22VOGEL2/VOGEL2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 910}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22VOGEL2/VOGEL2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 675, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22VOGEL2/VOGEL2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1552, 'width': 1023, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22VOGEL2/22VOGEL2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22VOGEL2/22VOGEL2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22VOGEL2/22VOGEL2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pollock and André Derain at Auction; Met Expands Hours', 'kicker': 'Inside Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vogel, Carol', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-21T20:18:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd767876-4f78-5149-be3c-c32c5470eacf', 'word_count': 947, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd767876-4f78-5149-be3c-c32c5470eacf'}, {'abstract': 'Jeff Gorton, the Rangers’ assistant general manager, will fill in for Glen Sather as the April 3 N.H.L. trade deadline approaches.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/21/sather-has-surgery-to-treat-prostate-cancer/', 'snippet': 'Jeff Gorton, the Rangers’ assistant general manager, will fill in for Glen Sather as the April 3 N.H.L. trade deadline approaches.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Glen Sather, the Rangers’ president and general manager, had surgery to treat prostate cancer on Thursday at Weill-Cornell Medical Center in New York and was expected to be off the job for a few days.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sather Has Surgery to Treat Prostate Cancer', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sather Has Operation for Cancer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sather, Glen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Rangers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-21T20:25:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/debafb8f-6ed7-5496-ac3a-20856e58fc5e', 'word_count': 431, 'uri': 'nyt://article/debafb8f-6ed7-5496-ac3a-20856e58fc5e'}, {'abstract': 'A forgotten corner of Paris’s Ninth Arrondissement is now home to one of the best big-city boutique hotels on the Continent.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/21/now-booking-hotel-de-nell-in-paris/', 'snippet': 'A forgotten corner of Paris’s Ninth Arrondissement is now home to one of the best big-city boutique hotels on the Continent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Paris, the interior designer Jean-Michel Wilmotte and the hotelier Lisa Konckier-Abrat have created an elegant and supremely handsome 33-room hotel that’s one of the best examples of big-city boutique lodgings on the Continent. Think luxe, calme et volupté.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Booking | Hotel de Nell in Paris', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Its Own Bonbon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hotels and Travel Lodgings', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-21T20:34:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexander Lobrano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexander', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lobrano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbbe9ffa-80f2-59fd-89a7-6909fb60bcc8', 'word_count': 299, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbbe9ffa-80f2-59fd-89a7-6909fb60bcc8'}, {'abstract': 'Emilia Clarke in “Breakfast at Tiffany’s” is the latest example of a screen actress struggling to adapt to the stage.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/21/theater-talkback-the-season-of-the-sputtering-starlet/', 'snippet': 'Emilia Clarke in “Breakfast at Tiffany’s” is the latest example of a screen actress struggling to adapt to the stage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The geese who fly east from Hollywood on an annual basis, looking for a little Broadway luster to boost their showbiz stock, aren’t laying as many golden eggs as they normally do this season. As Patrick Healy noted recently, the box office at the Richard Rodgers Theater, where Scarlett Johansson is starring in a revival of “Cat on a Hot Tin Roof,” has not exactly been inundated since the show opened to middling reviews in January. Nor did Katie Holmes’s ballyhooed return to Broadway after a tumultuous few years as constant tabloid fodder do much for “Dead Accounts,” the Theresa Rebeck play that closed quickly in the fall after opening to sour reviews and sluggish ticket sales.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Theater Talkback: The Season of the Sputtering Starlet', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Breakfast at Tiffany's (Play)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 