T01:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': "By Jake Siegel and Perry O'Brien", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Siegel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Perry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Brien", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97f56382-e037-50f3-ab4a-a612c15d26da', 'word_count': 1008, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97f56382-e037-50f3-ab4a-a612c15d26da'}, {'abstract': 'There’s nothing wrong with asking businesses to pay for a small part of the problem they helped create.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/21/burgers-fries-and-lies/', 'snippet': 'There’s nothing wrong with asking businesses to pay for a small part of the problem they helped create.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The old adage — what will not kill you makes you only stronger — certainly doesn’t apply to a bacon cheeseburger from Five Guys Burgers and Fries. This tasty gut bomb delivers two days’ worth of saturated fat and nearly 1,000 calories, a la carte. I hacked my way through one of these heart-stoppers the other day, and though I still have a pulse, it stayed with me in ways that would not please any doctor. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Burgers, Fries and Lies', 'kicker': 'Timothy Egan', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fast Food Industry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Heritage Foundation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T01:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion',