ajor': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T01:10:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f50ecbdb-c0a3-526b-be30-4386c6ac32a7', 'word_count': 445, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f50ecbdb-c0a3-526b-be30-4386c6ac32a7'}, {'abstract': 'In “Love and Honor,” a drama set in the Vietnam War era, two soldiers go AWOL to win the hearts of teenage girls.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/22/movies/love-and-honor-directed-by-danny-mooney.html', 'snippet': 'In “Love and Honor,” a drama set in the Vietnam War era, two soldiers go AWOL to win the hearts of teenage girls.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Danny Mooney’s “Love and Honor” is for anyone who thought “Taking Woodstock” was too hard-core. Set during the Vietnam War, this trifle about two soldiers who sneak away to America while on leave unfolds in a world about as realistic as a flashback on a sitcom. The film dresses up pretty young things in fatigues and retro T-shirts for a story so clichéd and brainless that it’s almost more disturbing than laughable.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22RDP_LOVE_SPAN/22RDP_LOVE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 280, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22RDP_LOVE_SPAN/22RDP_LOVE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 280}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/arts/22RDP_LOVE_SPAN/22RDP_LOVE_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 478, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 't