-22T12:46:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sabrina Tavernise', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sabrina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tavernise', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7c6c583-f9b7-562b-9b02-f4210e3dc41e', 'word_count': 910, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7c6c583-f9b7-562b-9b02-f4210e3dc41e'}, {'abstract': 'Winners, losers, and complexities in a world of steadily-later marriage.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/22/late-marriage-and-its-consequences/', 'snippet': 'Winners, losers, and complexities in a world of steadily-later marriage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Wednesday I had the privilege of joining a Brookings Institution event\xa0organized around the new report “Knot Yet: The Benefits and Costs of Delayed Marriage,” which tries to tease out the social implications of the steadily rising age of first marriage\xa0— now at 27 for women and 29 for men, both historic highs. Not surprisingly, a lot of the discussion focused on the impact of this trend on children, because the story of late marriage is entangled with the story of rising out-of-wedlock births, thanks to what the authors call “the great crossover”\xa0— the fact that the age of first marriage,\xa0which was once about a year earlier than the average age at which the first child was born, now lags the average age of first birth by about a year. Hence the report’s most attention-grabbing statistic:\xa0That 48 percent of overall first births, and 58 percent of first births to what the report calls “Middle Americans”\xa0— women with a high school diploma and maybe some college, but no 4-year degree\xa0— now take place outside of marriage,\xa0a trend\xa0whose negative consequences for children\xa0probably\xa0don’t need to be rehearsed here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Late Marriage and Its Consequences', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T12:48:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60365dd3-e3f6-54c4-a5c5-b7b0ed8237e6', 'word_count': 1261, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60365dd3-e3f6-54c4-a5c5-b7b0ed8237e6'}, {'abstract': 'The Web site, called NuevoWorld, was created by Luis Balaguer, Ms. Vergara’s business partner and the co-founder of Latin World Entertainment.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/22/site-brings-new-way-to-connect-with-vergara-and-other-latino-stars/', 'snippet': 'The Web site, called NuevoWorld, was created by Luis Balaguer, Ms. Vergara’s business partner and the co-founder of Latin World Entertainment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sofia Vergara’s 4.2 million Twitter followers have seen the actress post a consistent stream of photos of her family, her friends and her Modern Family co-stars. Soon, many of those photos and posts will take her Twitter fans to a new Web site called NuevoWorld, which will make its debut on Friday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Site Brings New Way to Connect With Vergara and Other Latino Stars', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hispanic-Americans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Online Advertising', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'pers