own as Typhoid Mary.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mary Beth Keane’s first novel, “The Walking People,” could stand as a historical sequel to her new novel, “Fever.” The earlier book is the story of two Irish sisters who immigrate to New York in the early 1960s, accompanied by a young man who belongs to the “walking people,” also known as the “traveling people” or tinkers. That novel is less about the anomalous position these itinerant peddlers occupy in Irish society than it is about the consequences of concealing from a child the circumstances of her birth. At the same time it’s a long, fond portrait of a family.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MCGRATH/24MCGRATH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 562, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MCGRATH/24MCGRATH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 562}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MCGRATH/24MCGRATH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 959, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MCGRATH/24MCGRATH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1917, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MCGRATH/24MCGRATH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MCGRATH/24MCGRATH-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MCGRATH/24MCGRATH-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Things She Carried', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Things She Carried', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Fever (Book)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keane, Mary Beth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T14:58:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8fb556c-4ace-590e-a2b3-8de9cf9e0e16', 'word_count': 896, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8fb556c-4ace-590e-a2b3-8de9cf9e0e16'}, {'abstract': 'Sexual transgression is only one means to an end for the characters in Amber Dermont’s stories.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/damage-control-by-amber-dermont.html', 'snippet': 'Sexual transgression is only one means to an end for the characters in Amber Dermont’s stories.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The other day a headline, “Female Prison Guard Pregnant by Cop Killer Inmate,” came pulsing across the electronic ticker of a morning news show. Paging T. Coraghessan Boyle! I thought. The outsize combination of tragedy and brinkmanship, of hope and human folly, was straight out of a Boyle story. Several books in, we know exactly what we mean by a T. C. Boyle story — or a William Trevor story or an Antonya Nelson story. Indeed our pleasure is heightened by knowing the lay of the land because we can read for the new: how this particular Alice Munro story differs from what we’ve come to expect from an Alice Munro story.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MACY/24MACY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 562, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MACY/24MACY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 562}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MACY/24MACY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 958, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MACY/24MACY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1867, 'width': 1995, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MACY/24MACY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MACY/24MACY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24MACY/24MACY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I’ve Been Meaning to Tell You', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I’ve Been Meaning to Tell You', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Macy, Caitlin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dermont, Amber', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T14:58:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caitlin Macy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caitlin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3f51b7b-466a-53b4-a54f-c2010da8eaee', 'word_count': 912, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3f51b7b-466a-53b4-a54f-c2010da8eaee'}, {'abstract': 'How to teach literature? With more attention to visceral reactions, and a lot less to theory and historical background.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/straight-through-the-heart.html', 'snippet': 'How to teach literature? With more attention to visceral reactions, and a lot less to theory and historical background.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As each semester begins at Grinnell College, a small liberal arts school nestled in the Iowa prairie, I get numerous e-mails from students pleading for a spot in my fiction workshop. The wait list is long, and as much as I’d love to take credit for the course’s popularity, I’m learning it’s less about the teacher and more about the way fiction writers approach the teaching of literature.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1063, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1063}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 578, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1156, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB/0324-Bakopoulos-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Straight Through the Heart', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Straight Through the Heart', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dean Bakopoulos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bakopoulos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9559b539-6301-536a-8ffc-c7daf1de95ac', 'word_count': 1283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9559b539-6301-536a-8ffc-c7daf1de95ac'}, {'abstract': 'No single issue of the Book Review could contain every one of this season’s significant newcomers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'No single issue of the Book Review could contain every one of this season’s significant newcomers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This winter, as we made our way through the usual stacks of upcoming titles on our shelves, we noticed an interesting trend: Many of the books that held our attention were written by first- or second-time authors who were relatively or entirely new to the scene. Apart from the unfamiliarity of the names on the title pages, not much united these works. There were novels, story collections, memoirs of food and art and unimaginable suffering. Their authors represented a range of backgrounds and sensibilities. And “new” didn’t necessarily mean “young” — youth (or age, for that matter) being no indicator of talent. But all the voices were fresh, and all made us sit up a bit straighter before settling in for the journey. Call it an accident of timing, but publishing’s spring crop offers a tangible rebuke to the lament that books are endangered, and suggests that literature’s future is in good hands.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Upfront/0324-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 565, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Upfront/0324-Upfront-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 565}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Upfront/0324-Upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 964, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Upfront/0324-Upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1527, 'width': 1622, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Upfront/0324-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Upfront/0324-Upfront-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Upfront/0324-Upfront-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:00:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2638a30f-c684-54a5-8ec1-dc18580f8205', 'word_count': 234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2638a30f-c684-54a5-8ec1-dc18580f8205'}, {'abstract': 'Prime Minister Shinzo Abe said he would ask Okinawan officials for a key permit to begin construction to relocate an unpopular American air base.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/23/world/asia/japans-leader-shinzo-abe-tries-to-restart-plan-to-move-okinawan-base.html', 'snippet': 'Prime Minister Shinzo Abe said he would ask Okinawan officials for a key permit to begin construction to relocate an unpopular American air base.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Seeking to remove a longstanding irritant in Japan’s ties with the United States, Prime Minister Shinzo Abe said on Friday that his government would ask local officials on the island of Okinawa for a key permit to begin construction to relocate an unpopular American air base to another part of the island.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan Leader Backs Move of U.S. Base on Okinawa', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan Leader Backs Move Of U.S. Base On Okinawa', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABE, SHINZO', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Military Bases and Installations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Okinawa and Other Ryukyu Islands (Japan)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:01:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c7cc1e86-4b68-5c0b-8c49-ad6a8ae0293c', 'word_count': 691, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c7cc1e86-4b68-5c0b-8c49-ad6a8ae0293c'}, {'abstract': 'Sheryl Sandberg’s “Lean In,” No. 1 on the hardcover nonfiction list, has renewed longstanding debates about the paucity of female executives and the best way to remedy it.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/31/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Sheryl Sandberg’s “Lean In,” No. 1 on the hardcover nonfiction list, has renewed longstanding debates about the paucity of female executives and the best way to remedy it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A BOARDROOM OF ONE’S OWN: “Lean In,” Sheryl Sandberg’s polarizing manifesto about the role of women in the workplace, roars onto the hardcover nonfiction list this week at No. 1. Sandberg is the chief operating officer of Facebook, and her book has renewed longstanding debates about the paucity of female executives and the best way to remedy it. (Sandberg’s answer, boiled down, is: “Work harder, girls!”) One sign that the book has touched a nerve is that, along with a steady stream of praise, it is also fielding criticism from across the ideological spectrum. On the one hand, in the “biology is destiny” camp, Christina Hoff Sommers recently charged in The Atlantic that Sandberg was “mired in 1970s-style feminism” with her earnest calls for “consciousness-raising groups” and her desire to “liberate her fellow Americans from the stereotypes of gender.” What if those stereotypes are innate, Sommers wondered, and women just aren’t interested in high-powered careers? “In the pursuit of happiness,” she wrote, “men and women often take different paths.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/books/review/22insidelist/22insidelist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 462, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/books/review/22insidelist/22insidelist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 462, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/books/review/22insidelist/22insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/22/books/review/22insidelist/22insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/22/books/review/22insidelist/22insidelist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:01:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b32eb70a-58b4-5fad-a2f3-dc6bcc4c7181', 'word_count': 603, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b32eb70a-58b4-5fad-a2f3-dc6bcc4c7181'}, {'abstract': 'Zombies, unicorns, shrink rays — an ordinary world is beset by unusual phenomena in Manuel Gonzales’s story collection.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/the-miniature-wife-by-manuel-gonzales.html', 'snippet': 'Zombies, unicorns, shrink rays — an ordinary world is beset by unusual phenomena in Manuel Gonzales’s story collection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the middle of Manuel Gonzales’s lucid and confident first collection of stories, “The Miniature Wife,” he tells a tale from the point of view of a half-\xadhuman, half-zombie with conflicting \xadurges. It’s one of the strongest (and funniest) in the collection, no small feat in our zombie-saturated cultural landscape. It also, in its way, sums up the central struggle of many of Gonzales’s characters: When does one just sit by, and when do darker impulses bubble up and take over? When do we stand aside, and when do we act? These stories vary widely — an elegiac description of a plane circling Dallas for decades, a futuristic wake mourning the loss of Africa into the ocean, social science “reportage” about false anthropological claims — but they work best when the characters, for whatever reasons, succumb to a buried darkness. Inner zombie rises up. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENDER/24BENDER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 894, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENDER/24BENDER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 894}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENDER/24BENDER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 688, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENDER/24BENDER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1375, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENDER/24BENDER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENDER/24BENDER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENDER/24BENDER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Varieties of Disturbance', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Varieties of Disturbance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gonzales, Manuel (1974- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:02:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Aimee Bender', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Aimee', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bender', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4650a39b-083e-5cfa-a913-826792579c88', 'word_count': 1004, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4650a39b-083e-5cfa-a913-826792579c88'}, {'abstract': 'Sonali Deraniyagala says of her family members killed in the 2004 tsunami: “They were my world. How do I make them dead?”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/wave-by-sonali-deraniyagala.html', 'snippet': 'Sonali Deraniyagala says of her family members killed in the 2004 tsunami: “They were my world. How do I make them dead?”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sonali Deraniyagala’s extraordinary memoir, “Wave,” opens on the morning of Dec. 26, 2004, as the author putters around a Sri Lankan beach-side hotel with her family. By chapter’s end she’s pantless, half-drowned, bleeding, bruised and numbly resistant to what she’ll soon be forced to know: The five people she loves most in the world are dead. Her two young sons. Her husband. Her parents. All of them killed by a force she can’t yet comprehend, though she was caught up in it and nearly killed by it too. She only knows that “something came for us.” It was, as she and the world will soon learn, a tsunami of epic scale that took an estimated 230,000 lives across a dozen countries.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24STRAYED/24STRAYED-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 703, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24STRAYED/24STRAYED-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 703}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24STRAYED/24STRAYED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 875, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24STRAYED/24STRAYED-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1713, 'width': 1463, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24STRAYED/24STRAYED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24STRAYED/24STRAYED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24STRAYED/24STRAYED-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Washed Away', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Washed Away', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strayed, Cheryl', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Deraniyagala, Sonali', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:03:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cheryl Strayed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cheryl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strayed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d270d5fb-6264-550f-a7e8-054bebed8d09', 'word_count': 1269, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d270d5fb-6264-550f-a7e8-054bebed8d09'}, {'abstract': '“Swing This,” Debby Boone’s show at Café Carlyle, featured songs from the Rat Pack era.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/22/arts/music/debby-boone-at-the-cafe-carlyle.html', 'snippet': '“Swing This,” Debby Boone’s show at Café Carlyle, featured songs from the Rat Pack era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'She was a just toddler when her father, Pat Boone, was a Las Vegas headliner who hobnobbed with the Rat Pack. But the singer Debby Boone had her eyes and ears wide open, and she remembers things like the look of Sammy Davis Jr.’s house and Dean Martin’s jokes about her father’s teetotaling. And on Tuesday evening at Café Carlyle Ms. Boone, glamorized to the max, was the very picture of a tastefully attired Las Vegas babe as she devoted an entire program to songs of the Rat Pack era in a show titled “Swing This.” The swing was provided by an excellent nine-member band under the direction of John Oddo, the longtime musical director of Ms. Boone’s mother-in-law, Rosemary Clooney. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Swinging Back to the Heyday of the Rat Pack', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Swinging Back to the Heyday of the Rat Pack ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cafe Carlyle', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boone, Debby', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:04:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d41c0e2-495d-52ff-8000-99b6ac80c0f1', 'word_count': 298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d41c0e2-495d-52ff-8000-99b6ac80c0f1'}, {'abstract': 'Leon Botstein and the American Symphony Orchestra, which specializes in obscure repertory, brought Marschner’s seldom-performed “Vampire” to Carnegie Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/22/arts/music/american-symphony-orchestra-performs-marschners-vampire.html', 'snippet': 'Leon Botstein and the American Symphony Orchestra, which specializes in obscure repertory, brought Marschner’s seldom-performed “Vampire” to Carnegie Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Witches are an operatic staple, but vampire protagonists rarely appear in productions today. Heinrich Marschner’s “Vampire” is one of the several early-19th-century vampire-theme works that are now seldom performed. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '186-Year-Old Vampire Haunts Carnegie Hall', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '186-Year-Old Vampire Haunts Carnegie Hall', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN SYMPHONY ORCHESTRA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Botstein, Leon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:04:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a112a9e1-3fea-504a-9857-1ac8a049df30', 'word_count': 448, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a112a9e1-3fea-504a-9857-1ac8a049df30'}, {'abstract': '“House of Cards”: Recapping episode 9. Revolt is in the air: Frank Underwood’s carefully crafted bill to prop up Peter Russo’s candidacy comes undone and it’s an inside job. The alliance of convenience between Frankand Zoe Barnes becomes an intimate battle for power.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/22/house-of-cards-no-9-alliances-crumbling-on-all-sides/', 'snippet': '“House of Cards”: Recapping episode 9. Revolt is in the air: Frank Underwood’s carefully crafted bill to prop up Peter Russo’s candidacy comes undone and it’s an inside job. The alliance of convenience between Frankand Zoe Barnes becomes an intima...', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you have loyally stayed with us to this point, you know the drill. Ashley Parker and David Carr put the old gimlet eye on “House of Cards” with an emphasis on the optics and verisimilitude of the show’s media and political angles. And if you are just arriving, you can catch up with recaps of episodes one, two,three, four, five, six,seven or eight. Lots of spoilers, so proceed with caution. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '“House of Cards” Recap, Episode 9: Alliances Crumbling on All Sides', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Kevin (1965- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:04:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Carr and Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carr', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9eefd670-d194-54f7-9279-52c5d81ad0b5', 'word_count': 1062, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9eefd670-d194-54f7-9279-52c5d81ad0b5'}, {'abstract': 'It has become easier than ever to take and share snapshots, for better or for worse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/fashion/weddings/start-ups-vie-for-a-slice-of-the-wedding-photo-cake.html', 'snippet': 'It has become easier than ever to take and share snapshots, for better or for worse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To promote photo sharing at their wedding in Manchester, N.H., last month, Laura Bishop and Walter Carroll placed postcards on the tables with instructions for their guests on how to download an application called WedPics. After the reception, the couple immediately signed onto WedPics and found some 200 photographs. ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/fashion/weddings/24fieldnotes/24fieldnotes-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 487, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/fashion/weddings/24fieldnotes/24fieldnotes-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 487}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/fashion/weddings/24fieldnotes/24fieldnotes-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 831, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/fashion/weddings/24fieldnotes/24fieldnotes-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1423, 'width': 1753, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/fashion/weddings/24fieldnotes/24fieldnotes-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/fashion/weddings/24fieldnotes/24fieldnotes-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/fashion/weddings/24fieldnotes/24fieldnotes-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Vying for a Shot of the Wedding', 'kicker': 'Field Notes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vying for a Shot of the Wedding', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:04:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Schultz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schultz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b2ca6b5-8ccb-5726-8d8b-33bf54f469ec', 'word_count': 804, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b2ca6b5-8ccb-5726-8d8b-33bf54f469ec'}, {'abstract': 'New technology brings more entrants to the field, often at much lower prices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/fashion/weddings/for-wedding-photography-competition-from-amateurs.html', 'snippet': 'New technology brings more entrants to the field, often at much lower prices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For professional photographers, advancements in technology pose an interesting predicament. Camera innovations have enabled them to take better photos, but it’s lowered the barrier to entry by competitors.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Photographers, Competition Gets Fierce', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'For Photographers, Competition Gets Fierce', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:07:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Society', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': 'Weddings', 'byline': {'original': 'By Caren Chesler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Caren', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chesler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9264c594-648a-5c79-84a0-086fe6fcdd4e', 'word_count': 551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9264c594-648a-5c79-84a0-086fe6fcdd4e'}, {'abstract': 'Plumber’s Permit Required; Questionable Co-op Voting; Resolving Disputes With a Mediator.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/realestate/new-york-real-estate-question-and-answer.html', 'snippet': 'Plumber’s Permit Required; Questionable Co-op Voting; Resolving Disputes With a Mediator.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Plumber’s Permit Required', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q & A', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Q & A', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooperatives', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Plumbing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARBITRATION, CONCILIATION AND MEDIATION', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:08:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'RealEstate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Romano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Romano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fc04fba9-6b3c-5b1b-bed7-3e603d0631ca', 'word_count': 797, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fc04fba9-6b3c-5b1b-bed7-3e603d0631ca'}, {'abstract': 'A writer’s relationship with her mother, both a drug addict and a life force, is at the heart of this memoir.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/with-or-without-you-by-domenica-ruta.html', 'snippet': 'A writer’s relationship with her mother, both a drug addict and a life force, is at the heart of this memoir.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nikki, the childhood incarnation of Domenica Ruta and the daughter of a sometimes charismatic, other times irascible Italian-American single mom, makes the following precocious observation early on in “With or Without You.” “Even nature programs revealed to us the brutal world as we recognized it: sharks leaving flesh wounds as part of the courtship ritual, procreation by gang rape and, for the finale, intrauterine cannibalism! Shakespeare couldn’t have done a finer job.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24FRAGOSO/24FRAGOSO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 689, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24FRAGOSO/24FRAGOSO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 689}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24FRAGOSO/24FRAGOSO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 892, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24FRAGOSO/24FRAGOSO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1783, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24FRAGOSO/24FRAGOSO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24FRAGOSO/24FRAGOSO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24FRAGOSO/24FRAGOSO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mommy Highest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mommy Highest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ruta, Domenica', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:10:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaux Fragoso', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaux', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fragoso', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc72f45a-8447-5168-b3f8-f38bf5057e15', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc72f45a-8447-5168-b3f8-f38bf5057e15'}, {'abstract': 'This epistolary novel about two writers is based on the correspondence of Flannery O’Connor and Robert Lowell.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/frances-and-bernard-by-carlene-bauer.html', 'snippet': 'This epistolary novel about two writers is based on the correspondence of Flannery O’Connor and Robert Lowell.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With enough distance, it’s possible to be nostalgic about pretty much anything, even the gray-flannel decade the poet Robert Lowell, who spent part of it on Thorazine, called the “tranquilized ’50s.” Ah, for the days of “duck and cover,” “The Pajama Game” and “I Like Ike.” Now Carlene Bauer, author of a precocious memoir, “Not That Kind of Girl,” about growing up evangelical in a secular age, has dug into the dawn of the “Mad Men” era for her first novel, about two young writers on the rise who meet at an artists’ colony modeled on Yaddo during the summer of 1957.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENFEY/24BENFEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 308, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENFEY/24BENFEY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 308}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENFEY/24BENFEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 526, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENFEY/24BENFEY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 614, 'width': 1195, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENFEY/24BENFEY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENFEY/24BENFEY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BENFEY/24BENFEY-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘To Talk to You in Letters’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘To Talk to You in Letters’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benfey, Christopher', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bauer, Carlene', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:11:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher Benfey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benfey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcce9779-87a1-575a-af9f-cbe5174c10f7', 'word_count': 1163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcce9779-87a1-575a-af9f-cbe5174c10f7'}, {'abstract': 'In his memoir, the chef Eddie Huang writes about his family, his love of hip-hop and his efforts to perfect red cooked skirt steak.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/fresh-off-the-boat-by-eddie-huang.html', 'snippet': 'In his memoir, the chef Eddie Huang writes about his family, his love of hip-hop and his efforts to perfect red cooked skirt steak.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hold the presses! Not all Asian-Americans are studying math and practicing the piano. Some, like the young Eddie Huang, are busy shoplifting CDs, taking Ecstasy and getting thrown in the clink. Huang’s rambunctious memoir boasts many classic adolescent moments — the employment of a SPAM launcher, for example, and the inevitable arrival of a Miss Lacey on the scene. There are tussles with his parents, tussles with his peers. There are cops and drugs. And of course, channeling his inner Holden Caulfield involves, well, language: ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24JEN/24JEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 715, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24JEN/24JEN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 715}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24JEN/24JEN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 806, 'width': 676, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24JEN/24JEN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 806, 'width': 676, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24JEN/24JEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24JEN/24JEN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24JEN/24JEN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Saucy Dishes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saucy Dishes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huang, Eddie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:11:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gish Jen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gish', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4332b5c-e8b5-596b-9546-8188f7eda5de', 'word_count': 1211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4332b5c-e8b5-596b-9546-8188f7eda5de'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel, a couple escape the devastation wrought by a tornado — for which their community destroys them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/falling-to-earth-by-kate-southwood.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel, a couple escape the devastation wrought by a tornado — for which their community destroys them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just before 1 o’clock on the afternoon of March 18, 1925, over the farmland of southeastern Missouri, a column of air, spinning like a skater with her arms folded, came down from the clouds and touched ground. To prevent public panic, the word “tornado” had been officially banned from United States weather forecasts since 1887, so the regional forecast called only for rain and shifting winds. It was possibly the greatest understatement in the history of meteorology.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BYRD/24BYRD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BYRD/24BYRD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 427}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BYRD/24BYRD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 473, 'width': 665, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BYRD/24BYRD-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 473, 'width': 665, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BYRD/24BYRD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BYRD/24BYRD-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24BYRD/24BYRD-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Everything in Its Path', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Everything in Its Path', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Southwood, Kate', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:12:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Max Byrd', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Max', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Byrd', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a30c1c97-e22e-5ac6-bde4-11ee9a689502', 'word_count': 617, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a30c1c97-e22e-5ac6-bde4-11ee9a689502'}, {'abstract': 'Tamara Shopsin — graphic designer, illustrator, short-order cook — chronicles a year of travel, work and misfortune.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/mumbai-new-york-scranton-by-tamara-shopsin.html', 'snippet': 'Tamara Shopsin — graphic designer, illustrator, short-order cook — chronicles a year of travel, work and misfortune.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve managed to eat at Kenny Shopsin’s legendary restaurant and \xadcustomer-hazing clinic only once, thanks to a regular who had passed muster and brought me along to breakfast. This was when Shopsin’s was still in Greenwich Village and felt like a crazed version of the general store it had once been; over the stack of plain pancakes that I ordered without knowing any better, I tried not to be too alarmed by the abusive banter in the kitchen between Shopsin and one of his sons — which sounded, to the uninitiated, like a job for 911. Could I call social services, I wondered, and still come back for more pancakes? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24ANASTAS/24ANASTAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 740, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24ANASTAS/24ANASTAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 740}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24ANASTAS/24ANASTAS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 831, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24ANASTAS/24ANASTAS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1661, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24ANASTAS/24ANASTAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24ANASTAS/24ANASTAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24ANASTAS/24ANASTAS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Feast for the Senses', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Feast for the Senses', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anastas, Benjamin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fulford, Jason', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shopsin, Tamara', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:13:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Anastas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anastas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/07ced92a-06b7-589d-bc3f-aefed6c305e7', 'word_count': 1184, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07ced92a-06b7-589d-bc3f-aefed6c305e7'}, {'abstract': 'The characters in Jim Gavin’s stories live in run-down Los Angeles neighborhoods and are the sons of journeymen and salesmen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/middle-men-stories-by-jim-gavin.html', 'snippet': 'The characters in Jim Gavin’s stories live in run-down Los Angeles neighborhoods and are the sons of journeymen and salesmen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A generous humanity and a fond wit animate Jim Gavin’s wonderful first collection, “Middle Men.” Gavin’s stories revolve around a familiar theme — men searching for identity and meaning — but they’re distinguished by his more specific concerns: how work shapes lives, and the differences in the way the young and the old confront the humbling problem of ambition and success. Gavin’s characters, most of whom live in down-at-the-heels neighborhoods of Los Angeles or in modest industrial towns south of the city, are sons of journeymen, low-paid assistants and traveling salesmen. These men know, or are beginning to suspect, that the mark they will make on the world is small. But “Middle Men” doesn’t wallow in pessimism; with deft portraiture, nimble prose and an intimacy with the lives of his amiable characters, Gavin shows us that the real work of a life takes place not when the sale is made but when the salesman goes back home, looks in a half-empty refrigerator and considers his options.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24SILVER/24SILVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 804, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24SILVER/24SILVER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 804}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24SILVER/24SILVER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 764, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24SILVER/24SILVER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1528, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24SILVER/24SILVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24SILVER/24SILVER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/24SILVER/24SILVER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Beautiful Losers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beautiful Losers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gavin, Jim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:13:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marisa Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/28c5fd92-febf-535c-a8e8-27fe676ce7b1', 'word_count': 1128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28c5fd92-febf-535c-a8e8-27fe676ce7b1'}, {'abstract': 'Stephan Talty makes the city of Buffalo both the locale and a major character in his first thriller, “Black Irish.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/black-irish-by-stephan-talty-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'Stephan Talty makes the city of Buffalo both the locale and a major character in his first thriller, “Black Irish.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it comes to great settings for a crime novel, there’s no place like home. For Stephan Talty, that’s Buffalo, which he has made both the locale and a major character in his first thriller, BLACK IRISH (Ballantine, $26). As viewed by Detective Absalom (Abbie) Kearney, lately returned to care for a father whose mind is going, Buffalo is a decaying place, sunk in despair, haunted by its illustrious past. “The whole city was entombed by the artifacts of its glory days,” she observes of the disintegrating waterfront, the bankrupt mills and “silent” smokestacks, the highways built for a population long gone. And nowhere is this sense of loss more keenly felt than in the section where Abbie grew up, “a patch of Ireland in the wilds of America” known as the County.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Crime/0324-Crime-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 693, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/books/review/0324-Crime/