 Taxes', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:38:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae564b71-63c9-5a2b-8f77-bba340fef6e6', 'word_count': 494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae564b71-63c9-5a2b-8f77-bba340fef6e6'}, {'abstract': 'Ireland’s first female president on sharp elbows, Margaret Thatcher and struggles at the U.N.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/magazine/mary-robinson-doesnt-need-to-be-popular.html', 'snippet': 'Ireland’s first female president on sharp elbows, Margaret Thatcher and struggles at the U.N.', 'lead_paragraph': ' In your memoir, “Everybody Matters,” you write that you were the third child amid four boys. Do you think having so many brothers affected your political path?  I do joke that this is the start of my belief in human rights and using my elbows. But more seriously, I had a stronger sense of myself as a young Irishwoman than many of my contemporaries. When I was elected president of Ireland in 1990, I was very conscious that I could use symbols in a way that would be harder, maybe, for a man. In my inauguration, I emphasized the word “love,” which is somehow easier for a woman to use.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24talk/24talk-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 386, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24talk/24talk-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1324, 'width': 498, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24talk/24talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24talk/24talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24talk/24talk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mary Robinson Doesn’t Need to Be Popular', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘If You Become Popular in That Job, You’re Probably Not Doing a Good Job’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ireland', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldman, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robinson, Mary (1944- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Everybody Matters (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:38:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Andrew', 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4557702-4f94-58e5-872b-536fe3fa262e', 'word_count': 670, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4557702-4f94-58e5-872b-536fe3fa262e'}, {'abstract': 'How Easter turned into sun, sand and beer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/magazine/who-made-spring-break.html', 'snippet': 'How Easter turned into sun, sand and beer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1958, Glendon Swarthout, an English professor at Michigan State University, overheard his students buzzing about their Easter-break trip to Fort Lauderdale. In that more-ecumenical era, students typically shuttled home to attend church services with their parents, but now word was spreading of another kind of spring break. Swarthout decided to tag along so that he could observe the rituals (pool-hopping, pith helmets, beatnik jazz) and capture the lingo (“beaucoup beers,” “schizoid,” “babyroo”). Upon his return, he dashed off a novel about coeds who cruise the beach and “play house” with boys from other colleges — a 1950s version of “Girls Gone Wild.” In a nod to the Easter season, he called his book “Unholy Spring,” but Hollywood executives persuaded him to change the title to “Where the Boys Are.” The result was a blockbuster book that was spun off into a movie — marketed with the phrase “spring vacation” — as well as a Connie Francis song.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24wmt/mag-24WMT-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 356, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24wmt/mag-24WMT-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 356}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24wmt/mag-24WMT-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 607, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24wmt/mag-24WMT-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1150, 'width': 1940, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24wmt/mag-24WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24wmt/mag-24WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24wmt/mag-24WMT-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made Spring Break?', 'kicker': 'Innovation', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That?(Spring Break)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kennedy, Pagan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MTV Networks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Easter and Holy Week', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swarthout, Glendon', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Where the Boys Are (Book)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:38:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pagan Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pagan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bce5288-5638-5eae-bd99-eb9a342ba67d', 'word_count': 535, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bce5288-5638-5eae-bd99-eb9a342ba67d'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/magazine/the-3-10-13-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE ALLERGY BUSTER', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 3.10.13 Issue', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Allergy Buster', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:38:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1d9eb56-2dcc-5587-a4d5-e2549b850c32', 'word_count': 639, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1d9eb56-2dcc-5587-a4d5-e2549b850c32'}, {'abstract': 'The British novelist hasn’t met a plot too complicated — even if it means telling a story backward or starting over in every chapter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/magazine/kate-atkinsons-groundhog-day-fiction.html', 'snippet': 'The British novelist hasn’t met a plot too complicated — even if it means telling a story backward or starting over in every chapter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine having the gift (or the curse) of continually dying and being reborn, so that you relive segments of your life again and again, differently each time, going down various paths and smoothing out rough areas until you get it right and can move on. Imagine, too, that you are not conscious that this is happening, but experience it as intermittent déjà vu, a sometimes-inchoate dread, an inexplicable compulsion at sudden moments to do one thing rather than another.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24atkinson1/mag-24Atkinson-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 739, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24atkinson1/mag-24Atkinson-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 739}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24atkinson1/mag-24Atkinson-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 832, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24atkinson1/mag-24Atkinson-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1218, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24atkinson1/mag-24Atkinson-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24atkinson1/mag-24Atkinson-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/magazine/24atkinson1/mag-24Atkinson-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Kate Atkinson’s ‘Groundhog Day’ Fiction', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Three Beginnings, Reverse Chronology and A Novel That Starts Over in Every Chapter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lyall, Sarah', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Atkinson, Kate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Life After Life (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:38:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Lyall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lyall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/34061ccb-051e-5424-ab93-e325eb346987', 'word_count': 2934, 'uri': 'nyt://article/34061ccb-051e-5424-ab93-e325eb346987'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE RIGHTEOUS MIND: Why Good People Are Divided by Politics and Religion,  by Jonathan Haidt. (Vintage, $16.) Why doesn’t the other side listen to reason? Haidt, a social psychologist, charts the “mental mechanisms that make us adept at promoting our group’s interests, in competition with other groups,” and explains why liberals and conservatives have such different intuitions about right and wrong. In Our Divided Political Heart: The Battle for the American Idea in an Age of Discontent (Bloomsbury, $18), the Washington Post columnist E. J. Dionne Jr. situates our current political dysfunction in the full sweep of American history. “Americans disagree about who we are,” he writes, “because we can’t agree about who we’ve been.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T15:39:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2f74366-82ad-5979-8b29-785ab1d56a75', 'word_count': 495, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2f74366-82ad-5979-8b29-785ab1d56a75'}, {'abstract': 'Pope Francis called for intense discourse with Islam and urged church leaders to renew dialogue with countries that do not have official ties with the Holy See.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/23/world/europe/pope-francis-urges-more-interreligious-dialogue.html', 'snippet': 'Pope Francis called for intense discourse with Islam and urged church leaders to renew dialogue with countries that do not have official ties with the Holy See.', 'lead_paragraph': 'VATICAN CITY — Pope Francis appealed for more intense dialogue with Islam on Friday, while calling on church leaders to renew diplomatic discourse with countries that do not have official ties with the Holy See, like China.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/23/world/23popes2_337/23popes2_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/23/world/23popes2_337/23popes2_337-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 410}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/23/world/23popes2_337/23popes2_337-ju