y. Everything from tea to gin-based cocktails will be featured.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Fare', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T16:29:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bao Ong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bao', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91df2258-39ec-564a-bd6f-1977bcc2f57d', 'word_count': 359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91df2258-39ec-564a-bd6f-1977bcc2f57d'}, {'abstract': 'The Bookseller magazine awarded the Diagram Prize, for the year’s oddest book title, to a work by Reginald Bakeley.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/22/years-oddest-book-title-goblinproofing-ones-chicken-coop/', 'snippet': 'The Bookseller magazine awarded the Diagram Prize, for the year’s oddest book title, to a work by Reginald Bakeley.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Imagine a book prize for which the finalists include titles like “How Tea Cosies Changed the World,” “Was Hitler Ill?” and “God’s Doodle: The Life and Times of the Penis.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Year’s Oddest Book Title: ‘Goblinproofing One’s Chicken Coop’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Contests and Prizes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bookseller, The', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-22T16:30:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b1159c4-66eb-5af2-a4a8-99e905b0e7e9', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b1159c4-66eb-5af2-a4a8-99e905b0e7e9'}, {'abstract': 'This week, a focus on reading and writing about technology, including a prompt that asks students to invent their own new technology to solve a decades-old crime.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/22/common-core-practice-drones-stolen-art-and-space-exploration/', 'snippet': 'This week, a focus on reading and writing about technology, including a prompt that asks students to invent their own new technology to solve a decades-old crime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A few weeks ago, two teachers, Sarah Gross and Jonathan Olsen, our collaborators for this series of writing prompts, showed how they integrate STEM topics — science, technology, engineering and mathematics — into their humanities classroom by exploring environmental issues. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/18/business/DRONE/DRONE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/18/business/DRONE/DRONE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/18/business/DRONE/DRONE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/18/business/DRONE/DRONE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'l