'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/70a10163-67d5-5fd5-8176-75d751d36886', 'word_count': 590, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70a10163-67d5-5fd5-8176-75d751d36886'}, {'abstract': 'When a friend or acquaintance has lost a job, the art of conversation becomes a lot trickier.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/fashion/tricky-conversations-with-unemployed-acquaintances.html', 'snippet': 'When a friend or acquaintance has lost a job, the art of conversation becomes a lot trickier.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TALKING TO UNEMPLOYED friends and colleagues requires a certain delicacy. The question “How was your day?,” when leveled with what is perceived as an undue amount of concern, can cause steam to emerge from ears. Robert Reich, the secretary of labor under President Clinton, said, “I don’t ask ‘What are you doing with yourself these days?’ or ‘How’s the job search coming?’ unless it’s a good friend. I wait for him to bring it up. Being jobless is distressing enough without people asking about it.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/fashion/0324CIRCA-NOW/0324CIRCA-NOW-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 181, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/fashion/0324CIRCA-NOW/0324CIRCA-NOW-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 181}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/fashion/0324CIRCA-NOW/0324CIRCA-NOW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 308, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/fashion/0324CIRCA-NOW/0324CIRCA-NOW-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 617, 'width': 2048, 