: 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a869457a-2191-517e-ab69-602999767131', 'word_count': 312, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a869457a-2191-517e-ab69-602999767131'}, {'abstract': 'In Gilles Bourdos’s “Renoir,” about the Impressionist painter Pierre-Auguste Renoir and his son Jean, paintings by the forger Guy Ribes play a significant role.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/movies/guy-ribess-paintings-lend-realistic-touches-to-renoir.html', 'snippet': 'In Gilles Bourdos’s “Renoir,” about the Impressionist painter Pierre-Auguste Renoir and his son Jean, paintings by the forger Guy Ribes play a significant role.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a sunny weekday morning in a cafe in Brooklyn the French director Gilles Bourdos is flipping through his laptop’s gallery of international art. There’s a charcoal study of Cézanne card players; Picasso’s inkblot Don Quixote, tilting his lance at the Spanish sun; and a blue-hued, Old World fantasy, bearing the kaleidoscopic hallmarks of Marc Chagall.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/arts/24RENOIR1_SPAN/24RENOIR1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 361, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/arts/24RENOIR1_SPAN/24RENOIR1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 361}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/arts/24RENOIR1_SPAN/24RENOIR1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 617, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'