otball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Fujita', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fujita', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c2c2f44d-a659-5659-b0b1-0852484a3572', 'word_count': 1191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c2c2f44d-a659-5659-b0b1-0852484a3572'}, {'abstract': 'What happens when a biographer learns about potentially explosive information after the book is finished?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/23/tipped-off/', 'snippet': 'What happens when a biographer learns about potentially explosive information after the book is finished?', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first time it happened was at a Fourth of July picnic in New Hampshire. I’d just turned in my book manuscript to the copy editor and was in a celebratory mood until a friend tugged at my elbow. She’d found a thick stack of letters written by my subjects, three New England sisters of the 19th century, in a trunk in her attic. Did I want to read them? ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tipped Off', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Tipped Off', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biographical Information', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reading and Writing Skills', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emerson, Ralph Waldo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thoreau, Henry David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-23T18:26:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Megan Marshall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Megan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marshall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b4cb694d-be7e-50fd-905d-1618cd84fb06', 'word_count': 1395, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b4cb694d-be7e-50fd-905d-1618cd84fb06'}, {'abstract': 'Wind, water and sun could power the United States. But will they?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/sunday-review/life-after-oil-and-gas.html', 'snippet': 'Wind, water and sun could power the United States. But will they?', 'lead_paragraph': 'WE will need fossil fuels like oil and gas for the foreseeable future. So there’s really little choice (sigh). We have to press ahead with fracking for natural gas. We must approve the Keystone XL pipeline to get Canadian oil. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/opinion/sunday/24cover/24cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 644, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/opinion/sunday/24cover/24cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 644}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/opinion/sunday/24cover/24cover-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/opinion/sunday/24cover/24cover-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/opinion/sunday/24cover/24cover-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'leg