 'headline': {'main': 'A Gamble With No Regrets, So Far', 'kicker': 'On Baseball', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stars of Cuba, Now and Then', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beane, Billy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kepner, Tyler', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oakland Athletics', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cespedes, Yoenis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-23T18:57:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c0a58d3-60d4-5477-8c89-2fe4fa8ee588', 'word_count': 1046, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c0a58d3-60d4-5477-8c89-2fe4fa8ee588'}, {'abstract': 'WIlliam Clarke Quantrill, the most feared bushwhacker of the Civil War.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/23/another-name-for-death/', 'snippet': 'WIlliam Clarke Quantrill, the most feared bushwhacker of the Civil War.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the winter of 1862-3, William Clarke Quantrill traveled from Missouri to Richmond, Va., to request a colonelcy from Confederate Secretary of War James A. Seddon. Normally, men fighting for the Confederacy wouldn’t have had to make such a trip, or had the temerity to do so. But Quantrill was no ordinary soldier. He made his request under the provisions of the Partisan Ranger Act, passed by the Confederate Congress in 1862 to organize and promote unconventional warfare along the western border. When Seddon asked Quantrill’s opinions about the war, Quantrill replied, in the florid rendering of the Confederate sympathizer John N. Edwards, one of his biographers, “I would wage such a war … as to make surrender forever impossible. I would cover the armies of the Confederacy all over with blood. … I would exterminate.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Another Name for Death’', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civilian Casualties', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Kansas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Missouri', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-23T19:00:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicole Etcheson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicole', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Etcheson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9026e4b6-8469-5bb0-9e8f-a982d3c38048', 'word_count': 1983, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9026e4b6-8469-5bb0-9e8f-a982d3c38048'}, {'abstract': 'The dawn of mainframe computers offered huge technological benefits, but also challenged notions of privacy. Now Big Data is bringing similar expectations and concerns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/technology/big-data-and-a-renewed-debate-over-privacy.html', 'snippet': 'The dawn of mainframe computers offered huge technological benefits, but also challenged notions of privacy. Now Big Data is bringing similar expectations and concerns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN the 1960s, mainframe computers posed a significant technological challenge to common notions of privacy. That’s when the federal government started putting tax returns into those giant machines, and consumer credit bureaus began building databases containing the personal financial information of millions of Americans. Many people feared that the new computerized databanks would be put in the service of an intrusive corporate or government Big Brother.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/business/24-UNBOX/24-UNBOX-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 627, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/24/business/24-UNBOX/24-UNBOX-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 627}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/business/24-UNBOX/24-UNBOX-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 980, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/business/24-UNBOX/24-UNBOX-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2032, 'width': 1943, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/business/24-UNBOX/24-UNBOX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/24/business/24-UNBOX/24-UNBOX-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/24/business/24-UNBOX/24-UNBOX-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Big Data Is Opening Doors, but Maybe Too Many', 'kicker': 'Unboxed', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Big Data Is Opening Doors, But Maybe Too Many', 'name': None, 'seo