{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alderman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3aa90d40-8b0b-5411-aaca-9aec22aa87a0', 'word_count': 1404, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3aa90d40-8b0b-5411-aaca-9aec22aa87a0'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/arts/television/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (HBO) PHIL SPECTOR (2013) A wigged-out Al Pacino reunites with the writer and director David Mamet\xa0(“Glengarry Glen Ross”) to portray the music producer and songwriter charged with the 2003 shooting death of Lana Clarkson inside his home in Alhambra, Calif. Helen Mirren, right with Mr. Pacino, plays the lawyer Linda Kenney Baden, who represented Mr. Spector alongside Bruce Cutler (Jeffrey Tambor) at his first trial, in 2007, which resulted in a hung jury. (He was convicted in 2009 and is serving 19 years to life.) The movie has already set off controversy: Mr. Spector’s wife, Rachelle, has complained about the film’s depiction of her husband as “a foul-mouthed megalomaniac.” Edward Lozzi, a former publicist for Ms. Clarkson, has taken exception to the film’s focus on the defense’s argument that she killed herself. The film is a work of fiction, Mr. Mamet said in an HBO interview, spun from his fascination with Mr. Spector’s character “and the mythological possibilities.” “The focus on the tender and respectful bond between lawyer and client looks a lot like a revisionist re-enactment and maybe even absolution,” Alessandra Stanley wrote in The New York Times. “Everyone has a right to the best available defense, but not everyone deserves to be played by the best available actor.”', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Sunday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-24T16:17:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/b43ad525-e98a-5b07-86c7-39c61b395ae7', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b43ad525-e98a-5b07-86c7-39c61b395ae7'}, {'abstract': 'Several dozen people formed a line in front of the Supreme Court over the weekend, hoping for one of the roughly 50 seats reserved for the public to witness arguments in the same-sex marriage case, which begins on Tuesday.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/24/days-early-a-line-forms-at-the-supreme-court/', 'snippet': 'Several dozen people formed a line in front of the Supreme Court over the weekend, hoping for one of the roughly 50 seats reserved for the public to witness arguments in the same-sex marriage case, which begins on Tuesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nearly 30 people formed a line in front of the Supreme Court over the weekend, hoping for one of the roughly 50 seats reserved for the public to witness arguments in the landmark same-sex marriage case that begins on Tuesday. With thousands of tourists streaming into Washington for the Easter break and the National Cherry Blossom Festival, gawkers and passers-by alike paused to ask what the line was about or to offer their opinions on the case.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Line at the Supreme Court, and Some Are Paid to Be There', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Homosexuality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-24T17:51:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/38c737f8-f978-5c0c-bac3-d00f26e7b876', 'word_count': 807, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38c737f8-f978-5c0c-bac3-d00f26e7b876'}, {'abstract': 'Democrats have enough power in Hartford to impose their legislative will, yet they and Republicans seek consensus and say they don’t want to make a mistake through haste.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/25/nyregion/connecticut-still-working-on-gun-law-frustrating-some.html', 'snippet': 'Democrats have enough power in Hartford to impose their legislative will, yet they and Republicans seek consensus and say they don’t want to make a mistake through haste.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HARTFORD — The original goal of producing legislation before the end of February has long passed. So has the next target, a bill within three months of the massacre of 20 children and 6 educators at the Sandy Hook Elementary School on Dec. 14. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/nyregion/CONNECTICUT/CONNECTICUT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/25/nyregion/CONNECTICUT/CONNECTICUT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {