line': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-24T22:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4c402b4f-9c8c-58fa-a968-e8cacae4cc66', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4c402b4f-9c8c-58fa-a968-e8cacae4cc66'}, {'abstract': 'Pink’s aerial acrobatics punctuated her hit-filled concert at Madison Square Garden.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/25/arts/music/pink-at-madison-square-garden.html', 'snippet': 'Pink’s aerial acrobatics punctuated her hit-filled concert at Madison Square Garden.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Truth About Love Tour,” which stopped at Madison Square Garden on Friday night, is built on a suspension of disbelief, but maybe not the one you’re bracing for. It isn’t about the way that Pink, the tour’s vocal dynamo and principal object in motion, defies logic and the laws of gravity during her unstoppable two-hour show. The aerial acrobatics, the taut cables and spinning cages, the whole business of soaring around an arena like a pop-punk Peter Pan: by now she has trained her audiences to expect this species of amazement in concert. It’s a condition of her current reality.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/arts/PINK-1364153945014/PINK-1364153945014-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/25/arts/PINK-1364153945014/PINK-1364153945014-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/arts/PINK-1364153945014/PINK-1364153945014-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 675, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/arts/PINK-1364153945014/PINK-1364153945014-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1350, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/arts/PINK-1364153945014/PINK-1364153945014-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/25/arts/PINK-1364153945014/PINK-1364153945014-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/arts/PINK-1364153945014/PINK-1364153945014-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Something in the Air? It Must Be the Headliner', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Something in the Air? It Must Be the Headliner', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pink (1979- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pop and Rock Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-24T22:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2277f770-715c-5a70-baff-af2750d8ae26', 'word_count': 710, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2277f770-715c-5a70-baff-af2750d8ae26'}, {'abstract': '“For Love,” at the Irish Repertory Theater, is a riotously tart and fiercely energetic dramedy about women in their mid-30s struggling in contemporary, recession-ravaged Dublin.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/25/theater/reviews/for-love-by-laoisa-sexton-at-irish-repertory-theater.html', 'snippet': '“For Love,” at the Irish Repertory Theater, is a riotously tart and fiercely energetic dramedy about women in their mid-30s struggling in contemporary, recession-ravaged Dublin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After recent excursions into Americana with the radio play of “It’s a Wonderful Life,” rural nostalgia with “Donnybrook!” and 20th-century institutional repression with “Airswimming,” the Irish Repertory Theater switches into high contemporary gear with “For Love,” a riotously tart and fiercely energetic production that opened on Tuesday. Billed as a “dark blue romantic comedy,” the play — a dramedy, really, only fleetingly romantic — plunges straight into Ireland’s fraught urban present. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/arts/FORLOVE/FORLOVE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/25/arts/FORLOVE/FORLOVE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1024}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/arts/FORLOVE/FORLOVE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 601, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit'