Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jake Appleman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jake', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Appleman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/07e2aaf7-43ad-5396-9f12-0705c7a5a597', 'word_count': 826, 'uri': 'nyt://article/07e2aaf7-43ad-5396-9f12-0705c7a5a597'}, {'abstract': 'Jay Leno is still the ratings king of late-night television, but his expected replacement, Jimmy Fallon, has Internet skills and cross-generational appeal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/25/business/media/nbcs-fallon-for-leno-tonight-shift-is-a-bet-on-future.html', 'snippet': 'Jay Leno is still the ratings king of late-night television, but his expected replacement, Jimmy Fallon, has Internet skills and cross-generational appeal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Green Bay Packers had a long, successful run with Brett Favre until they decided in 2008 that it was time to give the quarterback job to a rising talent — Aaron Rodgers — even though the future Hall of Famer was still winning games. Now NBC is following the same script in another hugely competitive arena: late-night television.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/business/NBC-dress/NBC-dress-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/25/business/NBC-dress/NBC-dress-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 649}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/business/NBC-dress/NBC-dress-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 946, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subt