the life of the first graduating class of Washington Metropolitan High School in the District of Columbia, a public school where only 7 percent of students are deemed proficient in math and 19 percent in reading. Still, its outspoken young principal, Tanishia Williams Minor, remains optimistic that her charges will succeed despite low test scores and numerous personal and academic obstacles. Among the students are Raven Coston, 17, above, who was displaced from New Orleans by Hurricane Katrina and is raising a baby and working part time; Raven Quattlebaum, 18, who grew up in foster care and spent her days robbing and assaulting people before becoming determined to go to college; Rufus McDowney, 16, who has been in and out of the juvenile justice system for three years; Tiara Parker, 18, an aspirational senior who maybe not be able to afford college; and Delaunte Bennett, 18, a sophomore who was kicked out of several schools because of fighting and now lags two years behind his peers.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Monday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-25T04:15:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/340041cc-f52c-5a74-9683-2b92653cb56b', 'word_count': 752, 'uri': 'nyt://article/340041cc-f52c-5a74-9683-2b92653cb56b'}, {'abstract': 'No. 13 La Salle squeezed by No. 12 Ole Miss in the final seconds to advance to the Round of 16, where they will face Wichita State.', 'web_url': 'https://www.ny