hel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nolan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be7bc22c-86e2-5047-84a4-fa6e306d6c31', 'word_count': 1084, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be7bc22c-86e2-5047-84a4-fa6e306d6c31'}, {'abstract': 'Until his death on Saturday, the oligarch Boris Berezovsky thought it was a brilliant idea for him to have backed the president who is ruining Russia.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/25/till-death-berezovsky-defends-backing-putin/', 'snippet': 'Until his death on Saturday, the oligarch Boris Berezovsky thought it was a brilliant idea for him to have backed the president who is ruining Russia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MOSCOW — The oligarch Boris Berezovsky, who died near London on Saturday, would have enjoyed watching Russians react to his death. He reportedly suffered from depression in the last few months of his life, and I know he was plagued by the nagging sense that he could no longer make himself relevant to Russian politics. Not only has his death dominated every media outlet and every conversation in Moscow, but President Vladimir Putin’s press secretary, Dmitri Peskov, stated that Berezovsky wrote to Putin a couple of months ago asking forgiveness and “requesting permission to return to the motherland.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Man Behind Putin', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berezovsky, Boris', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-25T09:12:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Masha Gessen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Masha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gessen', 'qualifier': No