times.com/2013/03/25/can-earth-hour-help-save-the-planet/', 'snippet': 'Even Vladimir Putin participated in Earth Hour for the first time this year, switching off the lights in the Kremlin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE HAGUE — Camera, lights off, action!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/blogs/25rdv-earth-art-paris/25rdv-earth-art-paris-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 225, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/25/blogs/25rdv-earth-art-paris/25rdv-earth-art-paris-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 225}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/blogs/25rdv-earth-art-paris/25rdv-earth-art-paris-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/blogs/25rdv-earth-art-paris/25rdv-earth-art-paris-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 767, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/blogs/25rdv-earth-art-paris/25rdv-earth-art-paris-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/25/blogs/25rdv-earth-art-paris/25rdv-earth-art-paris-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/blogs/25rdv-earth-art-paris/25rdv-earth-art-paris-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Earth Hour Help Save the Planet?', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Earth', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-25T14:18:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher F. Schuetze', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Schuetze', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7aa7b56b-3788-55c3-bb1d-eb313e9ff212', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7aa7b56b-3788-55c3-bb1d-eb313e9ff212'}, {'abstract': 'The American soccer team plays a World Cup qualifying match Tuesday in Mexico City, even as Costa Rica appeals its loss to the United States in blizzard-like conditions Friday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/26/sports/soccer/costa-rica-files-protest-wants-snowy-us-game-replayed.html', 'snippet': 'The American soccer team plays a World Cup qualifying match Tuesday in Mexico City, even as Costa Rica appeals its loss to the United States in blizzard-like conditions Friday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEXICO CITY — The Iditarod will not break out here Tuesday when the United States faces Mexico in a World Cup qualifying match. There will be a significant increase in elevation, smog and opponent’s skill level. But snowplows will not be on standby at Estadio Azteca. The only white stuff likely to fall is confetti.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Conquering Snow, U.S. Now Faces Altitude ', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Conquering Snow, U.S. Now Faces Altitude', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Cup 2014 (Soccer)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "United States Men's National Soccer Team", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Federation of Association Football (FIFA)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Costa Rica', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-25T14:23:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Soccer', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeré Longman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeré', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Longman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ac8837e8-8d2b-59de-a2d5-d8faeef36695', 'word_count': 868, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ac8837e8-8d2b-59de-a2d5-d8faeef36695'}, {'abstract': 'In Antwerp, a closer look at the intricately printed textiles from the firm Abraham, which supplied the couture houses Dior, Givenchy and Balenciaga.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/25/now-showing-the-abraham-archives/', 'snippet': 'In Antwerp, a closer look at the intricately printed textiles from the firm Abraham, which supplied the couture houses Dior, Givenchy and Balenciaga.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Though Paris was undisputedly the world capital of fashion in the immediate postwar period, one of its more important, if lesser-known, suburbs was Zurich, the home of the exclusive textile firm Abraham, which kept the couture houses of Christian Dior, Hubert de Givenchy, Cristóbal Balenciaga et al supplied with intricately printed, ultraluxurious Lyon silk. Abraham and its creative director, Gustave Zemsteg, who called on artist friends like Joan Miro and Marc Chagall to design prints, are the subjects of a new exhibition at Antwerp’s Mode Museum. Zemsteg’s most fruitful partnership was with his close friend Yves Saint Laurent, whom he met at the funeral of another client, Christian Dior. “I wouldn’t call their correspondence love letters, but they are certainly affectionate,” says the show’s curator, Karen Van Godtsenhoven. “It was a friendship as well as a business relationship.” Saint Laurent, then the newly appointed Dior heir, went on to use Abraham silk throughout his career; his last couture collection, in 2002, was the firm’s death knell. Faced with a dwindling market for its ultra-upscale fabrics, it closed its doors and donated its meticulous archive to the Swiss National Museum. “The bold flower prints, the fabrics with gold — what they did was incredibly opulent,” Van Godtsenhoven says.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Showing | The Abraham Archives', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Switzerland', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-25T14:30:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nancy MacDonell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nancy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacDonell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15b596d0-4fb2-5f42-86ad-4eff2e0258cc', 'word_count': 229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15b596d0-4fb2-5f42-86ad-4eff2e0258cc'}, {'abstract': 'An American University professor of international finance discusses how a deal to aid Cyprus will affect the global market.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/business/global/100000002136346/cyprus-losses-european-gains.html', 'snippet': 'An American University professor of internati