 light on the melt and generous with the greens.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/27/health/mushroom-melt-recipes-for-health.html', 'snippet': 'A vegetarian sandwich that is light on the melt and generous with the greens.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This vegetarian sandwich can be made in the oven, a toaster or in a panini grill. I use the toaster.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/26/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 382}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 651, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1303, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/26/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/science/27recipehealth/27recipehealth-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mushroom Melt With Parsley Pesto, Kale and Arugula', 'kicker': 'Recipes for Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mushrooms', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-25T15:26:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa50c9f6-4d64-57b7-b3e2-5fd1e63e8296', 'word_count': 367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa50c9f6-4d64-57b7-b3e2-5fd1e63e8296'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/25/the-gun-report-march-25-2013/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The new meme among gun absolutists appears to be hammers. “Why did you elect to report on guns as a choice of weapon, when according to current F.B.I. stats, bear claw hammers and baseball bats are the number 1 and number 2 choice of weapon?” wrote a pro-gun reader: “Guns are 3rd.”\xa0When I was interviewing Second Amendment absolutists for Saturday’s column, I heard the same thing. So of course, Jennifer Mascia and I looked into it.\xa0Guess what?\xa0It doesn’t appear to be true, as Slate reported in February. According to F.B.I. data, if you add up all the non-gun methods used to kill people in 2011 (fire, drowning, poison, strangling, hammers, etc.), you get 4,081 non-gun homicides. That’s fewer than half of the F.B.I.’s reported 8,583 gun homicides in 2011. Guns killed more than 17 times more people than hammers and are responsible for nearly 70 percent of total murders. Indeed, Slate reported, “only 496 people were killed by blunt objects, a category that includes not just hammers and baseball bats but crowbars, rocks, paving stones, statuettes, and electric guitars.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: March 25, 2013', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-25T15:39:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Nocera', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nocera', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9318af7b-a914-57d6-b886-d3c1d6d7f9f5', 'word_count': 1373, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9318af7b-a914-57d6-b886-d3c1d6d7f9f5'}, {'abstract': 'Micah C. Lasher, who served as the mayor’s chief lobbyist in Albany, will join the office of Eric T. Schneiderman as he gears up for re-election next year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/26/nyregion/attorney-general-picks-micah-lasher-to-be-chief-of-staff.html', 'snippet': 'Micah C. Lasher, who served as the mayor’s chief lobbyist in Albany, will join the office of Eric T. Schneiderman as he gears up for re-election next year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ALBANY — The state attorney general, Eric T. Schneiderman, has hired a former aide to Mayor Michael R. Bloomberg to serve as his chief of staff.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Attorney General Picks Ex-Bloomberg Aide to Be Chief of Staff', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lasher, Micah C', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schneiderman, Eric T', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-25T15:45:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f249ddb-2673-5c39-914f-77529d014f6b', 'word_count': 334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f249ddb-2673-5c39-914f-77529d014f6b'}, {'abstract': 'A survey found that employers are using both rewards and penalties in an effort to lower their health costs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/26/business/companies-offer-workers-more-incentives-for-health-changes.html', 'snippet': 'A survey found that employers are using both rewards and penalties in an effort to lower their health costs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Employers are increasingly trying to lower health care costs by using incentives to persuade workers to make better lifestyle choices, a new survey shows, but what remains less clear is whether a reward is better than a punishment — or whether the programs work at all. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/business/HEALTH/HEALTH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/26/business/HEALTH/HEALTH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/business/HEALTH/HEALTH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}