me': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2628c972-ef83-5c3e-8248-c227f4f252d5', 'word_count': 899, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2628c972-ef83-5c3e-8248-c227f4f252d5'}, {'abstract': 'More on the Iraq invasion’s impact on the Obama era.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/25/the-iraq-war-and-domestic-politics/', 'snippet': 'More on the Iraq invasion’s impact on the Obama era.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There was a lot of pushback, much of it from liberals, to my column yesterday arguing that the Obama era as we know it has been made possible by George W. Bush’s decision ten years ago to launch an invasion of Iraq. Some of this pushback, on Twitter and elsewhere, reflects a slight misreading of my point, so let me clarify: I was not arguing that absent the Iraq War, the Democratic Party would necessarily still be in the minority today, or that the Democratic presidential nominee would necessarily have lost the elections of 2008 and 2012. As I said at the top of the piece, it’s entirely plausible that in a world with no Iraq invasion we would be presently entering Hillary Clinton’s second term, and I agree (and have acknowledged many times before) that the current Democratic advantage is founded on deep demographic and social trends as well as the accidents of politics.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Iraq War and Domestic Politics', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Douthat, Ross G', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 3, 'major':