, 'pub_date': '2013-03-25T20:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Hessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Text', '_id': 'nyt://article/5fbe00a8-063d-5676-bfb3-bea0812af12b', 'word_count': 788, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5fbe00a8-063d-5676-bfb3-bea0812af12b'}, {'abstract': 'John W. Hessler’s book “A Renaissance Globemaker’s Toolbox” saves from obscurity Johannes Schöner, a 16th-century globe maker who rescued the nation’s 1507 “birth certificate.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/26/science/the-america-map-review-of-a-renaissance-globemakers-toolbox.html', 'snippet': 'John W. Hessler’s book “A Renaissance Globemaker’s Toolbox” saves from obscurity Johannes Schöner, a 16th-century globe maker who rescued the nation’s 1507 “birth certificate.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A DECADE AGO, the Library of Congress paid $10 million to acquire the only known original copy of a 1507 world map that has been called “the birth certificate of America.” The large map, a masterpiece of woodblock printing, has been a star attraction at the library ever since and the object of revived scholarly fascination about the earliest cartography of the New World. The research has also rescued from obscurity a little-known Renaissance man, the 16th-century globe maker Johannes Schöner, who was responsible for saving the map for posterity. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/science/26SCIB/26SCIB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/26/science/26SCIB/26SCIB-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 450}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/science/26SCIB/26SCIB-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/science/26SCIB/26SCIB-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/science/26SCIB/26SCIB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/26/science/26SCIB/26SCIB-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/science/26SCIB/26SCIB-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why America Is Called America', 'kicker': 'Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why America Is Called America', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilford, John Noble', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Maps', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Geography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hessler, John W', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vespucci, Amerigo', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waldseem ller, Martin (1470-1520)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schoner, Johannes (1477-1547)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ringmann, Mathias (1482-1511)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-25T20:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Noble Wilford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Noble', 'lastname': 'Wilford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8950fe61-722d-5e20-8f33-8247950c33eb', 'word_count': 933, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8950fe61-722d-5e20-8f33-8247950c33eb'}, {'abstract': 'Apple has acquired WiFiSlam, a start-up company that helps improve the accuracy of indoor maps and other services. Indoor maps look poised to become a new battleground between big tech companies.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/25/with-acquisition-apple-looks-indoors-for-future-of-maps/', 'snippet': 'Apple has acquired WiFiSlam, a start-up company that helps improve the accuracy of indoor maps and other services. Indoor maps look poised to become a new battleground between big tech companies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It appears Apple is thinking seriously about what the mobile maps of tomorrow will look like, not just fixing the maps service it has today.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/technology/25bits-iphone/25bits-iphone-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 412, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/25/technology/25bits-iphone/25bits-iphone-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 412}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/technology/25bits-iphone/25bits-iphone-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 703, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'le