 Though the Air', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where Bowls and Bodies Fly Though the Air', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Circuses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jaworowski, Ken', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Cirque du Soleil', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lepage, Robert', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-25T21:22:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Jaworowski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jaworowski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4655e090-ec41-56b1-808d-2687c4d28a9b', 'word_count': 328, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4655e090-ec41-56b1-808d-2687c4d28a9b'}, {'abstract': 'The crispness and acidity of good chardonnay pairs beautifully with chicken potpie and other rich, creamy foods.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/25/a-beige-wine-for-a-beige-meal/', 'snippet': 'The crispness and acidity of good chardonnay pairs beautifully with chicken potpie and other rich, creamy foods.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a meal of relentlessly beige food, may I suggest the beigest of wines? That would be chardonnay, which will go beautifully with Melissa Clark’s chicken potpie. I call it beige in the most insulting way, as chardonnay long ago became kind of a generic term for a glass of insipid white wine in any bar in the land. But of course chardonnay also makes some of the greatest, most distinctive white wines in the world. This potpie calls out for a crisp wine, with no oakiness and plenty of lively acidity and herbal-citrus zest to cut through the richness of the filling.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Beige Wine for a Beige Meal', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-25T21:23:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Asimov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Asimov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b072a5f5-7d51-565a-8d86-f6975cad3957', 'word_count': 231, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b072a5f5-7d51-565a-8d86-f6975cad3957'}, {'abstract': '“Happy Birthday,” written by Anita Loos as a vehicle for Helen Hayes, has been revived at the Beckett Theater.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/26/theater/reviews/happy-birthday-by-anita-loos-at-the-beckett-theater.html', 'snippet': '“Happy Birthday,” written by Anita Loos as a vehicle for Helen Hayes, has been revived at the Beckett Theater.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The history of romantic comedies would be considerably shorter without the presence of the shy girl. You know, the mousy one who’s overlooked until that magic moment when she. ... (Insert standard plot twist leading to capture of Prince Charming.) ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/arts/26HAPPY/26HAPPY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/26/arts/26HAPPY/26HAPPY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/arts/26