ussia and Tanzania, he meets with much goodwill – but also some concern among Africans that China may be a “new colonial power,” extracting resources and selling manufactured goods, as I reported on Sunday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/world/asia/26XiRDV/26XiRDV-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 339, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/26/world/asia/26XiRDV/26XiRDV-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 339}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/world/asia/26XiRDV/26XiRDV-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 579, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/world/asia/26XiRDV/26XiRDV-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1158, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/world/asia/26XiRDV/26XiRDV-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/26/world/asia/26XiRDV/26XiRDV-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/26/world/asia/26XiRDV/26XiRDV-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Oh Boy! So Many Questions!’ About China in Africa', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Buckley, Chris', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gettleman, Jeffrey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kikwete, Jakaya', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Xi Jinping', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "People's Bank of China", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Xinhua', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nigeria', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tanzania', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-26T04:58:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Didi Kirsten Tatlow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Didi', 'middlename': 'Kirsten', 'lastname': 'Tatlow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6455a1c-a42a-5f57-b756-382fc3ec31bc', 'word_count': 584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6455a1c-a42a-5f57-b756-382fc3ec31bc'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/26/arts/television/tv-schedule-for-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (13, 49) TAVIS SMILEY REPORTS: EDUCATION UNDER ARREST Mr. Smiley travels to Washington State, Louisiana, Missouri and California to examine the connection between the juvenile justice system and the dropout rate among American teenagers; as many as three-quarters of those incarcerated in detention centers withdraw from high school. Along the way he speaks with educational experts on the front lines of reform, including Jimmie Edwards, a juvenile court judge and the founder of the Innovative Concept Academy in St. Louis, who believes that the zero-tolerance policy applied by some schools — under which minor infractions, including disruptive behavior, foul language and truancy, can escalate into jail time — is not the best route; and the Loyola and Tulane University law students who work for Stand Up for Each Other!, an advocacy group for children who have been expelled from school. Part 2 of “180 Days: A Year Inside an American High School,” which chronicles one year in the life of the first graduating class of Washington Metropolitan High School — a public school where only 7 percent of students are deemed proficient in math, and only 19 percent in reading — follows at 9. (Mr. Smiley, above right, with Michael Triplett, principal of the Innovative Concept Academy.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Tuesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Today', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-26T05:00:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/62b58d5e-475c-5c41-b127-7abc54a1485d', 'word_count': 742, 'uri': 'nyt://article/62b58d5e-475c-5c41-b127-7abc54a1485d'}, {'abstract': 'The 21st-century dog has received an upgrade, from “pet” to “family,” and life-prolonging surgery is definitely an option.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/26/booming/old-dog-needs-6000-surgery-what-do-you-do.html', 'snippet': 'The 21st-century dog has received an upgrade, from “pet” to “family,” and life-pr