ne}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f2ba710-ff1e-5d85-b943-cdbd61371daa', 'word_count': 902, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f2ba710-ff1e-5d85-b943-cdbd61371daa'}, {'abstract': 'Tensions between China and Vietnam sharpened Tuesday after Vietnam said that a Chinese vessel fired flares and damaged a Vietnamese fishing vessel last Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/27/world/asia/china-vietnam-alleges-attack.html', 'snippet': 'Tensions between China and Vietnam sharpened Tuesday after Vietnam said that a Chinese vessel fired flares and damaged a Vietnamese fishing vessel last Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tensions between China and Vietnam sharpened Tuesday after Vietnam said that a Chinese vessel fired flares and damaged a Vietnamese fishing vessel last Wednesday in a disputed area of the South China Sea. Vietnam said it had lodged a formal complaint with China and asked for an investigation and compensation for the damaged vessel, which was fishing in a region claimed by both countries. The episode was the latest in a series of disputes between China and some of its neighbors in the South China Sea. In Beijing on Tuesday, a Chinese government spokesman did not say whether a government ship had fired flares but said that any action taken was “necessary and justified.” China said that the territory near the Paracel Islands is clearly Chinese and that the Vietnamese boat was fishing illegally. China also denied the vessel suffered damage. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: Vietnam Alleges Attack', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'China: Vietnam Alleges Attack', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South China Sea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barboza, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Vietnam', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-27T01:34:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Barboza', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barboza', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e9e1abb-bf0b-5831-bde5-ea0dcfae74bc', 'word_count': 141, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e9e1abb-bf0b-5831-bde5-ea0dcfae74bc'}, {'abstract': 'Congress may finally pass a bill allowing states to require sales taxes from online retailers. Now is the time to be fair to both Internet and bricks-and-mortar businesses.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/27/opinion/sales-tax-fairness.html', 'snippet': 'Congress may finally pass a bill allowing states to require sales taxes from online retailers. Now is the time to be fair to both Internet and bricks-and-mortar businesses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At long last, Congress may finally be ready to pass a bill allowing states to require online retailers to collect sales taxes. Under federal law, which largely predates the Internet, online retailers are required to collect sales tax only if they have a physical presence in the customer’s state. That lets many online retailers off the hook for sales-tax collection, giving them an unfair advantage over bricks-and-mortar stores and depriving states of an estimated $11 billion in annual sales-tax revenue. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sales Tax Fairness', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sales Tax Fairness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 