ecurity firm. Among the findings: a lower percentage of scheduled passengers among those interviewed carried prohibited items compared with charter passengers, and a lower percentage of scheduled passengers than charter passengers needed to be re-examined by security employees.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Screening Checkpoint Delays', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Airports: Screening Checkpoint Delays', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRPORTS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-27T15:00:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanya Mohn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mohn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5710cd49-dd82-5f29-a6c9-ede71a921108', 'word_count': 65, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5710cd49-dd82-5f29-a6c9-ede71a921108'}, {'abstract': 'The Confederacy was designed to maximize white freedom, but by 1863 its impressment policies had grown unbearable for many.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/27/impressed-in-the-south/', 'snippet': 'The Confederacy was designed to maximize white freedom, but by 1863 its impressment policies had grown unbearable for many.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In August 1862, the popular journalist, planter and politician J. F.H. Claiborne voiced a concern shared by thousands throughout the Confederacy. “We are now proving our loyalty by starvation,” he wrote to Gov. John Pettus of Mississippi. Claiborne’s plaint exposed the very real fear that spiraling inflation would combine with the military’s growing demands for food and supplies, and the central government’s ill-defined policies of how to satisfy them, in creating drastic food shortages, particularly in the South’s cities.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Impressed in the South', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Desertion (Military)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shortages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-27T15:04:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Beard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Beard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/47fe80b5-d322-5791-b774-6d35b4bbd5c3', 'word_count': 1678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47fe80b5-d322-5791-b774-6d35b4bbd5c3'}, {'abstract': 'The Paris Review has a great, long interview with Mark Leyner, the subject of a magazine profile last year.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/27/mark-leyner-on-the-art-of-being-sufficiently-carnivalesque/', 'snippet': 'The Paris Review has a great, long interview with Mark Leyner, the subject of a magazine profile last year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As part of its 60th anniversary issue, The Paris Review has a great, long interview with Mark Leyner, author of, most recently, “The Sugar-Frosted Nutsack.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/25/magazine/25-mark-leyner/25-mark-leyner-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 331, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'cr