ION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-27T17:13:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Keller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83a87c30-2645-5858-8e2e-3537354cc567', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83a87c30-2645-5858-8e2e-3537354cc567'}, {'abstract': 'In one of the stranger rhetorical runs in recent Albany memory, more than a half-dozen senators referred to kumquats in their musings about the state’s spending plan, which they approved at 4:15 a.m. Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/27/in-the-wee-hours-in-albany-the-talk-turns-to-kumquats/', 'snippet': 'In one of the stranger rhetorical runs in recent Albany memory, more than a half-dozen senators referred to kumquats in their musings about the state’s spending plan, which they approved at 4:15 a.m. Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ALBANY — The great kumquat debate began around 1 a.m. on Wednesday, when State Senator George S. Latimer, a Westchester County Democrat, likened a voluminous budget bill to a Christmas gift basket that contained some items that were desirable, and others that were not.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/27/nyregion/27cityroom-kumquat/27cityroom-kumquat-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/27/nyregion/27cityroom-kumquat/27cityroom-kumquat-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/27/nyregion/27cityroom-kumquat/27cityroom-kumq