icle', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Celia McGee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Celia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d4dc6b7b-2617-5517-9656-b94eee12c2de', 'word_count': 1235, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d4dc6b7b-2617-5517-9656-b94eee12c2de'}, {'abstract': 'The Clove Club, London’s newest restaurant in the Shoreditch neighborhood, is now serving a five-course tasting menu and small plates for sharing.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/27/table-talk-the-clove-club/', 'snippet': 'The Clove Club, London’s newest restaurant in the Shoreditch neighborhood, is now serving a five-course tasting menu and small plates for sharing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With arguably one of the most anticipated London restaurant openings this year, the Clove Club — on Old Street in the vibrant and edgy neighborhood of Shoreditch — is the creative and culinary collaboration of Daniel Willis, Johnny Smith and the chef Isaac McHale. After receiving much critical acclaim for their first restaurant, Upstairs at the Ten Bells, they felt it was time to make a bigger, bolder move. And to the historic Shoreditch Town Hall they decamped. It’s impossible not to be impressed by the restaurant’s ground-floor space, with its soaring high ceilings; original period features; bare, white walls; and dark, reclaimed wood. In the dinner-only room, McHale serves a constantly rotating five-course tasting menu; in the more casual bar area, an assortment of small, shared plates is available.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Table Talk | The Clove Club', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-27T20:39:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katie Chang', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chang', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c0efeff-2a32-539a-8df8-21a6a8304467', 'word_count': 326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c0efeff-2a32-539a-8df8-21a6a8304467'}, {'abstract': 'Google Street view provides a glimpse of the the eerily empty streets of Namie, a town deep in the evacuation zone around the crippled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/27/google-street-view-captures-ghost-towns-of-japan/', 'snippet': 'Google Street view provides a glimpse of the the eerily empty streets of Namie, a town deep in the evacuation zone around the crippled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The eerily empty streets of Namie, a town deep in the evacuation zone around the crippled Fukushima Daiichi nuclear power plant, are featured in the latest images captured by Google for its Street View mapping project.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Google Street View Captures Ghost Town in Fukushima’s Evacuation Zone', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Street View', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-27T20:40:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a8e384e-0c5c-5fe6-b120-593e8581a2c2', 'word_count': 305, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a8e384e-0c5c-5fe6-b120-593e8581a2c2'}, {'abstract': 'A range of HBO shows — including “Girls,” “Game Change” and “Real Sports” — were among the 39 recipients of the honors, which the University of Georgia awards for excellence in electronic media.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/27/lorne-michaels-and-louie-are-among-peabody-award-winners/', 'snippet': 'A range of HBO shows — including “Girls,” “Game Change” and “Real Sports” — were among the 39 recipients of the honors, which the University of Georgia awards for excellence in electronic media.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lorne Michaels, the creator of “Saturday Night Live,” as well as Scotusblog, the widely followed Supreme Court news source, and the FX series “Louie,” created by the comic Louis C.K., were among the recipients of the 2012 George Foster Peabody Awards, announced on Wednesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lorne Michaels and ‘Louie’ Are Among Peabody Award Winners', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Peabody Awards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Michaels, Lorne', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-27T20:40:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Stelter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stelter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'r