d last year and will be auctioned in June.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/28/books/william-faulkner-writings-go-to-auction.html', 'snippet': 'A cache of writings by William Faulkner, some previously unknown works, were found last year and will be auctioned in June.', 'lead_paragraph': 'William Faulkner typed the story on the back of University of Mississippi letterhead, an early exercise in fiction writing about a fur trapper’s trip to a big city. Its 13 browned pages, stashed in an old, mismarked box in a barn on the Faulkner family farm in Charlottesville, Va., were discovered just last year. Now they, along with a stack of hand-corrected manuscripts, letters, a hand-bound poetry book and Faulkner’s Nobel medal, are headed to Sotheby’s for auction in June. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28FAULKNERjp1/28FAULKNERjp1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 833, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28FAULKNERjp1/28FAULKNERjp1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 833}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28FAULKNERjp1/28FAULKNERjp1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 738, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28FAULKNERjp1/28FAULKNERjp1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1061, 'width': 764, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28FAULKNERjp1/28FAULKNERjp1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28FAULKNERjp1/28FAULKNERjp1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28FAULKNERjp1/28FAULKNERjp1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Faulkner’s Past Isn’t Dead; You Can Buy It at Auction', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Faulkner’s Past Isn’t Dead; You Can Buy It at Auction', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Faulkner, William', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Sotheby's", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Manuscripts', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-27T21:36:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/63ee12cd-e8b2-53e3-aee9-e495fd4e159f', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/63ee12cd-e8b2-53e3-aee9-e495fd4e159f'}, {'abstract': '“The Burgess Boys,” Elizabeth Strout’s follow-up to her Pulitzer-winning “Olive Kitteridge,” has three siblings, a legal case and many detours along the way.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/28/books/the-burgess-boys-a-novel-by-elizabeth-strout.html', 'snippet': '“The Burgess Boys,” Elizabeth Strout’s follow-up to her Pulitzer-winning “Olive Kitteridge,” has three siblings, a legal case and many detours along the way.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Burgess Boys” has the ring of a story about outlaws in the old West. But it’s about lawyers from small-town Maine instead. This new novel by Elizabeth Strout, the 2009 Pulitzer Prize winner for “Olive Kitteridge,” features two Burgess brothers, as well as a Burgess girl. Ms. Strout, whose first books were “Amy and Isabelle” and “Abide With Me,” writes most comfortably about women, but this book is mostly about the midlife crises of men. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28BOOK/28BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 880, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28BOOK/28BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 880}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28BOOK/28BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 699, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28BOOK/28BOOK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1397, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28BOOK/28BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28BOOK/28BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28BOOK/28BOOK-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pig’s Head Is Just One Plot Point of Many', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pig’s Head Is Just One Plot Point Of Many', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strout, Elizabeth', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-27T21:36:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Janet Maslin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Janet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Maslin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7749133-9a87-5fa7-b7f9-52841788bbff', 'word_count': 734, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7749133-9a87-5fa7-b7f9-52841788bbff'}, {'abstract': 'The country’s sudden reversal of fortunes implies that self-reliant Cyprus may need to brush up on its own relations with Turkey to make its gas wealth a reality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/28/business/global/for-cyprus-a-sudden-need-to-play-nice-with-turkey.html', 'snippet': 'The country’s sudden reversal of fortunes implies that self-reliant Cyprus may need to brush up on its own relations with Turkey to make its gas wealth a reality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRUSSELS — With its economy in tatters, Cyprus urgently needs to tap its natural gas deposits. But opening the spigot will force the easternmost member of the European Union to make the tough choices it has long been loath to confront.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Cyprus, a Sudden Need to Play Nice With Turkey', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To Cash In on Gas, Cyprus May Need to Sell to Turkey', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub