oing since resigning the New Jersey governorship in 2004, never feels as if it got to the core of the man. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28GRACE/28GRACE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28GRACE/28GRACE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 389}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28GRACE/28GRACE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 664, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28GRACE/28GRACE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 809, 'width': 1248, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28GRACE/28GRACE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28GRACE/28GRACE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/arts/28GRACE/28GRACE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In New Jersey, a Resignation, Then a Search for Redemption', 'kicker': 'Television Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In New Jersey, a Resignation, Then a Search for Redemption', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McGreevey, James E', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Fall to Grace (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-27T22:18:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/02a6d930-db2e-5949-aabb-6dac4650d291', 'word_count': 381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02a6d930-db2e-5949-aabb-6dac4650d291'}, {'abstract': 'James Gulliver Hancock set out to draw every building in New York; a new book chronicles the task so far.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/28/garden/james-gulliver-hancock-on-all-the-buildings-in-new-york-that-hes-drawn-so-far.html', 'snippet': 'James Gulliver Hancock set out to draw every building in New York; a new book chronicles the task so far.', 'lead_paragraph': 'James Gulliver Hancock is one of those people who set impossible tasks for themselves that evolve into blogs and then into books. For Mr. Hancock, a 35-year-old Australian artist and illustrator, the task has been to draw every building in New York. He started in April 2010, while living in Brooklyn. Now back home in Sydney with his wife and year-old son, he returns to the scene of his ambition from time to time and otherwise works from photographs and Google Maps. The drawings are posted on his Web site, allthebuildingsinnewyork.com, where they often inspire commissions, leading Mr. Hancock’s pen on an erratic course from Staten Island to the Bronx and from squat warehouses to soaring landmarks. Next week, Universe Publishing will release “All the Buildings in New York: That I’ve Drawn So Far” ($19.95), a sampler of the more than 500 drawings he has executed. Mr. Hancock spoke of his starry-eyed project on the phone from Sydney.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/garden/28QNA1/28QNA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 924, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/28/garden/28QNA1/28QNA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 924}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/garden/28QNA1/28QNA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 665, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/garden/28QNA1/28QNA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1330, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/garden/28QNA1/28QNA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/28/garden/28QNA1/28QNA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/garden/28QNA1/28QNA1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'New York as a Work in Progress', 'kicker': 'Currents | Q&A', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New York As a Work In Progress', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-27T22:19:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Lasky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lasky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/bfa0091c-a758-5ce3-815f-4a71122e7111', 'word_count': 714, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bfa0091c-a758-5ce3-815f-4a71122e7111'}, {'abstract': 'J. C. Penney is introducing home furnishing boutiques through partnerships with well-known designers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/28/garden/j-c-penney-prepares-to-open-home-furnishings-boutiques-in-its-stores.html', 'snippet': 'J. C. Penney is introducing home furnishing boutiques through partnerships with well-known designers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seeking the cachet of specialty stores, J. C. Penney (recently rebranded as JCP) is installing home-furnishings boutiques in almost half of its 1,100 stores. These shops will offer dedicated collections by well-known designers. “We wanted good partners who could appeal to a wide variety of people,” said Paul Rutenis, general merchandise manager for JCP Home.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/28/garden/28CURR4/28CURR4-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/28/garden/28CURR4/28CURR4-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': No