: [{'firstname': 'Nathan', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6cc377fd-0ba0-5146-a360-4703cc81a210', 'word_count': 1124, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6cc377fd-0ba0-5146-a360-4703cc81a210'}, {'abstract': 'Using only black Strathmore paper and glue, Matthew Shlian has updated the ancient art of origami.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/28/black-magic/', 'snippet': 'Using only black Strathmore paper and glue, Matthew Shlian has updated the ancient art of origami.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Matthew Shlian is an artist and paper engineer whose work combines science and the age-old Japanese practice of origami. His new series, now on sale at the online art and music concept shop the Ghostly Store, is called “Apophenia,” an interesting word that describes the tendency to see patterns in random data, to recognize order in chaos. By dissecting and then recombining different patterns, Shlian’s piece achieves this strange effect. The work is broken down into four formations — almost like freeze frames — each of which is made by hand with only black Strathmore paper and glue. From $150; theghostlystore.com. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Black Magic', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-28T13:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Heyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2a371d2-4e37-5162-bec8-44f7172e5258', 'word_count': 102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2a371d2-4e37-