ports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Basketball', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Yardley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yardley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/cec93be7-65eb-5695-9aee-5a97b6e8a33b', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cec93be7-65eb-5695-9aee-5a97b6e8a33b'}, {'abstract': 'Cases of valley fever in the American Southwest have risen sharply in the past decade for unknown reasons, federal health officials said Thursday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/29/health/cases-of-fever-caused-by-fungus-rise.html', 'snippet': 'Cases of valley fever in the American Southwest have risen sharply in the past decade for unknown reasons, federal health officials said Thursday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cases of valley fever in the American Southwest have risen sharply in the last decade for unknown reasons, federal health officials said Thursday. The fever is caused by inhaling a fungus that thrives in desert soil. The disease resembles flu or pneumonia. Though the illness is rarely fatal, more than 40 percent of known patients are hospitalized. From 1998 to 2011, cases went from 5 per 100,000 population in the region — Arizona, California, Nevada, New Mexico and Utah — to 43 per 100,000, according to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. The increase may be due to changing weather patterns or to excavation for new housing. Those who are often outside appear slightly more likely to get it, said Dr. Benjamin J. Park, the C.D.C.’s chief epidemiologist for fungal diseases, “but people who work in office buildings have gotten it, and there’s one case of someone who just passed through the Phoenix airport.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cases of Fever Caused by Fungus Rise', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Health', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cases Of Fever Caused By Fungus Rise', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coccidioidomycosis (Valley Fever)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNeil, Donald G Jr', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fungi', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Southwestern States (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Centers for Disease Control and Prevention', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T03:02:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donald G. McNeil Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donald', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'McNeil', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/16f2715d-a1e2-5f9e-b436-af128dd40dee', 'word_count': 155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16f2715d-a1e2-5f9e-b436-af128dd40dee'}, {'abstract': 'With its 10-deep rotation, Marquette smothered second-seeded Miami, whose only lead in the game came in the first three minutes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/29/sports/ncaabasketball/marquette-brushes-off-miami.html', 'snippet': 'With its 10-deep rotation, Marquette smothered second-seeded Miami, whose only lead in the game came in the first three minutes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — There is no mistaking Marquette Coach Buzz Williams’s disgust for how others have assessed his team during the N.C.A.A. tournament. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/29/sports/cityMIAMI/cityMIAMI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 906, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_