.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/29/tip-sheet-what-to-do-after-your-admission-decision-arrives/', 'snippet': 'Now that college acceptance and wait-list offers are out, a college counselor offers a list of what should — and shouldn’t — happen next.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, as the final college decisions are announced, The Choice blog is running a series of posts dedicated to the climax of the admissions season.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/17/education/choice-tipsheet-75/choice-tipsheet-75-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/17/education/choice-tipsheet-75/choice-tipsheet-75-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/17/education/choice-tipsheet-75/choice-tipsheet-75-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/17/education/choice-tipsheet-75/choice-tipsheet-75-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tip Sheet: What to Do After Your Admissions Decision Arrives', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADMISSIONS STANDARDS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Financial Aid (Education)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Assn for College Admission Counseling', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T10:05:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Chrysler Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chrysler', 'qualifier': 'Jr', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a61433f5-0360-52f7-aaaa-42c90c077f9b', 'word_count': 960, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a61433f5-0360-52f7-aaaa-42c90c077f9b'}, {'abstract': 'When I got to Aleppo, I expected to find raging battles. Instead, I saw a war progressing in slow motion.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/29/the-stalemate-in-syria/', 'snippet': 'When I got to Aleppo, I expected to find raging battles. Instead, I saw a war progressing in slow motion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ALEPPO, Syria — Syria’s largest city sits in a valley down from a hill with an old citadel. Over the centuries, the massive stone fortress has hosted Romans, Mongols and Ottomans; these days, it’s home to the soldiers of President Bashar al-Assad. The rebels occupy the eastern portion of the old city, a maze of alleyways and courtyards down below. It’s an ideal terrain for urban guerrilla: Tanks cannot enter the narrow passages, and the thick stone walls act as shields against artillery and air strikes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Stalemate in Syria', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALEPPO (SYRIA)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T10:15:31+0000', 'documen