e': 'Dating and Courtship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subways', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T12:30:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Sheeran', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sheeran', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a59eadb0-997a-51f2-b4cb-aba0fab0dbcd', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a59eadb0-997a-51f2-b4cb-aba0fab0dbcd'}, {'abstract': 'NBC has yet to learn how to handle successions gracefully, Barbara Walters will retire in 2014, and Christopher Cuomo and Kate Bolduan will host a new CNN morning show.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/29/the-breakfast-meeting-awkwardness-at-nbc-and-barbara-walters-retirement/', 'snippet': 'NBC has yet to learn how to handle successions gracefully, Barbara Walters will retire in 2014, and Christopher Cuomo and Kate Bolduan will host a new CNN morning show.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Networks never seem to absorb lessons of lineup changes gone bad, NBC most of all, Alessandra Stanley writes. Matt Lauer, the host of NBC’s “Today” show, who may be on the way out, was admirably suave onscreen despite reports that NBC may be seeking a new host. Mr. Lauer’s problems began with his succession last year, when many viewers blamed him when his co-anchor Ann Curry was clumsily cast aside. The drama is not new. \xa0More than 20 years ago, NBC replaced Jane Pauley with the younger Deborah Norville; that “Today” show shakeup became a founding fiasco of morning television, and Ms. Norville’s career never recovered.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline