or rodeos in the Texas Panhandle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Junior rodeos in the Texas Panhandle.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Cowgirl Way', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rodeos', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T14:40:30+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/28b86dc5-a174-5fa8-b883-3b34730d913d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/28b86dc5-a174-5fa8-b883-3b34730d913d'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/31/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WAVE,  by Sonali Deraniyagala. (Knopf, $24.) Deraniyagala’s unforgettable account of her struggle to carry on living after her husband, sons and parents were killed in the 2004 tsunami isn’t only as unsparing as they come, but also defiantly imbued with light.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T14:41:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ff70659-a5cc-593a-9eee-a85f321d2b06', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ff70659-a5cc-593a-9eee-a85f321d2b06'}, {'abstract': 'Weight loss; the Abbie Hoffman rule.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/31/magazine/its-a-natural-gas-gas-gas.html', 'snippet': 'Weight loss; the Abbie Hoffman rule.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m a biologist with a federal agency. My family owns land in northeast Pennsylvania. A portion is owned outright by my mother, who has entered into a contract with a natural-gas company to allow drilling on the property. Royalties from the gas extraction allow her to live comfortably in retirement. My mother made the arrangements for the gas drilling without my support. I’m not in favor of increasing fossil-fuel dependence. In light of the anticipated royalties, my mother was advised by her lawyer to establish a corporate partnership with my brother and me, for liability protection. This would mean a portion of the royalties will be mine. If I intend to work on any future climate-change projects professionally, should I decline the partnership with my mother? If I do not support natural-gas drilling, is that another reason to decline the partnership? And is this decision different from eventually inheriting money related to drilling? RICHARD, WASHINGTON', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 467, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 934, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s a Natural Gas, Gas, Gas', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It’s a Natural Gas, Gas, Gas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klosterman, Chuck', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Royalties', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drilling and Boring', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T14:41:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff270cd9-e62a-5af7-bfd1-b78d52888911', 'word_count': 825, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff270cd9-e62a-5af7-bfd1-b78d52888911'}, {'abstract': 'Sure, I want my kid to learn how to share. I also want her to learn how to thrive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/31/magazine/a-modest-proposal-for-more-back-stabbing-in-preschool.html', 'snippet': 'Sure, I want my kid to learn how to share. I also want her to learn how to thrive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Late last week, I was driving my daughter to her play-based, shoe-optional, sugar-free preschool — a magical Arcadia where an actual chicken is free to roam and grow fat off Pirate’s Booty, and where the major areas of academic focus revolve around turn-taking, problem-solving and the life story of Rosa Parks — when I experienced a moment of self-doubt so paralyzing I almost had to pull over. The radio in my car was tuned to an NPR show, on which callers were debating the decision by the C.E.O. of Yahoo, Marissa Mayer, to ban employees from working from home. I’d been thinking about Mayer since early that morning, having fallen down an Internet rabbit hole that plunged me deep into her art collection, her exclusive wardrobe and her estimated $300 million net worth. Specifically, I was thinking about the rather highhanded, Marie Antoinette-ish way in which she dismissed the need for extended maternity leave, as if it hadn’t occurred to her that building an en suite nursery for her newborn next to her office basically elided the need for it, since the baby could remain within a few feet of her all day long.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31riff/31riff-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 108, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31riff/31riff-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 108}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31riff/31riff-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 184, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31riff/31riff-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 216, 'width': 1205, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31riff/31riff-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31riff/31riff-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31riff/31riff-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Modest Proposal for More Back-Stabbing in Preschool', 'kicker': 'Riff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘What If the Kid Got It in Her Head That It Was a Good Idea to Go Into Public Service?’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chocano, Carina', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mayer, Marissa', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T14:41:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carina Chocano', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chocano', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/342b30f7-d449-515e-9e45-4dd583e9c5a7', 'word_count': 1894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/342b30f7-d449-515e-9e45-4dd583e9c5a7'}, {'abstract': 'I thought I’d left the habit behind — until I ran into my ex-husband.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/31/magazine/a-brief-resurrection.html', 'snippet': 'I thought I’d left the habit behind — until I ran into my ex-husband.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was sitting next to my husband in our neighborhood pub on a spring afternoon, munching on fish and chips. I reached for my beer and looked out the window, and there he was: a 30-something fellow in a scruffy beard. My ex-husband.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31lives/31lives-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 448, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31lives/31lives-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 448, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31lives/mag-31Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31lives/mag-31Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31lives/mag-31Lives-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Brief Resurrection', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Brief Resurrection', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Mary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Easter and Holy Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuns', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Teresa (Mother)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T14:41:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8fe820a2-d48e-58f0-81ed-6b9ccf4a7f38', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8fe820a2-d48e-58f0-81ed-6b9ccf4a7f38'}, {'abstract': 'The producer talks about keeping “The Next Day” under wraps and doing drugs with John Lennon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/31/magazine/tony-visconti-will-lie-for-david-bowie.html', 'snippet': 'The producer talks about keeping “The Next Day” under wraps and doing drugs with John Lennon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You produced David Bowie’s new album, “The Next Day,” which was recorded in total secrecy. How did you keep it under wraps?  It wasn’t difficult. Most of us had been with him for decades, myself the longest, 45 years. Maybe one or two people said, “Is David Bowie making a new album?” and then I’d have to lie.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31talk/31talk-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31talk/31talk-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31talk/31talk-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31talk/31talk-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 398, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31talk/31talk-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1256, 'width': 488, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Tony Visconti Will Lie for David Bowie', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "‘Rock 'n' Roll Is Like Dixieland Jazz Now'", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goldman, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bowie, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Visconti, Tony (1944- )', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T14:41:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Andrew', 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9279c3d-2e22-590f-b7aa-a8d480ee926a', 'word_count': 748, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9279c3d-2e22-590f-b7aa-a8d480ee926a'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/31/magazine/the-3-17-13-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IS THIS WHERE THE THIRD INTIFADA WILL START?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 3.17.13 Issue', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 3.17.13 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T14:41:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/df926b90-ae67-5ffa-ad93-cc0dd6b7ccc7', 'word_count': 772, 'uri': 'nyt://article/df926b90-ae67-5ffa-ad93-cc0dd6b7ccc7'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/31/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OUR LADY OF ALICE BHATTI,  by Mohammed Hanif. (Vintage, $15.) Alice Bhatti, a Karachi nurse with mysterious healing powers, is at the center of Hanif’s blistering comedy, which delivers a broadside on the socially sanctioned butchery of women and girls in Pakistan. Working miracles at Sacred Heart Hospital for All Ailments, a cesspit of incompetence, Alice is beloved, but not even her supernatural skill set can stem the tide of dead women.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T14:41:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea9fe94d-79c8-5b37-86e7-fb5d368b6dce', 'word_count': 489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea9fe94d-79c8-5b37-86e7-fb5d368b6dce'}, {'abstract': 'Karen O and her bandmates have never seemed comfortable as rock stars. Which has only made them that much more compelling.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/31/magazine/get-yer-yeah-yeah-yeahs-out.html', 'snippet': 'Karen O and her bandmates have never seemed comfortable as rock stars. Which has only made them that much more compelling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The fall of 2000 felt like end times for rock ’n’ roll in New York City. On the radio it was all boy bands and Britney Spears, and there hadn’t been a vital rock scene since punk in the 1970s. But on a Sunday night at a tiny club in Lower Manhattan, something was happening. There were four acts on the bill at the Mercury Lounge; the Yeah Yeah Yeahs were the openers, and it was still light out when they took the stage. The band was elemental: guitar and drums, no bass. But the singer, who called herself Karen O, was otherworldly. She was dressed in a kind of “Clockwork Orange” burlesque, with cutout hearts as pasties. Before the show, she doused herself in olive oil. Onstage, she danced around like a lunatic, manically grinning and flinging droplets of oil from her hair into the crowd. The guitar player, Nick Zinner, had seen this side of her. But the drummer, Brian Chase, had practiced with the band only once, and this was their first gig. “The only thing to do,” Chase remembers thinking, “is just be like, ‘Whoa.’ ”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31kareno4/31kareno4-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 666, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31kareno4/31kareno4-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 666}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31kareno4/31kareno4-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 923, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31kareno4/31kareno4-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1157, 'width': 1042, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31kareno4/31kareno4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31kareno4/31kareno4-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31kareno4/31kareno4-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Get Yer Yeah Yeah Yeahs Out', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Audacity of Awkwardness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodman, Lizzy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karen O', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Yeah Yeah Yeahs (Music Group)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T14:41:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lizzy Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lizzy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15add35b-d52c-5434-9a4e-4ab272333a81', 'word_count': 2891, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15add35b-d52c-5434-9a4e-4ab272333a81'}, {'abstract': 'Designing a hot rod for the sky.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/31/magazine/who-made-that-learjet.html', 'snippet': 'Designing a hot rod for the sky.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the early 1960s, William Lear invited potential buyers to tour the jet that he designed as a hot rod for the sky. The plane was sleek and narrow, with a cabin ceiling so low that you had to take care not to bang your head. When one man complained that he couldn’t stand up inside this so-called luxury plane, Lear groused, “You’re absolutely right — and you can’t stand up in your [expletive] Rolls-Royce, either.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31WMT-span/31WMT-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 271, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/03/31/magazine/31WMT-span/31WMT-span-articleLa