': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gosling, Ryan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-29T16:47:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af80c606-e1aa-56f0-8066-9d97c5c67778', 'word_count': 48, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af80c606-e1aa-56f0-8066-9d97c5c67778'}, {'abstract': 'Harlan Coben, whose thriller “Six Years” is at No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, has read every book by a fellow native of Newark: Philip Roth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/04/07/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Harlan Coben, whose thriller “Six Years” is at No. 1 on the hardcover fiction list, has read every book by a fellow native of Newark: Philip Roth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERSEY BOYS: In all the recent hoopla over Philip Roth’s 80th birthday, nobody much remarked on the existence of another follicle-challenged Jewish novelist born in Newark, 29 years after Roth. But Harlan Coben — whose latest thriller, “Six Years,” goes straight to No. 1 in its first week on the hardcover fiction list — was in a forgiving mood when I reached him on the phone recently. “I’ve read every book Philip Roth has ever written,” he said. “He’s my favorite writer. In fact, when I was growing up in Livingston, N.J., a woman who lived down the street from us was rumored to have been the girl from ‘Goodbye, Columbus.’ I think my mother was the one who told us that. It seemed very exciting and scandalous.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 