 might hold for a generation raised with smartphones and tablets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I recently watched my sister perform an act of magic.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/01/technology/01bits-disrupt/01bits-disrupt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 284, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/04/01/technology/01bits-disrupt/01bits-disrupt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 284}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/01/technology/01bits-disrupt/01bits-disrupt-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 485, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/01/technology/01bits-disrupt/01bits-disrupt-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 971, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/01/technology/01bits-disrupt/01bits-disrupt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2013/04/01/technology/01bits-disrupt/01bits-disrupt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/04/01/technology/01bits-disrupt/01bits-disrupt-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Child, the Tablet and the Developing Mind', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Child, the Tablet And the Developing Mind', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPad', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tablet Computers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-31T15:11:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99491ad0-6575-57d6-bff9-e401542c4a91', 'word_count': 808, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99491ad0-6575-57d6-bff9-e401542c4a91'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2013/03/31/arts/television/whats-on-sunday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '12:30 P.M. (Cartoon Network) CLOUDY WITH A CHANCE OF MEATBALLS (2009) Bill Hader, Anna Faris and Andy Samberg, among others, lend their voices to this animated comedy about an eccentric inventor, above, who creates a machine that can transform water into food. But the machine malfunctions and is accidentally launched into the atmosphere, where it begins to affect the weather, turning rain into cheeseburgers and a tornado into a swirling mass of spaghetti and meatballs. “Parents will enjoy tracking the endless stream of movie and television sendups,” Daniel M. Gold wrote in The New York Times, adding, “If the filmmakers opt to make only light statements about junk food, obesity and solid waste, they at least leave the audience sated on a single serving of inspired lunacy.”', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': "What's on Sunday", 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "What's On Sunday", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-31T16:16:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/f65ccb6d-3f2a-5c52-a1ae-34bd392a6133', 'word_count': 717, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f65ccb6d-3f2a-5c52-a1ae-34bd392a6133'}, {'abstract': 'The box-office performance validated an unusual decision by Paramount Pictures to delay the film’s release so that it could rework parts of the plot.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/31/g-i-joe-retaliation-has-a-strong-start/', 'snippet': 'The box-office performance validated an unusual decision by Paramount Pictures to delay the film’s release so that it could rework parts of the plot.', 'lead_paragraph': '“G.I. Joe: Retaliation” was a burly No. 1 at North American theaters over the Easter weekend, validating an unusual decision by Paramount Pictures to delay its release so that it could rework parts of the plot. “Retaliation,” which cost at least $130 million to make, took in about $41.2 million over the weekend, for a total since opening on Thursday of $51.7 million. Overseas, the movie — originally planned for release last summer — generated an additional $80.3 million in ticket sales.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘G.I. Joe: Retaliation’ Has a Strong Start', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘G.I. Joe: Retaliation’ Has a Strong Start', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hollywood.com', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-31T16:27:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_n