er', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Scrub Away Your Worries', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Anxiety and Stress', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Orderliness and Clutter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2013-03-31T22:31:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/305d3220-4e60-578a-af30-03dd5b207feb', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/305d3220-4e60-578a-af30-03dd5b207feb'}, {'abstract': 'Deficits are a monopartisan issue.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/03/31/more-stockman/', 'snippet': 'Deficits are a monopartisan issue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Not much more, I promise. As Mark Thoma points out, the verdict among everyone who knows anything is that Stockman’s piece, mysteriously given star treatment, was pathetic and embarrassing. It’s full of big numbers that are scary because they’re big numbers — we’ve run a current account deficit of $8 trillion. So? We have a $16 trillion a year economy; America’s net international investment position is a debt of about 30 percent of GDP, which isn’t that big; our balance of investment income is still positive. But it’s EIGHT TRILLION DOLLARS!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Stockman', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2013-03-31T22:41:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7948a5